T}
T{
\fBdirections\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
\fB[client,\: upstream]\fR
T}	T{
Which sides are scanned: what the client sends,\: what comes back
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:window\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB262144\fR
T}	T{
The buffer one direction scans in,\: and the bound on the overlap carried between windows. The overlap is what lets a match straddling two reads still be found;\: a pattern wider than a quarter of the window cannot be matched reliably across reads
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:bytes\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB33554432\fR
T}	T{
Stop scanning a direction after this many bytes;\: the connection carries on unscanned. \fB0\fR scans everything and warns,\: because a long transfer then costs arbitrarily much
T}
.TE
.PP
Nothing is held back waiting for a verdict: the bytes scanned are the bytes forwarded, because a stream cannot be paused without the peer noticing. What a match decides is whether the connection continues. QUIC flows on the same listener are not scanned and validation says so: they are encrypted, and a rule over ciphertext matches nothing.
.PP
Counters: \fByara_matches\fR, \fByara_scanned\fR; \fBxproxy_yara_matches_total\fR and \fBxproxy_yara_bytes_total\fR. A match is a \fByara_match\fR security event with the rules, their tags and the offset.
.SS "server.listeners[].udp (kind: udp)"
.PP
A \fBkind: udp\fR listener is a generic datagram relay: the symmetric primitive to \fBkind: tcp\fR for services this proxy has no parser for. Nothing in the payload is read. What it provides is an endpoint pool with a balancer and health checks in front of a UDP service, a set of bounds, and the telemetry the rest of the proxy has.
.PP
\fBThere is no connection, so there is a session table.\fR The first datagram from a client address picks an endpoint through the pool\(aqs balancer and opens a connected socket towards it; every later datagram from that address takes the same path, and what the endpoint answers goes back to that address. A session ends when it has been idle for \fBidle_timeout\fR, when it reaches one of its bounds, or at shutdown. The socket towards the endpoint is \fIconnected\fR, so the kernel drops anything arriving from another address: an answer forged by a third party never reaches the client.
.PP
\fBA datagram cannot be refused.\fR There is no reply that means "no", and an error sent to a source address that did not really send anything is itself an attack on whoever owns that address. So everything this relay will not forward is \fBdropped\fR, counted in \fBudp_dropped\fR and written to the security log as \fBudp_denied\fR with the reason in \fBdetail\fR \[u2014] which means bans apply to it. That is the whole difference in feel from \fBkind: tcp\fR, where a refusal is a closed connection the client sees.
.PP
\fBA source address is whatever the sender wrote\fR, which is why this listener needs an admission policy more than a stream one does:
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBallow_clients\fR, or \fBrate_limit\fR, or both. Validation \fBwarns\fR when a listener on a non\-loopback address has neither, because an open datagram relay is somebody else\(aqs amplifier: it answers a victim with traffic the victim never asked for, at whatever gain the service behind it provides.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBmax_sessions_per_ip\fR as well as \fBmax_sessions\fR. Without the per source bound, a few forged datagrams a second fill the table and the service stops for every client that is real.
.RE
.PP
.PP
\fBNo accept socket at all.\fR This is the one kind that binds only a datagram socket, so nothing holds the matching TCP port and a client that connects to it is refused by the kernel rather than left hanging. Everything that applies to accepted connections therefore does not apply here \[u2014] the shared connection limiter, \fBproxy_protocol\fR (which has no datagram form and is refused at load), a \fBtls\fR section (there is no handshake on a datagram to secure) \[u2014] and the bounds below are the whole of the admission policy. Any change to such a listener restarts the daemon rather than being applied in place, as it does for every listener that owns a UDP socket.
.PP
.TS
