.SS "server.listeners[].forward (kind: forward)"
.PP
A \fBkind: forward\fR listener is an explicit proxy that clients configure in their browser or \fBHTTPS_PROXY\fR. \fBCONNECT host:port\fR opens a tunnel (TLS stays end to end between the client and the destination; nothing is inspected) and absolute \fBhttp://\fR request lines are relayed with hop\-by\-hop headers removed and \fBVia: 1.1 xproxy\fR added. Requests that are neither (an ordinary origin\-form request, an \fBhttps://\fR URI) get 400. Every destination passes the policy below before a connection is made: the port must be listed, the name is resolved, the resolved addresses must not be private unless \fBallow_private\fR is set, \fBdeny\fR wins, and a non\-empty \fBallow\fR must match. The address that passed the check is the one dialled, so a name cannot rebind between check and connect. Refusals answer 403, are logged as security events (\fBforward_port\fR, \fBforward_private\fR, \fBforward_deny\fR, \fBforward_not_allowed\fR, \fBforward_resolve\fR) and count towards the \fBforward_denied\fR ban reason. A forward listener may terminate TLS from the client (\fBtls\fR), and with TLS may list \fBh2\fR so clients tunnel \fBCONNECT\fR over an HTTP/2 stream (the stream carries the tunnel, one per request, ending when the destination closes or the client resets); it takes no \fBtcp\fR, \fBredirect_to_https\fR, \fBh3\fR or \fBh2c\fR. Bans, the connection limits and the header timeouts apply as on every listener.
.PP
.TS
