.TE
.PP
Both need \fBHTTP/2 or HTTP/3\fR, because an extended CONNECT carries a \fB:protocol\fR pseudo\-header that HTTP/1.1 has no way to express: the listener needs \fBtls\fR with \fBh2\fR in its \fBprotocols\fR. Datagrams travel as capsules (RFC 9297) rather than HTTP datagrams \[u2014] the fallback RFC 9298 requires, reliable and ordered, which for a proxy applying a policy to each datagram is a feature: an unreliable path would make a refused datagram indistinguishable from a lost one.
.PP
Every extended CONNECT is answered here, not only the two protocols implemented: an unimplemented one gets 501 rather than falling through to the ordinary \fBCONNECT\fR path, where a request carrying a path and no authority would otherwise be treated as a TCP tunnel to whatever its \fB:authority\fR said.
.PP
\fBCONNECT\-IP needs a tunnel device\fR, and the proxy does not create one. A userspace process cannot put an arbitrary IP packet on the wire; a raw socket would need \fBCAP_NET_RAW\fR, would not receive the replies a session needs, and would let a bug here forge any packet on the network. A \fBtun\fR interface is the honest mechanism: the operator creates, addresses, routes and firewalls it (\fBip tuntap add mode tun xproxy0\fR, and the rest), and the proxy only opens it. The network policy of a VPN belongs to the host\(aqs configuration, not to this file. Where no device is available the request is refused with 501 and a reason in the error log rather than failing obscurely.
.PP
Packets are checked before forwarding: the source must be inside \fBip_assign\fR and the destination inside \fBip_routes\fR, or the packet is dropped and counted. That is the anti\-spoofing rule, and it is why both lists are required \[u2014] a client that has not been told a source address and a destination range has no business sending anything.
.SS "SOCKS5 on a forward listener"
.PP
\fBsocks5: true\fR accepts SOCKS5 on the same port as the HTTP proxy. The two cannot be confused: a SOCKS greeting starts with the version byte \fB0x05\fR and an HTTP request starts with a method, so the first byte decides and nothing is configured twice.
.PP
Everything the HTTP side applies applies here: the port list, \fBallow\fR, \fBdeny\fR, \fBallow_private\fR, the checked address being the one dialled, the tunnel bound, the idle timeout, the ban list and the counters. \fBauth\fR is enforced with RFC 1929 username/password against the same users file, sharing the same credential cache and the same bounded hashing \[u2014] so a listener with \fBauth\fR refuses a SOCKS client that offers only "no authentication" (reply \fB0xFF\fR), and one without \fBauth\fR is an open proxy for both protocols, which validation says out loud.
.PP
A policy refusal is answered with the closest SOCKS reply code rather than a blanket failure: \fB0x02\fR (connection not allowed) for a port, name, address or private\-range refusal, \fB0x04\fR (host unreachable) for a name that does not resolve. SOCKS4 is refused \[u2014] it has no authentication and no names \[u2014] and \fBBIND\fR is not implemented, because it asks the proxy to open a listening socket on a client\(aqs say\-so.
.PP
\fBWhy bother.\fR SOCKS5 is what everything that is not a browser speaks: \fBssh \-o ProxyCommand\fR, \fBgit\fR, \fBcurl \-\-socks5\-hostname\fR, database clients, package managers. Without it that traffic goes around the proxy; with it, it is under the same destination policy, the same logs and the same bans.
.PP
\fBUDP associations\fR (\fBsocks_udp: true\fR) are how DNS and QUIC travel through a SOCKS proxy. Each association binds its own socket, is fixed to the client address that opened it (the first datagram sets it), only relays answers from destinations that client has actually sent to, and dies with its TCP control connection \[u2014] which is what RFC 1928 requires and what keeps the socket from becoming an open reflector. Datagram headers are parsed with the same care as the handshake: fragments are dropped rather than reassembled, and the peer table is bounded. \fBforward_udp_associations\fR, \fBforward_udp_open\fR and \fBforward_udp_dropped\fR count the association and everything refused.
.PP
Each request writes one \fBforward\fR line to the access log with the client address, user, method, destination, status, bytes and duration. SOCKS connections write the same line with \fBprotocol: socks5\fR. Counters: \fBforward_requests\fR, \fBforward_tunnels\fR, \fBforward_tunnels_open\fR, \fBforward_denied\fR, \fBforward_auth_failed\fR, \fBforward_rejected\fR, \fBforward_errors\fR, \fBforward_bytes_in\fR, \fBforward_bytes_out\fR, \fBforward_socks\fR, \fBforward_udp_associations\fR, \fBforward_udp_open\fR, \fBforward_udp_dropped\fR, \fBforward_intercepted\fR, \fBforward_intercept_refused\fR, \fBforward_intercept_passed\fR, \fBforward_intercept_bytes\fR; \fBxproxy_forward_*\fR metrics. The policy and the users file reload; the address and TLS settings need a restart like every listener.
.SS "server.listeners[].dns (kind: dns)"
.PP
A \fBkind: dns\fR listener is a forwarding DNS proxy on the listener address over UDP and TCP. Queries are answered from a bounded cache when they can be, refused or blocked by policy, and otherwise forwarded to the upstream resolvers with a fresh transaction id on a fresh socket (random source port) per query; the answer must echo the id and the question. A truncated UDP answer is retried over TCP to the upstream, and an answer larger than the client\(aqs UDP size (512 bytes or its EDNS advertisement, honoured up to 1232 bytes per RFC 9715 so a spoofed source cannot draw a large reply) is truncated so the client retries over TCP. Only one question per query and the QUERY opcode are handled (FORMERR and NOTIMP otherwise); responses arriving as queries and packets from banned clients are dropped. A dns listener takes \fBaddress\fR, \fBdns\fR and optionally \fBtls\fR; bans and the global connection limits apply to TCP clients as on every listener. The policy, upstreams and cache bounds reload (the cache is kept); the address needs a restart.
.PP
With \fBtls\fR (certificates only, no ACME) the listener is encrypted: no plain UDP is bound, the TCP port serves DNS over TLS (RFC 7858, ALPN \fBdot\fR or none) and DNS over HTTPS (RFC 8484, ALPN \fBh2\fR or \fBhttp/1.1\fR) at \fBdoh_path\fR, chosen per connection by the negotiated protocol. The same policy, cache and counters serve both; \fBqueries_dot\fR and \fBqueries_doh\fR count them, and \fBxproxyctl tls\fR shows the certificate. Run a plain listener on 53 and an encrypted one on 853 (and 443 when browsers should use it) side by side.
.PP
.TS
