\fBallow_\:names\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
\fB[]\fR
T}	T{
Names allowed to point into a denied range,\: in the forms \fBblock\fR takes
T}
T{
\fBaction\fR
T}	T{
\fBnxdomain\fR,\: \fBrefuse\fR,\: \fBservfail\fR,\: \fBstrip\fR
T}	T{
\fBnxdomain\fR
T}	T{
What a denied answer becomes
T}
.TE
.PP
A section that denies nothing \[u2014] \fBdeny_private: false\fR with an empty \fBdeny\fR \[u2014] is refused at load. It reads like rebinding protection and is not one, and the operator who wrote it meant to get something for it.
.PP
\fBdeny_private\fR stands for \fB0.0.0.0/8\fR, \fB10.0.0.0/8\fR, \fB100.64.0.0/10\fR, \fB127.0.0.0/8\fR, \fB169.254.0.0/16\fR, \fB172.16.0.0/12\fR, \fB192.0.0.0/24\fR, \fB192.0.2.0/24\fR, \fB192.168.0.0/16\fR, \fB198.18.0.0/15\fR, \fB198.51.100.0/24\fR, \fB203.0.113.0/24\fR, \fB224.0.0.0/4\fR, \fB240.0.0.0/4\fR, \fB255.255.255.255/32\fR, \fB::/128\fR, \fB::1/128\fR, \fB::ffff:0:0/96\fR, \fB100::/64\fR, \fB2001:db8::/32\fR, \fBfc00::/7\fR, \fBfe80::/10\fR and \fBff00::/8\fR.
.PP
The IPv4\-mapped range earns its place: an AAAA record may hold \fB::ffff:127.0.0.1\fR, which is not inside \fB::1/128\fR and which every socket API connects to \fB127.0.0.1\fR anyway. Screening unmaps an address before testing it and keeps the range denied as well, so the address is caught whichever way it was written. 6to4 (\fB2002::/16\fR) and Teredo (\fB2001::/32\fR) embed an IPv4 address the same way and are deliberately not in the list: reaching the embedded address takes a relay most networks do not have, and denying them by default would refuse names that resolve legitimately. Add the two prefixes to \fBdeny\fR on a network that does carry them.
.PP
Both the name asked for and the owner name of the record are matched against \fBallow_names\fR, so a public name that is a CNAME into an internal zone is covered by exempting either end.
.PP
\fBaction\fR is about what the client is left with:
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBnxdomain\fR (the default) and \fBrefuse\fR say the name has no answer here. Nothing is cached, so the policy is re\-applied to the next query rather than frozen into the cache.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBservfail\fR says the resolver would not stand behind the answer, which is what a resolver says about an answer it rejected.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBstrip\fR removes the denied records and keeps the rest, for the name that legitimately has a public address as well as an internal one: a split\-horizon zone seen from the wrong side, an appliance that publishes its management address beside its service address. What is left may be an answer with no addresses in it, which is a NODATA and the correct thing to say \[u2014] the name exists and has nothing this client may be told. The stripped answer is what the cache keeps, so the removed address does not come back on the next query. Records whose set lost a member have their RRSIGs removed with them: a signature over a set one record short does not verify, and a client that checked it would call the answer bogus rather than short.
.RE
.PP
.PP
Only the answer section is screened. Glue in the additional section is a resolver\(aqs business \[u2014] a stub client connects to what it was answered, not to what the delegation mentioned \[u2014] and a delegation whose name servers sit on private addresses is ordinary in a split network, so screening glue would refuse names that work. Names this listener answers itself (\fBrecords\fR, \fBdiscovery\fR) and sinkhole answers are not screened either: they are this proxy\(aqs own answers, and \fBsinkhole_ipv4: 0.0.0.0\fR is deliberately inside a denied range.
.PP
A denied answer raises the \fBdns_answer_denied\fR security event with the address that tripped it, counts \fBxproxy_dns_answer_denied_total\fR, and is available as a ban reason. \fBstrip\fR counts \fBxproxy_dns_answer_stripped_total\fR and is not a refusal: the client got an answer.
.PP
The screen also runs on the way out of the cache, not only on the way in, because a reload can deny a range that an entry already in the cache points into. Such an entry is dropped rather than served.
.SS "server.listeners[].dns.cookies"
.PP
A UDP datagram proves nothing about where it came from, and everything unpleasant about an open resolver follows from that: an answer sent to an address that did not ask, a small question drawing a large reply for somebody else\(aqs link, a cache poisoned by a race the attacker enters with no packets of their own to lose, and a security event recorded against an address chosen by whoever sent the packet.
.PP
A DNS cookie (RFC 7873, with RFC 9018\(aqs server cookie layout) fixes the one thing underneath all of them: it makes the client prove it can \fIreceive\fR what it asked for. The client sends eight bytes of its own; the server returns them with a keyed hash over the client\(aqs address and those bytes, and expects that back next time. Nothing about it is secret and nothing about it is authentication \[u2014] an on\-path attacker sees the cookie \[u2014] but an off\-path one cannot produce it for an address it does not hold, which is exactly the attacker every item above depends on.
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBrespond\fR (the default) answers a client that sent a cookie with one, and treats a cookie this listener issued as proof of the address. It never refuses a query for the want of one, so a client that has never heard of cookies is unaffected.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBrequire\fR additionally refuses a UDP query that carries no valid cookie: BADCOOKIE (rcode 23) with a fresh cookie, so a cookie\-aware client retries once and succeeds, and nothing is looked up for the first attempt \[u2014] which is the whole saving. A client that sends no COOKIE option at all gets REFUSED, because there is nothing to echo and no retry to invite. \fBThat breaks every client that does not implement cookies, which is most stub resolvers\fR; validation warns about it on a listener with no \fBallow_clients\fR. It belongs on a listener whose clients are known.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBoff\fR ignores cookies entirely, which is also how to avoid the small cost below.
.RE
.PP
.PP
A stream transport is exempt from \fBrequire\fR: the peer completed a handshake to get here, which is what a cookie exists to establish (RFC 7873 section 5.2.3). A cookie is still echoed over TCP, DoT, DoH and DoQ, so a client can collect one there and use it over UDP.
.PP
\fBWhat a verified cookie buys.\fR A security event from a UDP query is normally not attributed to the address the datagram claims \[u2014] counting it towards a ban would let anybody have a third party banned by spoofing them, and logging one per datagram is a log flood at packet rate \[u2014] so those events are aggregated and attributed to nobody. A UDP query carrying a cookie this listener issued \fIhas\fR completed a round trip, so its events are attributed and can drive a ban like a TCP client\(aqs. Turning cookies on is therefore what makes \fBdns_blocked\fR and \fBdns_tunnel\fR bans work for UDP clients.
.PP
\fBcookie_lifetime\fR (1h by default) is how long a server cookie stays valid. A cookie past half its life is replaced in the answer, so a client that keeps asking never reaches the end of one. The secret is per listener and per process: it is never written anywhere, so a restart costs each client one extra round trip, and two nodes of a cluster do not accept each other\(aqs cookies \[u2014] a client moving between them also costs one BADCOOKIE round trip and then works.
.PP
\fBxproxy_dns_cookies_total\fR counts the exchange by result (\fBissued\fR, \fBverified\fR, \fBrefused\fR), and the refusals appear as \fBcookie_required\fR, \fBcookie_missing\fR and \fBcookie_malformed\fR.
.PP
\fBThe cost.\fR Putting a cookie into a response means rebuilding the message, which loses the name compression the upstream used, so a cookie\-carrying client\(aqs answers are a little larger and a large answer is a little more likely to be truncated into a TCP retry. The cookie\(aqs own space is reserved before that decision, so the datagram is never oversize \[u2014] but on a listener where this matters, \fBcookies: off\fR is the setting.
.SS "server.listeners[].dns.ecs"
.PP
EDNS Client Subnet (RFC 7871) lets a resolver tell an authoritative server which network a query is really for, so the answer can be the one nearest the client. It exists for a recursive resolver talking to a content network, and this listener is neither: it forwards to a resolver that adds its own option describing this proxy, which is the correct thing for that resolver to describe.
.PP
Forwarding a client\(aqs own option breaks the cache instead. The cache key here is the question \[u2014] name, type and class \[u2014] and nothing else, as it is in every forwarder of this shape. An answer tailored to one client\(aqs subnet is therefore stored for every client of the listener: one client asking for a name on behalf of \fB203.0.113.0/24\fR decides which address the next thousand get. A client that can pick the subnet can pick the answer, which is cache poisoning with no spoofing and no race in it, and it is also a way to have this proxy ask an upstream about somebody else\(aqs network a query at a time.
.PP
So \fBecs: strip\fR (the default) removes the option from the forwarded query and counts \fBxproxy_dns_ecs_stripped_total\fR. The rest of the OPT record \[u2014] a cookie, padding, anything else the client sent \[u2014] is kept: dropping the record wholesale would forward a different query than the one the client asked. \fBecs: forward\fR passes the option through, and is for the deployment whose clients are one network. With \fBdnssec\fR on the option is gone regardless, because validation replaces the OPT record with one of its own.
.SS "server.listeners[].dns.tunnel_detection"
.PP
DNS tunnelling is the oldest way out of a network that filters everything else, and it still works, because a resolver is usually the one thing every host may talk to. The payload goes up in the query name \[u2014] a few dozen encoded characters per label \[u2014] and comes back down in the answer, most often TXT. The tunnel\(aqs domain is delegated to the other end, so every query reaches them whatever this resolver forwards to: blocking the upstream does nothing, and a block list only helps if somebody already knew the name.
.PP
What gives it away is not any one query but the shape of a client\(aqs traffic under one registered domain: names carrying more information per character than words do, hundreds of distinct subdomains where a service has a handful, answers that are mostly TXT, and a high rate of NXDOMAIN from the probing and the encoding that produce names nothing resolves.
.PP
.TS
