Two things about it are security decisions rather than protocol, and both are worth knowing before turning it on.
.PP
\fBThe address policy sees the IPv4 address, not the synthesised one.\fR \fB64:ff9b::7f00:1\fR is not inside \fB127.0.0.0/8\fR and no prefix list would catch it, but it is \fB127.0.0.1\fR to everything past the translator. So the A lookup runs through the ordinary path \[u2014] the same one a client\(aqs own A query takes \[u2014] which screens it against \fBanswer_policy\fR and caches it; an address that policy denies is not embedded. Without that, DNS64 would be a way around rebinding protection rather than a feature beside it.
.PP
\fBA synthesised answer is never signed and never says it is.\fR The reply is built from the client\(aqs question, so the AD bit is clear by construction (RFC 6147 section 5.5). A validating client that wants the truth about the name asks for A itself, which this resolver answers and validates normally.
.PP
A name with an AAAA record of its own is answered with it, and a name that does not exist stays NXDOMAIN: synthesising over either would be this resolver inventing a second answer. At most 32 records are synthesised from one A answer, so an upstream with hundreds of addresses does not decide the size of this listener\(aqs reply. \fBqueries_synthesised\fR counts them (\fBxproxy_dns_synthesised_total\fR) and the access log source ends in \fB:dns64\fR.
.SS "server.listeners[].dns.views"
.PP
Split horizon: the same name answered differently by who asked.
.PP
One name with two answers is an ordinary requirement rather than a trick. \fBapp.example.com\fR is a private address from inside the estate and a public one from outside; a laboratory network resolves a name to the test system while everybody else reaches production; a guest network is held to a stricter block list than the staff network. Without views the answer is two resolvers on two addresses and a routing decision somewhere else.
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.0i) lbw(1.0i) lbw(1.1i) lbx
lw(1.0i) lw(1.0i) lw(1.1i) lx.
