\fBxclient\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
After the proxy\(aqs own EHLO,\: send \fBXCLIENT ADDR=\: PORT=\fR (the Postfix extension) when the upstream advertises it,\: so the mail server\(aqs logs and policies see the real client
T}
.TE
.PP
Every session writes one \fBsmtp\fR line to the access log with the client address, whether it was encrypted, messages, octets, refusals, the endpoint and why it closed. AUTH arguments, challenges and responses are never logged: they carry the password. Counters: \fBsmtp_sessions\fR, \fBsmtp_sessions_open\fR, \fBsmtp_messages\fR, \fBsmtp_refused\fR, \fBsmtp_rejected\fR, \fBsmtp_tls_upgrades\fR, \fBsmtp_protocol_errors\fR, \fBsmtp_bytes_in\fR; the matching \fBxproxy_smtp_*\fR metrics. Protocol violations are \fBsmtp_denied\fR deny events, so a \fBbans.triggers\fR entry on that reason turns a prober into a ban.
.SS "server.listeners[].mqtt (kind: mqtt)"
.PP
A \fBkind: mqtt\fR listener is a protocol\-aware MQTT proxy for 3.1.1 (OASIS, also ISO/IEC 20922) and 5.0. Every control packet is read; the ones that carry a policy question \[u2014] who is connecting, what they publish, what they subscribe to \[u2014] are decided before they reach the broker, and the rest are forwarded untouched.
.PP
The reason it is not a layer 4 listener: an MQTT broker\(aqs authorisation is per topic, and a topic is a string inside a packet. Without reading the packets there is nowhere to say that a device may publish its own telemetry and nothing else, and a device holding a broker credential holds the whole tree.
.PP
What that gets you beyond a splice:
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBA subscription is a filter, not a topic.\fR \fBsensors/#\fR is allowed only when an entry of \fBsubscribe_allow\fR covers everything that filter could deliver, and refused when it could reach anything in \fBsubscribe_deny\fR. Matching a filter as though it were a topic is how \fB#\fR slips through an allow list of \fBsensors/+\fR.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBThe will goes through the publish policy.\fR A will is a message the broker publishes for the client after it is gone; checking it at CONNECT is the only moment there is.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBA malformed packet ends the session, forwarding nothing.\fR Its remaining length is what the next read depends on, so a packet the proxy could not parse is a stream it can no longer frame \[u2014] in either direction.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBA session begins with CONNECT and has exactly one.\fR A first packet of another type never reaches the broker, and a second CONNECT would take a new identity on a session already authorised as another.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBOnly the versions the proxy parses are accepted.\fR A version it cannot parse is a packet it cannot check, so there is no "accept anything" setting.
.RE
.PP
.PP
MQTT has no in\-band upgrade, so \fBtls_mode\fR is \fBimplicit\fR or nothing; a plaintext listener stays plaintext for the life of the session. The listener takes \fBaddress\fR, \fBmqtt\fR and \fBtls\fR; \fBproxy_protocol\fR works as on an HTTP listener, and bans and the global connection limits apply at accept. Changing the \fBmqtt\fR section rebinds the listener on reload.
.PP
.TS
