\fBThe metrics are not decoded.\fR A Sparkplug payload is protobuf and the metric set is the plant\(aqs own; carrying a schema per estate is not this proxy\(aqs business. The two top\-level fields \[u2014] the timestamp and the sequence number, two varints at a fixed place in every payload \[u2014] are read, and the rest is forwarded untouched.
.PP
Every session writes one \fBmqtt\fR line to the access log with the client address, client id, username, version, subscriptions, publications and why it closed. Counters: \fBmqtt_sessions\fR, \fBmqtt_sessions_open\fR, \fBmqtt_published\fR, \fBmqtt_subscribed\fR, \fBmqtt_refused\fR, \fBmqtt_rejected\fR, \fBmqtt_protocol_errors\fR; the matching \fBxproxy_mqtt_*\fR metrics. The refusal reasons a \fBtopics\fR rule or the Sparkplug policy adds are \fBpayload_too_large\fR, \fBqos_too_high\fR, \fBqos_too_low\fR, \fBretain_refused\fR, \fBsparkplug_not_sparkplug\fR, \fBsparkplug_namespace\fR, \fBsparkplug_message_type\fR, \fBsparkplug_command_refused\fR, \fBsparkplug_no_birth\fR and \fBsparkplug_sequence\fR. Refusals are \fBmqtt_denied\fR deny events, so a \fBbans.triggers\fR entry on that reason turns a device walking the topic tree into a ban.
.PP
A note on \fB$\fR: a wildcard at the first level of a filter never matches a topic beginning with \fB$\fR (MQTT 3.1.1 section 4.7), so \fB#\fR does not hand a client the broker\(aqs own \fB$SYS\fR tree. \fBsubscribe_deny: ["$SYS/#"]\fR is still worth writing, because it refuses the client that asks for it by name.
.SS "server.listeners[].syslog (kind: syslog)"
.PP
A \fBkind: syslog\fR listener is a relay that reads what it forwards.
.PP
Reading it is the point. Almost every field in a syslog record is written by the sender and believed by the collector: the host name, the facility, the severity, the time. A message claiming to be \fBauth.emerg\fR from another machine costs nothing to send. And a message whose text carries a newline becomes \fBtwo\fR records in any collector that frames on newlines \[u2014] the second one saying whatever the sender wanted a record to say, with a priority of its own.
.PP
So every message is parsed, and every message is re\-emitted as RFC 5424 in one framing, whatever arrived. One dialect out is what makes the record a collector stores the record this relay decided about: a newline in the text is written as a visible symbol, a line ending in a structured data value is escaped, and a field that cannot appear in a header is replaced.
.PP
Both transports: TCP (RFC 6587 framing, either kind) and UDP (RFC 5426, one datagram per message), on the same address. TLS is RFC 5425.
.PP
.TS
