.PP
\fBThe secure upgrade\fR is \fBtls_mode: none\fR with \fBupstream_tls_mode: implicit\fR: a device that can only send clear syslog over UDP writes to this listener, and the records leave it as RFC 5425 TLS. It does not make the sender trustworthy \[u2014] between the device and this port the records are still in clear and still forgeable \[u2014] so put the port where only those devices can reach it, keep \fBallow_senders\fR tight, and use \fBhostname: observed\fR.
.PP
A message the relay cannot parse is refused, not forwarded: its facility, severity and host are exactly the fields every rule here decides on, and a record nobody could read is a record nobody can filter. A message with no timestamp is given the time the relay saw it, because a record nobody can order is a record that is hard to use.
.PP
On a delimited stream a message over the bound is dropped and the connection carries on \[u2014] the reader skips to the next line ending, so one long line does not cost every record behind it. On a counted stream it ends the connection, because refusing to read the octets a frame declared leaves the reader at an offset nobody knows.
.PP
Counters: \fBsyslog_received\fR, \fBsyslog_forwarded\fR, \fBsyslog_dropped\fR, \fBsyslog_refused\fR, \fBsyslog_rate_limited\fR, \fBsyslog_redacted\fR, \fBsyslog_queue_dropped\fR, \fBsyslog_send_failed\fR, \fBsyslog_connections\fR, \fBsyslog_rejected\fR. Refusals are \fBsyslog_denied\fR for the ban triggers.
.SS "server.listeners[].modbus (kind: modbus)"
.PP
A \fBkind: modbus\fR listener is a Modbus relay that reads every frame and decides about it.
.PP
Modbus is the protocol that runs the plant floor and has no security properties at all: no authentication, no integrity, no session. A frame says which device it is for, what to do and where, and the device does it. The devices cannot be fixed \[u2014] they are a decade old, the vendor is gone, and the process they run does not stop \[u2014] so the only place a policy can exist is in the path.
.PP
It works in both directions:
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBreverse\fR (the default): masters connect here and the relay dials the devices. This is how a PLC that cannot be patched gets an allow list, a read\-only historian, a value bound on a setpoint and an audit trail.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBforward\fR: the plant\(aqs masters use this listener as the controlled egress to reach devices elsewhere, and the \fBroutes\fR say which destination each unit identifier may reach at all. A unit no route claims is refused with a gateway exception rather than sent somewhere invented.
.RE
.PP
.PP
All three framings, in either direction: Modbus/TCP (MBAP), and the two serial framings every "Modbus gateway" ever sold tunnels over TCP \[u2014] Modbus RTU and Modbus ASCII. \fBframing\fR is what arrives, \fBupstream_framing\fR is what leaves, and setting them differently makes this listener a protocol converter, which is what a serial device behind a terminal server needs.
.PP
.TS
