.SS "What the relay decides, and in what order"
.PP
1. The client address, against \fBdeny_clients\fR then \fBallow_clients\fR. 2. TLS and the role, when \fBsecurity\fR asks for one. A role can only come from a certificate and a certificate can only come from TLS, so a listener asking for roles without TLS refuses every session and says \fBsecurity_requires_tls\fR rather than leaving a mystery. 3. The frame, whole. A frame that does not parse is refused: a relay that forwarded what it could not read would be forwarding what it could not decide about, and the device behind it will read those bytes somehow. 4. \fBread_only\fR, which no rule can override. 5. \fBunits\fR. 6. The rules, in order. The first \fBallow\fR or \fBdeny\fR decides; an \fBobserve\fR rule records and the search continues. A frame that matches no rule takes \fBdefault_action\fR. 7. The value bounds of the rule that matched. A value outside them is refused \fBby that rule\fR rather than falling through to a later rule that would permit it, because a bound that can be escaped by writing another rule underneath it is not a bound.
.SS "Architectural decisions"
.PP
\fBRequests are serialised towards each device.\fR A Modbus slave has one scan. A relay that pipelined into it would be turning a policy engine into a load generator, so each session holds one connection per route and one request in flight at a time, with \fBmax_pending\fR bounding the queue behind it. A master whose queue is full is answered with exception 06 (server busy), which is the protocol\(aqs own way of saying what happened. The serial framings default to \fBmax_pending: 1\fR because they have no transaction identifier: a second request in flight could not be told from the first.
.PP
\fBA forwarded frame keeps its bytes.\fR The frame that reaches the device is the frame that arrived, byte for byte, and the answer that reaches the master is the answer that arrived. The relay re\-encodes only when it must \[u2014] the framings differ, or \fBunit_override\fR rewrote the identifier \[u2014] because re\-encoding a frame is how a relay and a device come to disagree about what was said. The MBAP transaction identifier is the one exception in the other direction: the master\(aqs own value is put back on the answer, whatever the relay used towards the device, because that is the field the master matches on.
.PP
\fBA refusal is Modbus, not a disconnection.\fR \fBdeny_response: exception\fR answers with the exception a master already understands \[u2014] illegal function for a code the policy does not permit, illegal data address for a range it does not, illegal data value for a value outside a bound, gateway path unavailable for a unit with no route \[u2014] so a master\(aqs diagnostics say something true and the session carries on. \fBdrop\fR and \fBclose\fR are there for the cases where a master must not learn anything from the answer.
.PP
\fBThe device\(aqs own answer is checked too.\fR A response the relay cannot parse, or one answering for a different unit identifier, is not handed to the master: the two would read the same bytes differently, which is the whole class of bug this relay exists to prevent. The master gets exception 04 (server failure) and the event is logged.
.PP
\fBLearning is observe\-only unless it says otherwise.\fR \fBlearn.enforce\fR defaults to false, and validation warns while it is off, because a learning run left on by accident is a relay that decides nothing.
.PP
Counters: \fBmodbus_sessions\fR, \fBmodbus_sessions_open\fR, \fBmodbus_requests\fR, \fBmodbus_responses\fR, \fBmodbus_denied\fR, \fBmodbus_would_deny\fR, \fBmodbus_exceptions\fR, \fBmodbus_malformed\fR, \fBmodbus_refused\fR, \fBmodbus_rejected\fR, \fBmodbus_rate_limited\fR, \fBmodbus_queue_full\fR, \fBmodbus_upstream_failed\fR, \fBmodbus_traced\fR, \fBmodbus_learned\fR. Refusals are \fBmodbus_denied\fR for the ban triggers, and the fine\-grained reason is in the refusal counters: \fBclient_not_allowed\fR, \fBtls_handshake\fR, \fBno_client_certificate\fR, \fBno_role\fR, \fBrole_not_allowed\fR, \fBsecurity_requires_tls\fR, \fBframing\fR, \fBframe_too_large\fR, \fBmalformed\fR, \fBmalformed_response\fR, \fBresponse_unit_mismatch\fR, \fBno_route_for_unit\fR, \fBmax_connections\fR, \fBrate_limit\fR, \fBqueue_full\fR, \fBread_only\fR, \fBread_only_unknown_function\fR, \fBunit_not_allowed\fR, \fBrule_deny\fR, \fBno_rule\fR, \fBvalue_out_of_range\fR, \fBvalue_delta\fR, \fBvalue_transition\fR, \fBvalue_rate\fR, \fBvalue_no_select\fR, \fBvalue_unknown\fR, \fBvalue_masked_write\fR, \fBcoil_set_not_allowed\fR, \fBcoil_clear_not_allowed\fR. A selector that does not match is not a refusal of its own: the frame falls through to the next rule, and to \fBno_rule\fR if none matches.
.SS "server.listeners[].iec104 (kind: iec104)"
.PP
IEC 60870\-5\-104 is the protocol that operates electricity transmission and distribution, and it is the grid\(aqs Modbus: a controlling station (the SCADA master) and a controlled station (a substation gateway or an RTU) exchange application service data units over a plain TCP connection on port 2404, with no authentication, no integrity and no confidentiality anywhere in the standard. Anyone who can reach the gateway can trip a breaker on it. IEC 62351\-3 adds TLS and is almost nowhere deployed, because the controlled stations are substation equipment with twenty\-year service lives.
.PP
What the protocol \fIdoes\fR have, and Modbus does not, is a structure that says what a message means, and that structure is what this relay enforces:
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
Every I\-format frame carries a \fBtype identification\fR (what this is), a \fBcause of transmission\fR (why it was sent), an \fBoriginator address\fR (which control centre) and a \fBcommon address\fR (which station).
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
The \fBprocess commands\fR \-\- single, double, regulating step, setpoint, bitstring \-\- are a small numbered set, and so are the \fBsystem commands\fR, two of which reset a station and move its clock.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
The dangerous commands have a \fBtwo\-step form\fR: \fIselect\fR (an activation with the S/E bit set) then \fIexecute\fR. The standard describes it; the equipment mostly does not enforce it.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
Every I frame is \fBsequence numbered in both directions\fR, which is the only thing in the protocol that can detect a lost, duplicated or replayed frame.
.RE
.PP
.PP
So the policy here is written in those terms rather than in ports: which stations may be addressed, which commands may be sent to which points by whom, whether a command has to be selected before it is executed, and whether the numbering adds up.
.PP
\fBThere is no per\-address routing on this kind, on purpose.\fR An IEC 104 connection is a long\-lived \fIassociation\fR between one controlling station and one controlled station, and the first frame a control centre sends is \fBSTARTDT_act\fR \-\- a U\-format frame with no common address in it. A relay that chose a pool from the common address could not choose one until the first I frame, by which time the association is up and the handshake answered. One listener per association is the honest shape; \fBcommon_addresses\fR is what bounds which stations may be addressed through it.
.PP
.TS
