.PP
\fBWhat is checked before the rules, and cannot be shadowed.\fR A frame the relay could not read is refused whether or not the listener is enforcing: forwarding what it cannot decide about would hand the substation octets it will read somehow. So are the rate limits, and so is a \fIstation\fR sending an activation to its own control centre \-\- not a shape the standard has, and what a compromised substation gateway pivoting upstream looks like.
.PP
\fBSelect\-before\-operate is narrow on purpose.\fR A selection belongs to the connection that made it, to one common address, one information object address and one type identification, and it expires. A selection that outlived its connection would let a later client execute on an earlier one\(aqs intention; one that never expired would let an execute sent hours later operate a breaker somebody selected and thought better of. A selection authorises one execution, a deactivation withdraws it, and the bitstring commands \-\- which have no two\-step form in the standard \-\- are exempt, because requiring a selection for them would refuse every use of them for ever.
.PP
\fBA sequence gap is one refusal.\fR The state moves to what arrived, so a single lost frame does not take a substation off the air until somebody restarts the link.
.PP
\fBThe metric payloads are never decoded.\fR A measurement\(aqs scaled value, quality descriptor and timestamp are forwarded untouched. Decoding every one of the hundred\-odd type identifications would be a second implementation of the standard, and a relay that got one wrong would corrupt a reading nobody could trace. What is read is the ASDU header, the object addresses and a command\(aqs qualifier \-\- which is what a policy is written about. On a \fIsequence\fR ASDU only the first information object address is on the wire, so that is the one an \fBaddresses\fR rule checks.
.PP
Counters: \fBiec104_sessions\fR, \fBiec104_sessions_open\fR, \fBiec104_frames\fR, \fBiec104_commands\fR, \fBiec104_system_commands\fR, \fBiec104_denied\fR, \fBiec104_would_deny\fR, \fBiec104_malformed\fR, \fBiec104_rejected\fR, \fBiec104_rate_limited\fR, \fBiec104_selects\fR, \fBiec104_executes\fR, \fBiec104_unselected\fR, \fBiec104_selects_held\fR, \fBiec104_sequence_gaps\fR, \fBiec104_window_full\fR, \fBiec104_upstream_failed\fR. Refusals are \fBiec104_denied\fR for the ban triggers, and the fine\-grained reason is in the refusal counters: \fBclient_not_allowed\fR, \fBtls_handshake\fR, \fBmalformed\fR, \fBframe_too_long\fR, \fBmax_connections\fR, \fBrate_limited\fR, \fBcommand_rate_limited\fR, \fBmonitor_only\fR, \fBcommon_address\fR, \fBrule\fR, \fBdefault_deny\fR, \fBcontrol\fR, \fBstation_command\fR, \fBsequence\fR, \fBwindow\fR, \fBack_ahead\fR, \fBunselected\fR, \fBselect_unavailable\fR.
.SS "server.listeners[].ldap (kind: ldap)"
.PP
A directory is the one service in an estate that knows who everybody is, and LDAP is how everything asks. That makes it two things at once: the authentication path for every application that has not moved to OIDC, and the most complete map of an organisation that exists anywhere on its network \-\- every person, every group, every service account, every machine, with the memberships that say who is an administrator.
.PP
Five things are deliberate in the data path.
.PP
\fBA bind with a name and an empty password is refused by default.\fR RFC 4513 \[u00A7]5.1.2 calls it an \fIunauthenticated bind\fR and says it is anonymous. A great many directories answer it with \fBsuccess\fR, and a great many applications are written as "bind as the user, and if it worked the password was right". That is an authentication bypass in the application, reachable with an empty string, and the directory cannot tell the difference between it and a correct login. The relay can. \fBmethods\fR is the list, and it defaults to \fBsimple\fR and \fBsasl\fR \-\- the two that actually authenticate.
.PP
\fBA password in the clear is refused by default.\fR LDAP on port 389 with a simple bind puts a directory password on the wire in plaintext, and the client library that did it will not tell anyone. \fBrequire_tls\fR (default true) stops it, and it is \fBnot shadowable\fR: by the time a policy could be consulted the password has already gone past, so a listener in \fBpolicy: {mode: shadow}\fR refuses it anyway. SASL \fBPLAIN\fR counts as a password, because it is a simple bind with extra steps.
.PP
\fBThe attribute policy applies to the answer, not only to the question.\fR A search that asks for \fB*\fR never names \fBuserPassword\fR, and the directory sends it anyway. So a denied attribute is \fBremoved from the entry on its way back\fR \-\- which is the half a request\-side access list cannot do \-\- while a request that names one plainly is refused, because stripping it would answer a plain question with a silence the client cannot tell from an empty directory. A \fIfilter\fR naming one is refused too: \fB(userPassword=a*)\fR is a password oracle, one character at a time.
.PP
\fBA subtree is a suffix compared one relative name at a time.\fR A string suffix test admits \fBdc=notexample,dc=com\fR under \fBexample,dc=com\fR, and a string prefix test admits \fBou=peoplex\fR under \fBou=people\fR. Distinguished names are a tree and are compared as one, with RFC 4514\(aqs escaping resolved, the attribute type folded to lower case and the insignificant space removed.
.PP
\fBThe entries are counted and the filter is measured.\fR An unbounded subtree search with \fB(objectClass=*)\fR is how a directory is copied, and a filter of a hundred substring terms with leading wildcards is a hundred full scans from a request two hundred octets long. \fBmax_entries\fR cuts the search with the directory\(aqs own \fBsizeLimitExceeded\fR, so the client knows it got part of an answer rather than hanging; \fBmax_filter_terms\fR, \fBmax_filter_depth\fR and \fBallow_leading_wildcard\fR bound the filter.
.PP
.TS
