.TE
.PP
\fBThe built\-in \fBdeny_attributes\fR list\fR is the password and key material of the directories people actually run:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
userPassword  unicodePwd  dBCSPwd  ntPwdHistory  lmPwdHistory  pwdHistory
supplementalCredentials  msDS\-ManagedPassword  ms\-Mcs\-AdmPwd
ms\-Mcs\-AdmPwdExpirationTime  krbPrincipalKey  sambaNTPassword
sambaLMPassword  sambaPasswordHistory  userPKCS12
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
Naming \fBdeny_attributes\fR replaces it rather than adding to it, and validation warns if the replacement drops \fBuserPassword\fR. A rule\(aqs own \fBdeny_attributes\fR adds to whichever list is in force. An attribute\(aqs transfer option is not part of its name, so a policy about \fBuserCertificate\fR covers \fBuserCertificate;binary\fR.
.PP
\fBWhat is checked before the rules, and cannot be shadowed.\fR A message the relay could not parse; a message past \fBmax_message_bytes\fR; message identifier zero, which is reserved for the server\(aqs unsolicited notification; the request and bind rate limits; the client list; a response the directory sent against a message identifier no request used; and a bind carrying a password on an unprotected connection.
.PP
\fB\fBread_only\fR and \fBbase_dns\fR are checked before any rule and cannot be overridden by one.\fR A read\-only listener one rule could write through is not a read\-only listener, and a listener for one naming context that could be asked about another is not a listener for one naming context.
.PP
\fBThe identity is the directory\(aqs to grant.\fR The relay watches the bind \fIresponse\fR, not the request: the identity a rule names is adopted only when the directory answers success, because believing the request would let anyone be anybody by binding with the wrong password. A StartTLS upgrade discards it, as RFC 4513 \[u00A7]5.1.7 requires \-\- and because keeping it would let a client bind in the clear and then hide behind TLS with what that bind gave it.
.PP
\fBA password never reaches a log\fR, and neither does a filter\(aqs assertion value. The filter\(aqs \fIshape\fR is logged \-\- how many terms, how deep, whether a leading wildcard \-\- and what it was looking for is not: a search for a person\(aqs name is that person\(aqs business, and a log of every one is a surveillance record the estate did not ask for.
.PP
Counters: \fBldap_sessions\fR, \fBldap_sessions_open\fR, \fBldap_requests\fR, \fBldap_binds\fR, \fBldap_bind_failures\fR, \fBldap_searches\fR, \fBldap_writes\fR, \fBldap_entries\fR, \fBldap_stripped\fR, \fBldap_truncated\fR, \fBldap_starttls\fR, \fBldap_denied\fR, \fBldap_would_deny\fR, \fBldap_malformed\fR, \fBldap_rejected\fR, \fBldap_rate_limited\fR, \fBldap_upstream_failed\fR, \fBldap_outstanding\fR. Refusals are \fBldap_denied\fR for the ban triggers, and the fine\-grained reason is in the refusal counters: \fBclient_not_allowed\fR, \fBtls_handshake\fR, \fBupstream_tls\fR, \fBmax_connections\fR, \fBrate_limited\fR, \fBbind_rate_limited\fR, \fBmalformed\fR, \fBmalformed_response\fR, \fBmessage_too_large\fR, \fBframing\fR, \fBmessage_id_zero\fR, \fBwrong_direction\fR, \fBwrong_direction_response\fR, \fBanonymous_bind\fR, \fBunauthenticated_bind\fR, \fBbind_method\fR, \fBsasl_mechanism\fR, \fBbind_in_clear\fR, \fBbind_failed\fR, \fBversion\fR, \fBread_only\fR, \fBbase_dn\fR, \fBextended\fR, \fBcontrol\fR, \fBfilter_terms\fR, \fBfilter_depth\fR, \fBleading_wildcard\fR, \fBfilter_attribute\fR, \fBattribute\fR, \fBattribute_not_allowed\fR, \fBrule\fR, \fBdefault_deny\fR, \fBmax_entries\fR, \fBunsolicited_entry\fR, \fBtoo_many_outstanding\fR, \fBstarttls_unavailable\fR, \fBstarttls_twice\fR, \fBstarttls_outstanding\fR.
.SS "server.listeners[].snmp (kind: snmp)"
.PP
SNMP runs every switch, router, printer, uninterruptible supply and building controller in an estate, and versions 1 and 2c authenticate with a community string: a cleartext password in every datagram, \fBpublic\fR to read and \fBprivate\fR to write on anything nobody reconfigured, with no integrity, no replay protection and no confidentiality. One datagram reads a device\(aqs whole configuration; one changes it. Version 3 has a real security model and also has \fBnoAuthNoPriv\fR, which is version 2c with more fields.
.PP
The devices cannot be fixed \-\- they are switches and printers and building controllers with firmware nobody ships updates for \-\- so the relay is the only place a policy can live.
.PP
Five things are deliberate in the data path.
.PP
\fBEvery message is parsed whole.\fR A relay that forwarded what it could not read would be forwarding what it could not decide about, and the agent behind it will read those octets somehow.
.PP
\fBThe amplification is bounded in two directions.\fR SNMP is a classic reflection vector: a forty\-octet GETBULK with a repetition count of ten thousand asks for a response of megabytes, to whatever address the datagram claimed to come from. So a repetition count past \fBmax_repetitions\fR is \fIlowered\fR rather than refused \-\- a poller asking for more than it should get still gets an answer, which is what keeps the bound deployable in an estate whose pollers nobody can reconfigure \-\- and a response past \fBmax_response_bytes\fR, or more than \fBmax_response_ratio\fR times the size of the request that asked for it, is refused outright.
.PP
\fBA response is matched to its request.\fR The request identifier is the only thing in the protocol that pairs them, so an answer nobody asked for is recognisable \-\- and on UDP that is the shape of a response\-spoofing attack on the manager: an answer to a question it did ask, from somewhere else, arriving first. The table of outstanding requests is bounded by \fBmax_pending\fR, and a full table refuses the new request rather than forgetting an old one, because forgetting would make that pairing unreliable.
.PP
\fBThe version can be rewritten downwards.\fR \fBupgrade_version\fR is the secure upgrade: a manager speaks v3 with authentication to this relay, or TLS on the stream side, and the relay speaks v2c to a switch whose firmware has neither, with an \fBupstream_community\fR the manager never needs to know. A response is rebuilt in the version its request arrived in, so the manager sees the version it spoke.
.PP
Two rewrites this relay will not do, both because it holds no USM keys and will not forge an authentication that did not happen. It cannot \fIproduce\fR v3, which is refused at load. And it cannot downgrade a v3 \fBrequest\fR: the answer would come back as v2c and handing that to a v3 manager means authenticating it with a key that is not here, so such a request is refused rather than half\-translated. A v3 \fBnotification\fR downgrades cleanly, because nothing comes back \-\- a modern device sending v3 traps to a collector that understands only v2c is exactly what \fBtraps: true\fR with \fBupgrade_version: v2c\fR is for.
.PP
\fBThe values are not interpreted.\fR A binding\(aqs type and extent are read; what a \fBCounter64\fR \fImeans\fR is not. A policy about values would need a MIB per estate, and a relay that mis\-decoded one would corrupt a reading nobody could trace.
.PP
A listener always accepts the streams of RFC 3430 on its port, and \fBtransport: udp\fR (the default) adds the datagram socket every poller and every agent actually speaks. RFC 6353 TLS is the stream half, on port 10161; DTLS on 10162 is not implemented, so a listener that is TLS throughout is \fBtransport: tcp\fR.
.PP
.TS
