\fB8192\fR
T}	T{
One response. The other half of the amplification bound: \fBmax_\:repetitions\fR bounds what was asked for and this bounds what came back,\: and an agent that ignores the first still cannot get past the second
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:response_\:ratio\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB50\fR
T}	T{
Refuse a response more than this many times the size of its request. The bound that is about \fIreflection\fR rather than size: a large answer to a large question is a walk,\: and a large answer to a tiny question is an amplifier
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:pending\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB32\fR
T}	T{
Requests outstanding towards agents,\: per datagram listener and per stream session
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:connections\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB32\fR
T}	T{
Live stream sessions
T}
T{
\fBidle_\:timeout\fR
T}	T{
duration
T}	T{
\fB60s\fR
T}	T{
A stream session that says nothing
T}
T{
\fBrequest_\:timeout\fR
T}	T{
duration
T}	T{
\fB5s\fR
T}	T{
How long the agent has to answer before its answer is too late to forward
T}
T{
\fBconnect_\:timeout\fR
T}	T{
duration
T}	T{
\fB5s\fR
T}	T{
Dialling the agent on the stream path
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:message_\:bytes\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB8192\fR
T}	T{
One message;\: the protocol\(aqs own floor is 484 octets and 1472 is what fits an Ethernet datagram. A message past this is refused unread
T}
T{
\fBrate_\:limit\fR,\: \fBrate_\:burst\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB0\fR
T}	T{
Messages per second per client address. An SNMP poll is periodic and its rate is known,\: so this bound is unusually easy to set correctly
T}
T{
\fBlog_\:messages\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
An access line per message. A poller asks the same questions every thirty seconds,\: so this is a lot of lines
T}
T{
\fBlog_\:writes\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBtrue\fR
T}	T{
An access line for every SetRequest and every refusal,\: leaving the polling alone: what was \fIchanged\fR through this relay is the record an estate is asked for
T}
T{
\fBalert_\:on_\:deny\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBtrue\fR
T}	T{
A security event per refusal
T}
T{
\fBproxy_\:protocol\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
Send a PROXY protocol v2 header to the agent on the stream path
T}
.TE
.SS "server.listeners[].snmp.rules[]"
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.2i) lbw(0.8i) lbx
lw(1.2i) lw(0.8i) lx.
T{
Key
T}	T{
Type
T}	T{
Description
T}
T{
\fBname\fR
T}	T{
string
T}	T{
Required;\: names the rule in the logs and the counters
T}
T{
\fBaction\fR
T}	T{
\fBallow\fR,\: \fBdeny\fR,\: \fBobserve\fR
T}	T{
Default \fBallow\fR. \fBobserve\fR records the message and keeps looking,\: which is how a rule is tried on live traffic before it decides anything
T}
T{
\fBclients\fR
T}	T{
list of CIDR
T}	T{
Networks the manager is in
T}
T{
\fBversions\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
The protocol versions this rule covers
T}
T{
\fBcommunities\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
The community strings (v1 and v2c) this rule covers. A rule naming communities cannot match a v3 message,\: and one naming users cannot match a v2c one: letting either cross over would make a rule written about one authentication scheme apply to another
T}
T{
\fBusers\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
The v3 USM user names this rule covers
T}
T{
\fBmin_\:security_\:level\fR
T}	T{
string
T}	T{
The lowest v3 level this rule covers,\: so that "this subtree only with authPriv" is one rule
T}
T{
\fBpdus\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
Operations by name: \fBget\fR,\: \fBget_\:next\fR,\: \fBget_\:bulk\fR,\: \fBset\fR,\: \fBtrap\fR,\: \fBtrap_\:v1\fR,\: \fBinform\fR,\: \fBresponse\fR,\: \fBreport\fR
T}
T{
\fBaccess\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
\fBread\fR,\: \fBwrite\fR,\: \fBnotify\fR: what the operation \fIdoes\fR. The durable way to write a policy,\: because it does not change when a later revision adds an operation
T}
T{
\fBoids\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
Object identifier subtrees,\: as \fB1.\:3.\:6.\:1.\:2.\:1\fR or a single object. A message naming an object outside all of them does not match. The comparison is per sub\-identifier,\: so \fB1.\:3.\:6.\:1.\:2.\:1\fR does not cover \fB1.\:3.\:6.\:1.\:2.\:11\fR \-\- a policy written with string prefixes allows a subtree nobody named
T}
T{
\fBdeny_\:oids\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
Subtrees this rule does not cover even when \fBoids\fR would match,\: which is how an exception inside an allowed subtree is written: all of mib\-2 except the ARP table
T}
T{
\fBwrite_\:oids\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
Apply instead of \fBoids\fR to a SetRequest,\: so one rule can allow a wide read and a narrow write. The write list \fIreplaces\fR the read list rather than adding to it
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:repetitions\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
This rule\(aqs own GETBULK bound. A rule does not cover traffic past its own bound,\: so the next rule \-\- or the default \-\- decides;\: matching and then allowing would make the bound a suggestion
T}
T{
\fBcontexts\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
The v3 context names this rule covers,\: for an engine that fronts several agents
T}
T{
\fBschedule\fR
T}	T{
object
T}	T{
\fB{days,\: from,\: to,\: timezone}\fR;\: a window whose \fBto\fR is before its \fBfrom\fR spans midnight and belongs to the day it started on
T}
.TE
.PP
\fBWhat is checked before the rules, and cannot be shadowed.\fR A message the relay could not parse is refused whether or not the listener is enforcing: forwarding what it cannot decide about would hand the agent octets it will read somehow. So are the message and response bounds, the repetition bound, the response ratio, an answer no request matched, a message arriving from the agent side that is not an answer, the rate limit and the client list. A relay whose amplification bounds were in shadow mode would be a working amplifier.
.PP
\fB\fBread_only\fR is checked before any rule and cannot be overridden by one.\fR A read\-only listener that a single rule could write through is not a read\-only listener.
.PP
\fBAn encrypted v3 payload is decided about, not inspected.\fR When the security level is \fBauthPriv\fR the scoped PDU is ciphertext: the header parses, the user and the level are checked, and there is no operation and no object identifier to decide about. The decision says \fBsnmp_encrypted\fR rather than refusing traffic the listener was configured to carry or pretending it was inspected.
.PP
\fBA community string is never written to a log.\fR It is a credential, and an access log or security event that printed every guessed one would be a list of the estate\(aqs passwords with a timestamp beside each. The v3 user name \fIis\fR logged, because it is an identity rather than a secret.
.PP
Counters: \fBsnmp_messages\fR, \fBsnmp_sessions\fR, \fBsnmp_sessions_open\fR, \fBsnmp_reads\fR, \fBsnmp_writes\fR, \fBsnmp_traps\fR, \fBsnmp_denied\fR, \fBsnmp_would_deny\fR, \fBsnmp_malformed\fR, \fBsnmp_rejected\fR, \fBsnmp_rate_limited\fR, \fBsnmp_amplified\fR, \fBsnmp_truncated\fR, \fBsnmp_upgraded\fR, \fBsnmp_timed_out\fR, \fBsnmp_upstream_failed\fR, \fBsnmp_unsolicited\fR, \fBsnmp_pending\fR. Refusals are \fBsnmp_denied\fR for the ban triggers, and the fine\-grained reason is in the refusal counters: \fBclient_not_allowed\fR, \fBtls_handshake\fR, \fBupstream_tls\fR, \fBmalformed\fR, \fBmalformed_response\fR, \fBmessage_too_large\fR, \fBframing\fR, \fBmax_connections\fR, \fBrate_limited\fR, \fBversion\fR, \fBcommunity\fR, \fBuser\fR, \fBsecurity_level\fR, \fBread_only\fR, \fBdirection\fR, \fBvar_binds\fR, \fBrule\fR, \fBdefault_deny\fR, \fBmax_repetitions\fR, \fBresponse_too_large\fR, \fBresponse_ratio\fR, \fBresponse_too_late\fR, \fBunsolicited_response\fR, \fBencrypted_response\fR, \fBwrong_direction\fR, \fBtoo_many_pending\fR, \fBupgrade_failed\fR.
.SS "server.listeners[].dhcp (kind: dhcp)"
.PP
DHCP is the one protocol where \fBanswering\fR is the attack. A client broadcasts "who will configure me", and it believes whatever answers first: its address, its \fBdefault route\fR, its \fBresolvers\fR, its \fBproxy\fR (option 252) and, on a machine that boots from the network, the \fBfile it boots\fR (options 66 and 67). Nothing in the exchange authenticates anybody \-\- a transaction identifier and a hardware address, both visible to everyone on the segment \-\- and the client has no address yet, so it cannot even be told apart by one.
.PP
That makes this the one relay kind here whose interesting half faces \fIupstream\fR. Five things are deliberate.
.PP
\fBA reply from an address this listener does not admit as a server is dropped before it is read.\fR Every switch vendor sells this as DHCP snooping and implements it as a trusted port; here it is \fBallow_servers\fR, and it is \fBnot shadowable\fR: a listener that evaluated the list without enforcing it would be a listener that relays a rogue server\(aqs answer and writes it down. Leaving the list out is not leaving it open \-\- it is filled in from the \fBupstream\fR pool\(aqs own endpoints at load, because an operator who wrote none meant "the servers I configured".
.PP
\fBThe options a server sends are a configuration, not data.\fR Option 121 and Microsoft\(aqs 249 are a routing table in a broadcast reply \-\- the most direct interception in the protocol. Option 252 is a proxy. Options 66, 67 and 43 are what a machine boots. Each is an option some estate legitimately needs, so each is a decision: \fBdeny_options\fR has a built\-in list, \fBallow_options\fR turns the policy inside out, and \fBon_denied_option\fR defaults to \fBstrip\fR because a client that still gets its address and no longer gets a route it should not have is a client that works.
.PP
\fBThe addresses in a reply are checked against the estate\(aqs own.\fR An operator knows their gateways, their resolvers and their boot servers; a reply naming anything else is wrong \fIwhoever sent it\fR, and that check catches a compromised real server as surely as a rogue one. The boot server is checked in both places it lives \-\- option 66 and the \fBsiaddr\fR header field \-\- because a check on one has a way round it.
.PP
\fBA client does not get to say which segment it is on.\fR RFC 3046 \[u00A7]2.1 says a relay discards the agent information option arriving from a client, because the option exists so that the \fIrelay\fR tells the server which circuit the request came from. So option 82 from a client is stripped and this relay adds its own; on the way back it removes what it added, because the option is a note between the relay and the server.
.PP
\fBThe starvation bound is keyed on the hardware address.\fR Pool exhaustion is one host sending thousands of DISCOVERs with a made\-up address in each, and a rate limit keyed on the source address would see one sender doing nothing unusual. \fBmax_clients\fR bounds the table that does the keying, because otherwise the flood of new addresses would exhaust that instead.
.PP
.TS
