lbw(1.1i) lbw(0.9i) lbw(1.1i) lbx
lw(1.1i) lw(0.9i) lw(1.1i) lx.
T{
Key
T}	T{
Type
T}	T{
Default
T}	T{
Description
T}
T{
\fBmode\fR
T}	T{
\fBreverse\fR,\: \fBforward\fR
T}	T{
\fBreverse\fR
T}	T{
\fBreverse\fR: clients send here and the relay forwards to the servers. \fBforward\fR: this listener is the controlled egress a downstream relay agent uses to reach a server elsewhere
T}
T{
\fBupstream\fR
T}	T{
string
T}	T{
required
T}	T{
The server pool
T}
T{
\fBallow_\:servers\fR
T}	T{
list of CIDR
T}	T{
the pool\(aqs endpoints
T}	T{
The addresses a reply may come from,\: and the most valuable line in the file. Empty means the endpoints of \fBupstream\fR,\: so it is never open by accident;\: a pool whose endpoints are named by hostname makes this required,\: because guessing is the wrong kind of helpful on the one check this kind most depends on
T}
T{
\fBallow_\:clients\fR
T}	T{
list of CIDR
T}	T{
all
T}	T{
Networks a message may arrive from. On a segment this is every client \-\- a client with no address sends from \fB0.\:0.\:0.\:0\fR,\: and the unspecified address is admitted whatever the list says,\: because refusing it would refuse every first\-time client. It is the useful control on a listener that fronts \fBother relay agents\fR
T}
T{
\fBdeny_\:clients\fR
T}	T{
list of CIDR
T}	T{

T}	T{
Evaluated before \fBallow_\:clients\fR,\: and it beats the unspecified address too
T}
T{
\fBmessage_\:types\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
\fB[discover,\: request,\: decline,\: release,\: inform]\fR
T}	T{
Types a client may send. The default leaves out the \fBlease\-query family\fR (RFC 4388): that is a relay agent\(aqs own diagnostic,\: and an inventory of every lease in the estate to anything else
T}
T{
\fBdeny_\:options\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
the built\-in list
T}	T{
Options a \fBserver\fR may not send;\: see below for the built\-in list. Setting the list \fBreplaces\fR it. An option may be named or numbered,\: so an estate\(aqs own vendor option can be named
T}
T{
\fBallow_\:options\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{

T}	T{
The positive model: an option outside the list is removed. On a network whose clients need six options this is shorter and safer than a deny list. The message type and the server identifier are always carried,\: because a reply without them is not a reply
T}
T{
\fBon_\:denied_\:option\fR
T}	T{
\fBstrip\fR,\: \fBdeny\fR
T}	T{
\fBstrip\fR
T}	T{
\fBstrip\fR removes the option and forwards the rest. \fBdeny\fR refuses the whole reply,\: which leaves the client with \fBno address at all\fR \-\- which is why it is not the default
T}
T{
\fBdeny_\:requested_\:options\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{

T}	T{
Options a client may not \fBask\fR for,\: in its option 55 parameter list. The ask is trimmed rather than the message refused: a client that asked for a proxy and is not told about one is a client that works
T}
T{
\fBallow_\:gateways\fR
T}	T{
list of IPv4
T}	T{
any
T}	T{
The addresses option 3 may name
T}
T{
\fBallow_\:resolvers\fR
T}	T{
list of IPv4
T}	T{
any
T}	T{
The addresses option 6 may name
T}
T{
\fBallow_\:boot_\:servers\fR
T}	T{
list of IPv4
T}	T{
any
T}	T{
The addresses option 66 and \fBsiaddr\fR may name
T}
T{
\fBallow_\:routes\fR
T}	T{
list of CIDR
T}	T{
none
T}	T{
The destinations options 121 and 249 may carry,\: for an estate that uses them. Containment,\: not equality: \fB10.\:0.\:0.\:0/\:8\fR allows a route to \fB10.\:1.\:0.\:0/\:16\fR and \fBnot\fR a default route. A route outside the list is refused and the refusal names the route
T}
T{
\fBboot_\:files\fR
T}	T{
list of pattern
T}	T{
any
T}	T{
Shell patterns the boot filename may match,\: checked in both places it lives (option 67 and the \fBfile\fR field)
T}
T{
\fBmin_\:lease_\:time\fR,\: \fBmax_\:lease_\:time\fR
T}	T{
duration
T}	T{
\fB0\fR
T}	T{
Bound the lease a server may hand out. A lease past the bound is \fBshortened\fR,\: not refused,\: so the client still boots. A very long lease is an address pool exhausted by every device that ever visited
T}
T{
\fBrequire_\:client_\:id_\:match\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
Refuse a message whose option 61 names a different hardware address from the header. Only the Ethernet form is compared,\: because RFC 2132 allows any opaque value;\: a real signal,\: and there are real clients that get it wrong,\: so it is a decision rather than a default
T}
T{
\fBrefuse_\:hidden_\:options\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBtrue\fR
T}	T{
Refuse a message that carried an option in the \fBsname\fR or \fBfile\fR field (option 52) or split across instances (RFC 3396). Both are legal,\: almost nothing sends them,\: and both make one message say different things to different parsers. \fBNot shadowable\fR
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:hops\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB4\fR
T}	T{
The hops field,\: which counts the relay agents a message has crossed. RFC 2131 makes 16 the outer limit
T}
T{
\fBrelay_\:address\fR
T}	T{
IPv4
T}	T{
required in reverse mode
T}	T{
What this relay puts in \fBgiaddr\fR,\: which tells the server which segment to allocate from. A relay agent that left it empty would be asking the server to answer a broadcast it never saw. An existing \fBgiaddr\fR from a downstream agent is \fBnot\fR overwritten,\: because that is where the reply has to go back to
T}
T{
\fBcircuit_\:id\fR,\: \fBremote_\:id\fR
T}	T{
string
T}	T{

T}	T{
The two suboptions of RFC 3046\(aqs option 82. Empty leaves the option out
T}
T{
\fBon_\:client_\:agent_\:option\fR
T}	T{
\fBstrip\fR,\: \fBdeny\fR
T}	T{
\fBstrip\fR
T}	T{
What to do when a client sends option 82. \fBstrip\fR is what RFC 3046 \[u00A7]2.1 requires
T}
T{
\fBrules\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{

T}	T{
Per\-message rules,\: first match wins;\: see below
T}
T{
\fBdefault_\:action\fR
T}	T{
\fBallow\fR,\: \fBdeny\fR
T}	T{
\fBallow\fR
T}	T{
Unlike the other relay kinds here the default is \fBallow\fR. DHCP is infrastructure: a listener that refused every request until somebody wrote a rule would stop an estate booting,\: and the protections in this kind are the answer policy and the server list,\: which are on by default and do not depend on a rule existing
T}
T{
\fBdeny_\:response\fR
T}	T{
\fBdrop\fR,\: \fBnak\fR
T}	T{
\fBdrop\fR
T}	T{
\fBdrop\fR leaves the client retrying,\: which is what it does when nothing answers. \fBnak\fR sends a DHCPNAK,\: which makes a client stop and start over \-\- honest,\: and also a way to stop a client dead,\: so not the default. Only a REQUEST is NAKed: DHCPNAK is defined as the answer to a request for a particular address
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:pending\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB256\fR
T}	T{
Requests outstanding towards servers. A full table refuses the new request rather than forgetting an old one,\: because forgetting is what would make a reply undeliverable to the client that actually asked
T}
T{
\fBrequest_\:timeout\fR
T}	T{
duration
T}	T{
\fB10s\fR
T}	T{
How long a server has to answer before its answer is too late to pair
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:message_\:bytes\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB1500\fR
T}	T{
One message. A message past this is refused unread
T}
T{
\fBrate_\:limit\fR,\: \fBrate_\:burst\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB0\fR
T}	T{
Messages per second per \fBhardware address\fR
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:clients\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB8192\fR
T}	T{
Distinct hardware addresses tracked at once
T}
T{
\fBlog_\:messages\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
An access line per message
T}
T{
\fBlog_\:leases\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBtrue\fR
T}	T{
A line for every address handed out: which hardware address,\: which vendor prefix,\: which address,\: for how long,\: from which server,\: what it was told and what it asked for. The record an estate is asked for,\: and the beginning of an asset inventory
T}
T{
\fBalert_\:on_\:deny\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBtrue\fR
T}	T{
A security event per refusal
T}
.TE
.SS "server.listeners[].dhcp.rules[]"
.PP
.TS
