.TE
.PP
\fBSaying what an option may contain is how a rule allows it.\fR This is the one turn in the kind worth reading twice, and it is the same one the LDAP attribute policy makes. The built\-in \fBdeny_options\fR list strips the boot options from every reply \-\- which is right for a network with no PXE clients and wrong for the one segment that has them. A rule with \fBallow_boot_servers\fR or \fBboot_files\fR is a rule about machines that boot from the network, so those options are \fInot\fR stripped from its traffic: they are checked against the list instead. The same holds for \fBallow_routes\fR and the two route options. Without that, "the build segment may be told a boot server and nothing else may" could not be written at all.
.PP
\fBThe built\-in \fBdeny_options\fR list\fR is the options that carry a machine\(aqs configuration rather than a value it displays:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
121 classless_static_route   249 ms_classless_static_route   33 static_route
252 wpad_url                 66 tftp_server                  67 boot_file
43 vendor_specific
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
Naming \fBdeny_options\fR replaces it rather than adding to it, and validation warns if the replacement drops the route option or WPAD. A rule\(aqs own \fBdeny_options\fR adds to whichever list is in force.
.PP
\fBWhat is checked before the rules, and cannot be shadowed.\fR A reply from an address that is not in \fBallow_servers\fR; a message the relay could not parse; a message past \fBmax_message_bytes\fR; a message carrying an option in a header field or split across instances; the hop bound; the rate limit; the pending bound; the lease bounds; a message type arriving from the wrong side (a DHCPOFFER from the client side, or a DHCPDISCOVER from a server); and a malformed option value, because a client would read it somehow and "somehow" is where two readings differ.
.PP
\fBA reply is broadcast only when there is nowhere to unicast to.\fR RFC 2131 \[u00A7]4.1 says to honour the broadcast flag, and a client that sent from \fB0.0.0.0\fR has no address to unicast to whatever its flags say. A request relayed from another agent, or a renewal from a client that already has an address, is answered where it came from. Broadcasting needs a route for \fB255.255.255.255\fR on the listener\(aqs own interface: on Linux that is usually \fBip route add 255.255.255.255/32 dev <iface>\fR, and it is a deployment matter rather than something the proxy can arrange.
.PP
\fBA listener takes no \fBtls\fR section and binds no TCP port.\fR DHCP has no stream transport and no transport security of any kind, so a listener carrying a certificate would be promising something the protocol cannot do; validation refuses it.
.PP
\fBDHCPv6 (RFC 8415) is not implemented\fR, and this kind does not pretend otherwise. It is a different packet format with different message types, a different relay mechanism and its own options; reading it as if it were DHCPv4 would be worse than not reading it. A segment that runs both needs its v6 relaying done elsewhere, and \fBdocs/RFC.md\fR says so in the table.
.PP
Counters: \fBdhcp_messages\fR, \fBdhcp_discovers\fR, \fBdhcp_requests\fR, \fBdhcp_replies\fR, \fBdhcp_leases\fR, \fBdhcp_releases\fR, \fBdhcp_denied\fR, \fBdhcp_would_deny\fR, \fBdhcp_rogue\fR, \fBdhcp_stripped\fR, \fBdhcp_malformed\fR, \fBdhcp_rejected\fR, \fBdhcp_rate_limited\fR, \fBdhcp_timed_out\fR, \fBdhcp_upstream_failed\fR, \fBdhcp_unsolicited\fR, \fBdhcp_pending\fR, \fBdhcp_clients\fR. \fB\fBdhcp_rogue\fR is the one to alert on\fR: it counts the replies from an address this listener does not admit as a server, which is somebody answering on the segment. \fBdhcp_stripped\fR is the one to read next \-\- a route, a proxy or a boot file removed from a reply. Refusals are \fBdhcp_denied\fR for the ban triggers, and the fine\-grained reason is in the refusal counters: \fBclient_not_allowed\fR, \fBrogue_server\fR, \fBmalformed\fR, \fBmalformed_reply\fR, \fBmalformed_option\fR, \fBmessage_too_large\fR, \fBreply_too_large\fR, \fBreply_from_client_side\fR, \fBwrong_direction\fR, \fBunsolicited_reply\fR, \fBhidden_options\fR, \fBclient_id_mismatch\fR, \fBmessage_type\fR, \fBtoo_many_hops\fR, \fBtoo_many_pending\fR, \fBrate_limited\fR, \fBclient_agent_option\fR, \fBoption_stripped\fR, \fBoption_denied\fR, \fBboot_server_not_allowed\fR, \fBboot_file_not_allowed\fR, \fBrule\fR, \fBdefault_deny\fR, \fBunencodable\fR, \fBunencodable_reply\fR.
.SS "server.listeners[].tftp (kind: tftp)"
.PP
TFTP is the protocol under provisioning. A switch pulls its firmware over it, a machine with no operating system yet pulls a boot image, a telephone pulls its configuration, and an engineer pushes a running configuration off a router with it. It has \fBno authentication of any kind\fR: no user, no password, no token, no transport security, and no extension that adds one. A request is a filename and a mode, and a server that receives one answers it.
.PP
The clients are switches, telephones and boot ROMs, so none of that can be fixed where it lives. The relay is the only place a policy can be, and there are exactly four things such a policy can be about.
.PP
\fBThe filename is read as a path and refused by shape.\fR There is no identity, so the decision is the address it came from and the path it asked for \-\- and a path is where this protocol has been exploited for forty years. A deny list of \fIstrings\fR is a list of the spellings somebody thought of: it stops \fB../../etc/shadow\fR and not \fB..\e..\eetc\eshadow\fR, stops that one and not \fB/etc/shadow\fR, stops that one and not \fBsecret.txt.\fR, which Windows opens as \fBsecret.txt\fR. So the name is classified and the \fBclass\fR is refused:
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.0i) lbx
lw(1.0i) lx.
T{
Class
T}	T{
What it is
T}
T{
\fBtraversal\fR
T}	T{
a \fB.\:.\fR element anywhere,\: over either separator. Any of them,\: not only the ones that escape: \fBfirmware/\:.\:.\:/\:firmware/\:x\fR resolves inside the directory on a server that normalises the name and somewhere else on one that walks it a symbolic link at a time
T}
T{
\fBabsolute\fR
T}	T{
rooted at the top of the file system,\: by either separator
T}
T{
\fBdrive\fR
T}	T{
a Windows drive letter (\fBc:\:\ex\fR) or a UNC prefix (\fB\e\ehost\eshare\ex\fR),\: the second of which is a request the server makes to a third machine
T}
T{
\fBbackslash\fR
T}	T{
a backslash elsewhere in the name,\: which is a separator on a server running on Windows and an ordinary character on one that is not
T}
T{
\fBtrailing\fR
T}	T{
an element ending in a space or a dot,\: both of which Windows strips when it opens the file
T}
T{
\fBnon_\:ascii\fR
T}	T{
a byte above 0x7f. RFC 1350 says the filename is netascii,\: so this is outside the standard \-\- but devices that send UTF\-8 names exist
T}
T{
\fBnul\fR,\: \fBcontrol\fR,\: \fBempty\fR
T}	T{
a name this relay and the server would read differently. \fBThese three can never be allowed\fR,\: and they are refused in \fBpolicy:\: {mode:\: shadow}\fR too: a decision about a name the server will not see is not a decision
T}
.TE
.PP
\fBallow_path_classes\fR names the ones this listener accepts besides an ordinary relative path; a rule\(aqs own list widens it for that rule\(aqs traffic only, which is how one legacy server that really does serve absolute paths is written down without opening the class for everything.
.PP
\fBA write is a separate decision from a read, and the default is no.\fR A write is a device putting a file onto the server, which is how a configuration leaves an estate and how firmware arrives in it. \fBoperations\fR defaults to \fB[read]\fR.
.PP
\fBThe amplification is bounded by rewriting rather than by refusing.\fR A twenty\-octet read request yields a whole file to whatever address the datagram claimed to come from, and RFC 7440\(aqs \fBwindowsize\fR multiplies it: a window of sixty\-four is sixty\-four data packets per acknowledgement. A request asking for more than \fBmax_window_size\fR or \fBmax_block_size\fR is \fBrewritten to the bound and forwarded\fR, because a switch whose TFTP client nobody can reconfigure is the normal case and a bound that only refuses is a bound somebody turns off. Two things cannot be lowered and are refused instead: a \fBtsize\fR on a write declaring more than \fBmax_transfer_bytes\fR, because the client has said in advance how much it intends to send; and a server that acknowledges a \fIlarger\fR block or window than it was offered, because the two ends would then disagree about how much is coming.
.PP
\fBA transfer speaks to exactly two addresses.\fR TFTP moves to an ephemeral port pair after the first packet: the server answers from a new port, and the rest of the transfer runs between that port and the client\(aqs. So each transfer here gets a socket of its own, and only the client\(aqs transfer identifier and the first port the server answered from may use it. A datagram from anywhere else is dropped and counted rather than answered \-\- answering is how a relay becomes a reflector \-\- and on a protocol with no integrity protection that third address is the whole attack, because a packet injected into a firmware transfer \fIis\fR firmware.
.PP
.TS
allbox;
