T}
.TE
.PP
\fBWhat is checked before the rules, and cannot be shadowed.\fR A client outside the address list; the rate limit; a packet the relay could not parse; a packet on the request port whose opcode is not a request, because RFC 1350 gives that port one job; a filename in the \fBnul\fR, \fBcontrol\fR or \fBempty\fR class; \fBmax_filename_bytes\fR and \fBmax_depth\fR; \fBmax_block_size\fR, \fBmax_window_size\fR and \fBmax_transfer_bytes\fR; a data packet larger than the block size the transfer negotiated; and a datagram from an address that has no part in the transfer.
.PP
\fBA listener takes no \fBtls\fR section.\fR The protocol has no transport security and no extension that adds one, so a listener carrying a certificate would be promising something it cannot do; validation refuses it. There is no TCP port either: \fBkind: tftp\fR binds UDP and nothing else.
.PP
\fBWhat this relay does not do is look inside a transfer.\fR What is in a firmware image is the estate\(aqs business; that the image is one the policy allows, of a size the policy allows, to a path the policy allows, is the relay\(aqs. For content inspection of a file moving through an estate, the ICAP and YARA paths on \fBsftp\fR and \fBftp\fR are where that lives \-\- and both of those protocols have a user.
.PP
Counters: \fBtftp_requests\fR, \fBtftp_transfers\fR, \fBtftp_transfers_open\fR, \fBtftp_reads\fR, \fBtftp_writes\fR, \fBtftp_bytes_in\fR, \fBtftp_bytes_out\fR, \fBtftp_denied\fR, \fBtftp_would_deny\fR, \fBtftp_path_refused\fR, \fBtftp_lowered\fR, \fBtftp_oversize\fR, \fBtftp_malformed\fR, \fBtftp_rejected\fR, \fBtftp_rate_limited\fR, \fBtftp_timed_out\fR, \fBtftp_upstream_failed\fR, \fBtftp_unsolicited\fR. \fBtftp_lowered\fR is the one to watch first: it counts the requests whose block size or window this relay rewrote, which says the amplification bound is working without anything being refused. Refusals are \fBtftp_denied\fR for the ban triggers, and the fine\-grained reason is in the refusal counters: \fBclient_not_allowed\fR, \fBrate_limited\fR, \fBmalformed\fR, \fBmalformed_response\fR, \fBnot_a_request\fR, \fBnot_in_transfer\fR, \fBwrong_source\fR, \fBwrong_direction\fR, \fBpacket_too_large\fR, \fBblock_too_large\fR, \fBpath_traversal\fR, \fBpath_absolute\fR, \fBpath_drive\fR, \fBpath_backslash\fR, \fBpath_trailing\fR, \fBpath_non_ascii\fR, \fBpath_control\fR, \fBpath_nul\fR, \fBpath_empty\fR, \fBpath_too_deep\fR, \fBfilename_too_long\fR, \fBdirectory_denied\fR, \fBdirectory_not_allowed\fR, \fBfilename_denied\fR, \fBfilename_not_allowed\fR, \fBoperation_not_allowed\fR, \fBmode_not_allowed\fR, \fBrule\fR, \fBdefault_deny\fR, \fBblock_size_invalid\fR, \fBblock_size_malformed\fR, \fBwindow_size_invalid\fR, \fBwindow_size_malformed\fR, \fBtimeout_malformed\fR, \fBtransfer_size_malformed\fR, \fBtransfer_too_large\fR, \fBoack_malformed\fR, \fBoack_block_too_large\fR, \fBoack_window_too_large\fR, \fBrequest_unencodable\fR, \fBtoo_many_transfers\fR, \fBtoo_many_per_client\fR, \fBshutting_down\fR, \fBnot_udp\fR.
.SS "server.listeners[].ntp (kind: ntp)"
.PP
A \fBkind: ntp\fR listener is an NTP and NTS security gateway: it reads every packet, decides about it in the protocol\(aqs own terms, and compares the servers behind it with each other.
.PP
Three jobs live on this port and only one of them is "forward a packet", so they are kept apart deliberately:
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBforwarding\fR time packets between clients and servers, which is what this listener does;
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBauthenticating\fR them, which belongs to whoever holds the key \[u2014] symmetric keys this listener can check, and NTS it deliberately cannot;
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBkeeping an accurate clock\fR, which is the local time daemon\(aqs job and not this relay\(aqs. A relay that tried to be a time source would be a time source nobody calibrated.
.RE
.PP
.PP
What a relay can do that a client cannot is \fBcompare\fR. It sees every server the estate has, measures each the same way, and can refuse to pass on an answer from a server whose time disagrees with its peers or whose own dispersion says not to trust it. A server that is reachable, synchronised, authenticated and \fIwrong\fR is the case every other check passes.
.PP
It works in both directions. \fBmode: reverse\fR (the default) fronts the estate\(aqs own time servers: the devices point at this listener and it forwards to them. \fBmode: forward\fR is the controlled egress towards servers somewhere else, where \fBallow_servers\fR bounds the destinations.
.PP
Datagram only: no TCP port is bound, so nothing can connect to one and hang. NTS key establishment is TCP and is a listener of its own (\fBkind: ntske\fR, below).
.PP
.TS
