T}	T{
What happens when no server is usable: \fBpass\fR (keep forwarding and keep saying so) or \fBrefuse\fR. A blanket fail\-closed stops the estate\(aqs clocks,\: which is itself an outage,\: so \fBrefuse\fR warns
T}
.TE
.PP
The monitor keeps four states apart, because the operator\(aqs next action differs: \fBunreachable\fR (no answer), \fBunsynchronised\fR (it answers and says not to use its time), \fBsuspect\fR (it answers, claims to be fine and disagrees with its peers) and \fBhealthy\fR. With three or more sources the median is the estate\(aqs opinion and the outlier is named; with two that disagree neither can be called wrong, so both are marked and the event says exactly that.
.PP
\fB\fBholdover\fR\fR bounds how long a server whose time cannot be verified is still used: \fBmax_duration\fR (0 disables it). Past it the listener says the holdover has expired, and the estate\(aqs \fBon_all_suspect\fR decides whether that stops the answers.
.PP
\fB\fBchange_detection\fR\fR asks a different question from every bound in \fBquality\fR. Those ask whether one answer is good enough; this asks whether the server is still the same server, answering the same way \[u2014] which is the question the interesting attack leaves open, because it passes every static bound. A source that was a GPS clock at stratum 1 and now answers as something else at stratum 4 is inside \fBmax_stratum: 8\fR. An offset that steps by fourteen seconds between two polls is inside \fBmax_offset: 30s\fR. A dispersion that grows from a millisecond to a second is inside \fBmax_root_dispersion: 2s\fR. A server that stops carrying NTS is carrying valid NTP.
.PP
.TS
