Six detections, each with a counter and a security event of its own: \fBntp_source_changed\fR (the reference identifier), \fBntp_stratum_jumped\fR, \fBntp_offset_stepped\fR, \fBntp_dispersion_grew\fR, \fBntp_nts_lost\fR and \fBntp_leap_announced\fR. They are fed by both the monitor\(aqs own probes and the exchanges the relay forwards, so an estate whose devices poll once an hour still notices within a probe interval. The baseline moves to what was measured whether or not the change was reported, so one change is one alert rather than one per poll for ever; a kiss\-o\(aq\-death and an unsynchronised answer never become the baseline, since neither carries a measurement of the server\(aqs time.
.PP
\fB\fBkod\fR\fR is the kiss\-o\(aq\-death: a stratum\-0 answer whose four reference identifier octets are a code. It is the protocol\(aqs own way of saying "not now", and a client that gets one backs off.
.PP
