.PP
\fB\fBlearn\fR\fR records what actually asks this listener for the time \[u2014] the clients, the versions, the modes, whether any of it is authenticated, and the poll intervals it really uses \[u2014] and writes it out as the three lists a policy is made of, ready to paste. \fB{enabled, file, interval (10s..24h, default 5m), max_subjects (default 8192), enforce}\fR. \fBenforce\fR is false by default and validation warns while it is off; a learning run also does not apply \fBallow_clients\fR, because a run written to discover the clients cannot be stopped from seeing them by the list it is discovering.
.PP
\fB\fBtrace\fR\fR writes one JSON object per packet: \fB{file, max_bytes (1MiB..64GiB, default 100MiB), requests, responses}\fR. At the bound it writes one line saying it stopped rather than filling the disk.
.SS "Architectural decisions"
.PP
\fBPackets are forwarded as the bytes that arrived.\fR Never re\-encoded. An NTS\-protected packet re\-encoded is a packet the client will reject; an authenticated one re\-encoded is worse. So the client\(aqs own transmit timestamp reaches the server and the server\(aqs own answer reaches the client, which is what lets the client verify the exchange itself \[u2014] the relay is in the path, not in the middle of the cryptography.
.PP
\fBEvery timeout, expiry and rate limit is on the monotonic clock.\fR This is a relay for the protocol that changes the wall clock. Wall\-clock arithmetic here would be a timeout that fires when the time is set: every association expiring at once when the clock jumps forward, and none of them ever when it jumps back.
.PP
\fBThe association table and the outstanding\-request table are separate.\fR An association is a client address and port, and its server is chosen once and kept: a client that asked a different server every poll would see a different offset every poll, and the jitter it measured would be the relay\(aqs doing. Nothing here is round robin per packet, and nothing hedges. The outstanding table is keyed by the backend and the client\(aqs transmit timestamp, because a client may have several requests in flight, an answer may arrive after its association moved, and a server may answer something nobody asked \[u2014] and each of those is a counter rather than a confusion.
.PP
\fBAn answer is tied to its question by the origin timestamp.\fR That is the only thing in NTP that ties them, so an answer whose origin matches no outstanding request is dropped and counted (\fBntp_unsolicited\fR), even though it came from the right address on a connected socket. Interleaved mode is the documented exception: there the server echoes its own previous transmit timestamp, which is a second table and a switch of its own.
.PP
\fBVersion and mode dispatch happen before the parser.\fR A mode 6 or mode 7 packet is not a time packet with an odd number in it \[u2014] it is the control protocol and the vendor\-private protocol, whose headers are not this one, and whose \fBmonlist\fR request is the amplifier this port is famous for. A version 5 packet is a different layout again. All three are refused from the first octet, before any field of the body is read; \fBallow_version5\fR forwards version 5 as opaque bytes rather than parsing it.
.PP
\fBThe relay adds asymmetry, and that is a number.\fR A packet through a relay takes one path out and another back, and the offset a client computes is wrong by half the difference between them: \fB(forward \[u2212] reverse delay) / 2\fR. Nothing can remove it, so the data path is kept short \[u2014] the policy is compiled, the logging and the learning are off the forwarding path, the queues are bounded, and there is no deep inspection of anything, because there is nothing in a time packet to inspect deeply. An estate that needs better than the asymmetry allows puts a time server near its consumers rather than a relay.
.PP
Counters: \fBntp_requests\fR, \fBntp_forwarded\fR, \fBntp_responses\fR, \fBntp_answered\fR, \fBntp_denied\fR, \fBntp_would_deny\fR, \fBntp_dropped\fR, \fBntp_malformed\fR, \fBntp_unsolicited\fR, \fBntp_rate_limited\fR, \fBntp_kiss_sent\fR, \fBntp_timed_out\fR, \fBntp_associations\fR, \fBntp_associations_open\fR, \fBntp_upstream_failed\fR, \fBntp_upstream_unavailable\fR, \fBntp_send_failed\fR, \fBntp_interleaved\fR, \fBntp_nts_forwarded\fR, \fBntp_version5\fR, \fBntp_probes\fR, \fBntp_probe_failed\fR, \fBntp_disagreements\fR, \fBntp_source_healthy\fR, \fBntp_source_unhealthy\fR, \fBntp_holdover_expired\fR, \fBntp_source_changed\fR, \fBntp_stratum_jumped\fR, \fBntp_offset_stepped\fR, \fBntp_dispersion_grew\fR, \fBntp_nts_lost\fR, \fBntp_leap_announced\fR, \fBntp_leap_unexpected\fR. Refusals are \fBntp_denied\fR for the ban triggers, and the fine\-grained reason is in the refusal counters: \fBbanned\fR, \fBclient_not_allowed\fR, \fBrate_limit\fR, \fBcontrol_mode\fR, \fBprivate_mode\fR, \fBversion5\fR, \fBversion\fR, \fBversion_not_allowed\fR, \fBmode_not_allowed\fR, \fBnot_a_peer\fR, \fBbroadcast_not_allowed\fR, \fBmalformed\fR, \fBpacket_too_large\fR, \fBtoo_many_extensions\fR, \fBunknown_extension\fR, \fBambiguous_mac\fR, \fBnts_required\fR, \fBauth_required\fR, \fBauth_failed\fR, \fBmax_associations\fR, \fBoutstanding_full\fR, \fBno_server\fR, \fBserver_not_allowed\fR, \fBmalformed_response\fR, \fBunsolicited\fR, \fBresponse_mode\fR, \fBkiss_of_death\fR, \fBunsynchronised\fR, \fBunsynchronised_stratum\fR, \fBstratum_too_high\fR, \fBstratum_not_allowed\fR, \fBroot_delay\fR, \fBroot_dispersion\fR, \fBroot_distance\fR, \fBdelay\fR, \fBoffset\fR, \fBbogus_timestamps\fR, \fBbogus_refid\fR, \fBrefid_not_allowed\fR, \fBleap_announced\fR, \fBleap_unexpected\fR, \fBsource_changed\fR, \fBnts_stripped\fR, \fBauth_stripped\fR.
.SS "server.listeners[].ntske (kind: ntske)"
.PP
A \fBkind: ntske\fR listener is NTS key establishment (RFC 8915) on TCP 4460: TLS with the ALPN \fBntske/1\fR, relayed to the key establishment servers whose keys it is.
.PP
It is a separate listener from \fBkind: ntp\fR because it is a separate port, a separate transport and a separate security property. A deployment that wants NTS runs both, and having to write both down is the point: an estate with a time listener and no key establishment listener has clients that cannot get cookies, and that is better seen in the configuration than found in the logs.
.PP
It \fBrelays\fR rather than terminates, for the reasons under \fBnts\fR above. What it does is the part a relay can do honestly: read the one thing a TLS handshake shows in the clear \[u2014] the server name and the application protocol the client offers \[u2014] refuse a connection that is not an NTS client, bound the handshakes in flight, and hand the rest to the server.
.PP
.TS
