T}
.TE
.PP
A zero is no bound. Two checks apply whatever \fBbounds\fR says: a rectangle must lie inside the framebuffer it belongs to (a viewer writes a rectangle\(aqs pixels at the offset the rectangle gives, and one reaching past the end is a write past the end of the buffer the viewer made for it), and a pixel format must be 8, 16 or 32 bits, because every length in the picture is measured in whole pixels.
.PP
\fBFraming decides what a client may ask for.\fR A gateway can only frame an encoding whose payload length it can compute, so the encodings it cannot are removed from the client\(aqs \fBSetEncodings\fR list and the desktop never uses one. What survives: \fBraw\fR, \fBcopyrect\fR, \fBrre\fR, \fBcorre\fR, \fBhextile\fR, \fBzlib\fR, \fBzrle\fR, the cursor and desktop\-size pseudo\-encodings, the compression and quality hints, \fBfence\fR, continuous updates and the extended clipboard. What is removed: \fB\fBtight\fR\fR and \fBtrle\fR, whose lengths depend on a filter, a palette size and a pixel width that is not the pixel format\(aqs; \fBcursor\-with\-alpha\fR; and anything unregistered. A client asks for every encoding it has, so a filtered list still leaves a session that draws \-\- \fBzrle\fR is the usual survivor, and \fBraw\fR is added if nothing else is left.
.PP
A listener that must have \fBtight\fR sets \fBpixel_stream: opaque\fR. The desktop\(aqs bytes are then forwarded unread: \fBmax_framebuffer_pixels\fR and \fBmax_cut_text\fR still apply, and the three bounds that need the stream read do not. Validation warns, because that is the trade.
.PP
The \fBclient\(aqs\fR messages are framed either way. A message is only as long as its type says, so framing is what lets a gateway drop one and forward the next \-\- which is how \fBview_only\fR and \fBclipboard\fR work \-\- and a message type whose length the gateway does not know cannot be forwarded at all without losing the stream after it. \fBThat is why a file transfer cannot cross this gateway\fR: TightVNC\(aqs and UltraVNC\(aqs transfers are client messages 252 and 255, whose lengths are the vendors\(aq own, so a session that sends one ends. It is a consequence of framing rather than a rule, which is the strongest kind.
.PP
One restriction comes with framing: a \fBSetPixelFormat\fR is allowed before the first framebuffer update request and refused after it. RFB has no message that says "the next rectangle is in the new format", so a gateway that kept framing past a mid\-stream change would be guessing where the rectangles are. Viewers that re\-negotiate colour depth automatically (TigerVNC\(aqs automatic mode) need a fixed depth configured, or \fBpixel_stream: opaque\fR.
.PP
Every refusal on this path is counted under \fBxproxy_refusals_total{kind="vnc"}\fR with a reason of its own (\fBframebuffer_too_large\fR, \fBdecode_ratio\fR, \fBtoo_many_rectangles\fR, \fBencoded_rectangle_too_large\fR, \fBrectangle_outside_framebuffer\fR, \fBunframable\fR, \fBcut_text_too_large\fR, \fBresize_refused\fR, \fBpixel_format_changed\fR) and logged as a \fBvnc_denied\fR security event with the numbers that caused it.
.SS "VNC over TLS, and VNC over SSH"
.PP
Three ways to stop the session crossing a network in clear. The two that encrypt the client\(aqs leg are alternatives; the third is the target\(aqs leg and composes with either.
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBVeNCrypt\fR, negotiated inside RFB (\fBtls_mode: negotiated\fR, the default). The socket carries RFB from the first byte, and the certificate in the listener\(aqs \fBtls\fR section is presented inside the handshake. This is the one a modern viewer offers by itself, and the \fBx509\-*\fR subtypes are the ones with a certificate to check. The security type \fBtls\fR (18) works the same way, with no certificate checked.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBA socket that is TLS from the first byte\fR (\fBtls_mode: wrap\fR). The client connects with TLS and speaks RFB inside it, which is what a viewer reaching a \fBstunnel\fR\-wrapped port does.
.PP
\fBwrap\fR together with the \fBvencrypt\fR or \fBtls\fR security type is a configuration error rather than two layers: the first byte a client sends is either a TLS record or \fBRFB 003.008\fR, so a port is one or the other. An estate with both kinds of viewer uses one listener for each, which is what the viewers are already pointed at.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fB\fBssh\fR\fR, for the target\(aqs leg. The gateway opens an SSH connection and reaches the VNC server through it, so the RFB never crosses the network in clear even when the server itself speaks only RFB. This is the usual \fBssh \-L\fR arrangement, done once by the gateway rather than by every operator.
.RE
.PP
.PP
.TS
allbox;
