.PP
An operator who prefers to tunnel themselves still can: \fBssh \-L\fR to the \fBkind: ssh\fR bastion and point the viewer at the forwarded port. That needs nothing here. The \fBssh\fR section is for doing it once, centrally, so the recording and the policy still apply.
.SS "The second factor"
.PP
\fBmfa\fR needs a security type whose credential carries a user name, and validation refuses the section without one. The reason is in the protocol: a DES challenge proves knowledge of one shared desktop password and says nothing about who is holding it, so there is nothing to look an enrolment up by. Four things carry a name \[u2014] a VeNCrypt \fBplain\fR subtype, \fBmslogon2\fR, \fBard\fR, and the \fBrsa\-aes\fR types \[u2014] and any of them satisfies it. The name identifies the person and the password field carries their one\-time code; with a plain subtype both are inside the TLS tunnel, which is the arrangement to prefer.
.PP
\fBx509\-plain\fR is added to \fBvencrypt_subtypes\fR automatically when \fBmfa\fR is configured and no plain subtype is listed.
.PP
The code is checked inside the handshake, before the security result is sent: a wrong code is a failed authentication the viewer can show, rather than a session that is told it succeeded and then closes. The desktop is not dialled either way. A viewer that authenticates with a type carrying no name \[u2014] where one is also offered \[u2014] is refused for the same reason: there is nothing to look an enrolment up by.
.SS "The recording"
.PP
The file holds the server\-to\-client RFB stream \[u2014] what was on the screen \[u2014] with the timing of it, in the asciicast v2 container the other recordings use, named \fB*.rfb.cast\fR. Its header carries \fBXPROXY_PROTOCOL: rfb\fR, the framebuffer size and the RFB version, and the first mark names the desktop, both versions and both security types.
.PP
With \fBrecording.input\fR the viewer\(aqs own stream is kept as well, as asciicast \fBi\fR events beside the \fBo\fR ones: every key and pointer event, the clipboard writes and the encoding and format negotiations. It is off by default for the same reason a terminal\(aqs keystroke recording is, and a message the policy refused is written as a mark rather than as bytes \[u2014] a replay then shows that the viewer tried and the gateway said no, which is the part an investigation is looking for.
.PP
\fBIt is not a video.\fR The event data is the protocol stream, so replaying it needs a player that speaks RFB rather than a terminal: \fBxproxy\-replay\fR(8), which is shipped with the product and decodes what RFC 6143 specifies. The data is base64 inside the container, because a pixel is any byte at all and JSON cannot hold one that is not valid UTF\-8; the header says so with \fBXPROXY_ENCODING: base64\fR, and the pixel format the desktop announced is in it too, since the handshake that carried it happened before the recording began. Recording the stream is what keeps the cost bounded and loses nothing: a decoder can be written against this file afterwards, and one that decoded at capture time would have to understand every encoding a server might choose and would silently lose whatever it did not.
.PP
Counters: \fBvnc_sessions\fR, \fBvnc_sessions_open\fR, \fBvnc_rejected\fR, \fBvnc_refused\fR, \fBvnc_recorded\fR, \fBvnc_mfa_ok\fR, \fBvnc_mfa_failed\fR. Every session writes one \fBvnc\fR access line with both versions, both security types, the desktop name and size, and how it ended. Refusals are \fBvnc_denied\fR deny events, so bans apply.
.SS "server.listeners[].rdp (kind: rdp)"
.PP
A \fBkind: rdp\fR listener is a Remote Desktop gateway: the proxy terminates the connection sequence of MS\-RDPBCGR on both legs, so it is an RDP server to the client and an RDP client to the desktop.
.PP
\fBWhy this is not a \fBtcp\fR listener.\fR Everything worth deciding about an RDP session is settled in the connection sequence, before a single pixel moves: which security protocol is used, which virtual channels exist, who is connecting and with what credential. A relay that does not sit in that sequence decides none of it and can record none of what follows.
.PP
The channel list is the important part. Every redirection RDP has \[u2014] drives, printers, serial and parallel ports, smart cards, the clipboard, audio \[u2014] rides a virtual channel, and nothing can be used that was not both announced and granted. A gateway that rewrites that list decides what a session is able to do before it does it.
.SS "What is supported"
.PP
\fBProtocol versions.\fR All of them. This gateway does not decode graphics, input, capability sets or any of the session\(aqs own traffic \[u2014] it relays those as they arrive \[u2014] so an RDP 4 client and a Windows Server 2025 desktop work the same way. What it decodes is the connection sequence, the channel list, the device announcement and the credential packet, and those have been stable since the protocol was documented.
.PP
\fBSecurity protocols\fR, which are negotiated at the very start:
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.5i) lbw(1.4i) lbx
lw(1.5i) lw(1.4i) lx.
T{
Protocol
T}	T{
Towards a client
T}	T{
Towards a desktop
T}
T{
TLS (\fBtls\fR,\: \fBPROTOCOL_\:SSL\fR)
T}	T{
Supported,\: the default
T}	T{
Supported,\: the default
T}
T{
Network level authentication (\fBnla\fR,\: \fBPROTOCOL_\:HYBRID\fR)
T}	T{
\fBNot offered,\: and cannot be\fR \[u2014] see below
T}	T{
Supported: CredSSP over NTLMv2
T}
T{
The protocol\(aqs own encryption (\fBrdp\fR,\: \fBPROTOCOL_\:RDP\fR)
T}	T{
Supported,\: and it authenticates nothing \[u2014] see below
T}	T{
Supported,\: and worth what the section below says it is
T}
.TE
.PP
\fBA client that asks for network level authentication is answered with TLS.\fR That is not a gap, it is how the gateway works at all, and it is what every remote desktop gateway does. Network level authentication proves the person\(aqs Windows credential to the server \fIbefore\fR the RDP connection sequence starts, using CredSSP. For a gateway to accept that from a client it would have to verify that credential itself, which means holding every person\(aqs Windows password \[u2014] the one credential a gateway should never hold. Answering with TLS moves the credential into the connection sequence, where the gateway can check a second factor against it and substitute its own. The cost is stated plainly: between the client and the gateway, authentication happens after the connection is established rather than before it, so the gateway itself must be reachable only by the people who should reach it (\fBallow_clients\fR, and a network that agrees).
.PP
\fBNetwork level authentication towards a desktop\fR (\fBupstream_security: nla\fR) is what a current Windows install requires by default. The gateway proves a credential with CredSSP (MS\-CSSP) carrying NTLM version 2 (MS\-NLMP) inside the TLS tunnel, before the connection sequence starts.
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
It needs \fBupstream_user\fR and \fBupstream_password_file\fR, and validation says so. There is no way to pass the person\(aqs own credential through it: the exchange happens before the person has sent anything at all, which is the whole reason network level authentication exists.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
The exchange is \fBbound to the tunnel it runs in\fR. Each end proves it saw the same certificate, from CredSSP version 5 onwards as a hash over a fresh nonce, so tokens relayed into another connection fail. A desktop whose binding does not match gets no credential.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
The gateway insists on \fBextended session security\fR and a key exchange. A server offering neither is refused rather than fallen back to: the credential\(aqs protection rests on those session keys.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
Only the client half is implemented \[u2014] this proves a credential, it never checks one. Checking would mean the gateway holding a password hash for whoever connects, which is the thing it exists to avoid.
.RE
.PP
.PP
The NTLM key derivation is tested against the worked example of MS\-NLMP \[u00A7]4.2.4, and the whole exchange against a stand\-in for the Windows side. It has \fBnot\fR been verified against a real Windows desktop in this repository\(aqs tests.
.PP
\fBThe protocol\(aqs own encryption\fR \[u2014] RC4 under keys derived from two random values, one of them sent under an RSA key the server puts in the connection sequence \[u2014] is where the two directions differ.
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBTowards a desktop\fR (\fBupstream_security: rdp\fR) it works. The desktop\(aqs certificate is walked to its key, this end\(aqs random is sealed under it and sent as the security exchange, the session keys are derived, and from there everything the client sends is signed and encrypted on the way out while everything the desktop sends is decrypted on the way in \[u2014] on both framings, the connection sequence\(aqs data units and the fast path the session itself runs on. The key rolls over every 4096 packets as the protocol requires. Setting it logs a warning at load, because it is a downgrade an operator should have meant.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBTowards a client\fR (\fBsecurity: [rdp]\fR) it works too. The listener draws a 512\-bit RSA key at start \[u2014] the size the protocol carries, not a choice \[u2014] puts it in a proprietary certificate, and signs that certificate with the \fBTerminal Services signing key published in MS\-RDPBCGR \[u00A7]5.3.3.1.1\fR. The strongest method the client offered is chosen, at encryption level \fIclient compatible\fR, so both directions are encrypted. The client\(aqs random arrives sealed under the listener\(aqs key, the session keys follow, and from there the gateway decrypts what the client sends before the policy sees it and encrypts what it sends back.
.PP
\fBThat signature authenticates nothing.\fR Microsoft published the private key, which is what lets any implementation sign one \[u2014] so a client verifying it has learned that the other end read the specification, and nothing about who it is talking to. There is no server authentication on such a leg, and no way to add any: the protocol has nowhere to put it. A listener offering this is protected by the network it sits on, \fBallow_clients\fR, and nothing else.
.PP
Setting it warns at load. A client that can use TLS should be made to (\fBsecurity: [rdp, tls]\fR offers both and TLS is preferred whenever the client asks for it), and a listener that offers only \fBrdp\fR needs no \fBtls\fR section at all.
.RE
.PP
.PP
Three things follow from the gateway being \fIinside\fR that encryption rather than outside it, and they are the reason to be there: the channel and device policy still applies, the second factor is still checked, and the recording holds the session rather than ciphertext.
.PP
The two legs are independent. An old client and an old desktop each get their own key exchange, their own method and their own key schedule; nothing is forwarded from one to the other. A client on the protocol\(aqs own encryption can reach a desktop on TLS or on network level authentication, and the other way round.
.PP
Session traffic towards a legacy client \fBwaits\fR for that client\(aqs key exchange rather than going out in the clear: a client that agreed to encryption cannot read an unencrypted packet, so sending one early would end the session rather than degrade it. The wait is bounded by \fBhandshake_timeout\fR.
.PP
What the encryption itself is worth is nothing, against anyone on the path: MD5 and SHA\-1 derivation, RC4 \[u2014] 40, 56 or 128 bit, whichever the desktop asks for \[u2014] and a certificate a client has no way to check, because the protocol never had anywhere to check it against. This gateway reads the key out of that certificate without checking it, which is the same position every client is in. FIPS mode (3DES with a different derivation and a different packet layout) is not implemented: a desktop that insists on it is refused rather than downgraded.
.PP
So it is here for equipment that speaks nothing else \[u2014] an appliance, an embedded console, a Windows install too old to offer TLS. Such a desktop reached through this gateway is better off than reached directly, because of the three things above. It is not better off because the connection is protected.
.PP
A desktop that asks for no encryption at all (\fBENCRYPTION_LEVEL_NONE\fR) is served, and the gateway writes a warning naming it, because a session nobody encrypts looks exactly like one everybody does.
.PP
\fBWhat the gateway does not decode\fR: the graphics, input, clipboard contents, audio, licensing and capability exchange. Those are relayed byte for byte. A recording is therefore the protocol stream, not a video \[u2014] see below.
.SS "The channel policy"
.PP
\fBchannels.allow\fR names the static virtual channels a session may have, and \fBthe default is none\fR: a session that can see the desktop and drive it, and nothing else. The usual names are \fBrdpdr\fR (device redirection), \fBcliprdr\fR (clipboard, including file copy), \fBrdpsnd\fR (audio out), \fBaudin\fR (microphone), \fBdrdynvc\fR (dynamic channels) and \fBrail\fR (seamless applications).
.PP
A refused channel is \fBnot removed from the list\fR, and the reason is worth knowing: the desktop answers with one identifier per channel the client asked for, in the order it asked, and a client that gets back a different number of identifiers does not recover. So the gateway replaces the \fIname\fR of a refused channel with one nothing speaks \[u2014] the name field is a fixed eight bytes, so the lengths do not move. The desktop registers a channel no software has a handler for, the identifiers still line up, and the gateway drops whatever the client sends on it. The desktop never registers the real channel, which is the property that matters.
.PP
\fBdrdynvc\fR is warned about: dynamic channels carry more redirection inside them, and what rides one is decided by the two ends rather than by this list.
.SS "File transfer and ports"
.PP
Both are device redirection, which rides \fBrdpdr\fR, and both are decided by \fBdevices.allow\fR:
.PP
.TS
