T}
.TE
.PP
A transfer is not an HTTP message, so the file is wrapped in the one a scanner expects: a \fBPUT\fR for an upload, a \fBGET\fR and its response for a download, with the \fBftp://\fR URL of the real file so the scanner\(aqs own log names something findable, the client address in \fBX\-Client\-IP\fR and the login in \fBX\-Authenticated\-User\fR. Directory listings are not sent: they are not files.
.PP
Scanning means holding the file until the service answers, because a verdict that arrives after the bytes have gone is not a control. The service\(aqs own \fBmax_body\fR, \fBbody_limit_action\fR and \fBfail\fR apply exactly as they do to an HTTP body: a file past \fBmax_body\fR is refused, or passed unscanned with a warning in the security log where \fBbody_limit_action: bypass\fR says so; a service that cannot be reached refuses the transfer unless \fBfail: open\fR, which also logs. A blocked transfer is cut and the client gets \fB426\fR naming the reason.
.SS "server.listeners[].ftp.mfa"
.PP
Asks for a second factor after the target accepts the password, on the control channel, before any other command is allowed. The fields are the ones documented under \fBserver.listeners[].ssh.mfa\fR.
.PP
FTP has no prompt of its own, so the code is taken the only two ways the protocol allows:
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fB\fBACCT\fR\fR, which RFC 959 defines for exactly this. The proxy answers the accepted password with \fB332\fR and takes the code as the argument of the \fBACCT\fR that follows. \fBACCT\fR is added to the relayed commands automatically when this section is present.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBAppended to the password\fR, after a comma: \fBPASS secret,123456\fR. The proxy takes the code off and the target sees only the password. This needs nothing of the client at all, which is what most one\-time\-password FTP deployments rely on.
.RE
.PP
.PP
Until the factor is verified the session is not logged in: every command but \fBACCT\fR, \fBQUIT\fR, \fBNOOP\fR, \fBFEAT\fR, \fBHELP\fR, \fBSTAT\fR, \fBSYST\fR and \fBREIN\fR is refused with \fB530\fR. A user with no enrolment is refused where \fBrequire_enrolment\fR is on, because an optional second factor is one an attacker can decline by using an account that never enrolled. Wrong codes count against \fBmax_errors\fR and feed the ban triggers, since a client working through codes is doing what one working through passwords does.
.PP
Validation warns when \fBmfa\fR is set with \fBtls_mode: none\fR: the code then crosses the network in clear beside the password it is meant to back up.
.PP
Counters: \fBftp_sessions\fR, \fBftp_sessions_open\fR, \fBftp_transfers\fR, \fBftp_refused\fR, \fBftp_rejected\fR, \fBftp_auth_failed\fR, \fBftp_scanned\fR, \fBftp_scan_blocked\fR, \fBftp_recorded\fR, \fBftp_mfa_ok\fR, \fBftp_mfa_failed\fR. Every session writes an \fBftp\fR access line and every transfer an \fBftp_transfer\fR line with the command, the path, the octets and whether it was cut; a finished recording writes \fBftp_recording\fR with the file and its size. Refusals are \fBftp_denied\fR for the ban triggers, and a refused factor is \fBftp_mfa_failed\fR.
.SS "server.listeners[].ssh (kind: ssh)"
.PP
A \fBkind: ssh\fR listener is an SSH bastion: the proxy is an SSH server to the client and an SSH client to the target, with its own host key, its own authentication and its own credential onwards.
.PP
The two connections are the point. A jump host that forwards the stream cannot see which channel is a shell and which is a port forward, so the only policy it can hold is "may connect". Here every channel and every request inside the session is a decision: a service account can be given sftp to one directory and nothing else, and a port forward to a database is a rule rather than an assumption.
.PP
It also means the target never sees the client\(aqs key. The client authenticates to the proxy; the proxy authenticates to the target with a credential the client never holds, so a key that leaves the estate is not a key that opens a server in it. \fBupstream_known_hosts\fR is what makes the bastion the one place that can notice a machine in the middle.
.PP
\fBHost certificates.\fR An estate that rebuilds machines signs each new host key with a host CA precisely so that nobody has to edit \fBknown_hosts\fR everywhere, and an \fB@cert\-authority\fR line is how the file says so. The certificate is then checked as OpenSSH checks it: the signature against that authority, that it is a \fBhost\fR certificate rather than a user one, its validity window, and that its principals cover the host being reached (the name without the port, as OpenSSH does). An authority is trusted only for the hosts its own line names.
.PP
\fBRevocation wins.\fR \fB@revoked\fR refuses the key it names whatever else the file says \[u2014] the case the marker exists for is a key that is still listed as trusted somewhere \[u2014] and for a certificate it covers the key inside it and the authority that signed it.
.PP
An ssh listener takes \fBaddress\fR and \fBssh\fR and no \fBtls\fR: SSH carries its own transport security. Bans and the global connection limits apply at accept. Changing the \fBssh\fR section rebinds the listener on reload, and the credentials are read then \[u2014] not per connection, so a key added to \fBauthorized_keys\fR takes effect on reload rather than mid\-session.
.PP
.TS
