.PP
A denied principal never reaches the target: the refusal is at authentication, before a channel or an upstream connection exists.
.PP
A principal\(aqs \fBrecording\fR replaces the listener\(aqs, which is how one entry is recorded and another is not; \fBrecording: {enabled: false}\fR is how a principal is spared where the listener records.
.PP
An entry that brings its own \fBsftp\fR section to a listener that has none also inherits the default that section implies: file transfer helpers are refused for that principal, because \fBscp\fR beside a careful \fBsftp\fR policy is the policy with a door next to it.
.PP
##### What the certificate says, beyond the signature
.PP
A user certificate is not only a signature over a key and a list of principals. It carries the CA\(aqs own restrictions, and a gateway that checks the signature and ignores the rest is a gateway where those restrictions do not exist. All of these are enforced:
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fB\fBsource\-address\fR\fR, the critical option naming the networks the certificate may be used from. It is enforced here because it is the one option whose check needs the client\(aqs address, which is why the Go library deliberately leaves it to the caller. A list this gateway cannot parse refuses the certificate rather than being treated as absent.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fB\fBforce\-command\fR\fR, the critical option fixing what the session runs. The client\(aqs command is \fIreplaced\fR by it, as OpenSSH does \[u2014] a certificate issued to run one thing is issued for a reason \[u2014] and a \fBshell\fR request becomes that command too. Both are written to the security log as \fBssh_force_command\fR, with what was asked and what ran.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBThe extensions are permissions, and their absence is a denial.\fR \fBpermit\-pty\fR, \fBpermit\-port\-forwarding\fR, \fBpermit\-agent\-forwarding\fR, \fBpermit\-X11\-forwarding\fR. A certificate made with \fBssh\-keygen \-O clear \-O permit\-pty\fR grants a terminal and nothing else, whatever this listener\(aqs \fBallow_channels\fR and \fBallow_requests\fR would otherwise allow. A certificate issued the ordinary way carries all five, so nothing changes for one.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBAny other critical option refuses the certificate.\fR A critical option is critical: the CA meant it to be honoured or the credential refused, so an option this gateway does not implement must not be quietly ignored.
.RE
.PP
.PP
Both policies apply and the narrower wins: the CA says what this credential may do, the listener says what anybody may do here. A session authenticated by a plain key is restricted by the listener alone.
.PP
##### \fBallow_commands\fR is patterns, and a shell is not
.PP
\fBallow_commands\fR is a list of RE2 patterns over the command line, and a pattern is a weak thing to hold a shell to: \fB^journalctl .*$\fR matches \fBjournalctl \-u x; rm \-rf /\fR exactly as happily as what it was written for. So the line is read the way a shell would split it \fIfirst\fR, and one carrying an operator \[u2014] \fB;\fR \fB&\fR \fB|\fR \fB<\fR \fB>\fR a backquote, a \fB$(\fR, a brace, a glob, a control character, an unbalanced quote \[u2014] is refused with \fBshell_syntax\fR before any pattern is tried.
.PP
This is deliberately crude and deliberately broad. A bastion does not need to know what a line would do; it needs to know that the line is a command with arguments, which is the only shape a list of patterns can be written against. \fBallow_shell_syntax: true\fR turns the check off for a listener that genuinely needs shell syntax, and warns, because the patterns then have to be written knowing it.
.PP
The strong form of a command policy is \fBforce\-command\fR on the certificate: the CA fixes the command and the client\(aqs own is replaced, so there is no line to pattern\-match at all.
.PP
##### server.listeners[].ssh.command_rules
.PP
A pattern is a weak boundary, and for the commands that move files it is the wrong shape of statement. \fB^scp \-t /srv/incoming$\fR is somebody writing \fI"uploads into that directory, nothing else"\fR, and each of these is past it:
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.0i) lbx
lw(1.0i) lx.
T{
The line
T}	T{
What a pattern misses
T}
T{
\fBscp \-\:f /\:srv/\:incoming\fR
T}	T{
The other direction,\: spelled with the same words
T}
T{
\fBscp \-\:rt /\:srv/\:incoming\fR
T}	T{
Bundled flags,\: so the pattern does not match \[u2014] and recursion is a different permission
T}
T{
\fB/\:usr/\:bin/\:scp \-\:t /\:srv/\:incoming\fR
T}	T{
A path,\: so the pattern does not match
T}
T{
\fBscp  \-\:t  /\:srv/\:incoming\fR
T}	T{
Two spaces
T}
T{
\fBscp \-\:t /\:srv/\:incoming/\:.\:.\:/\:.\:.\:/\:etc/\:ssh\fR
T}	T{
A path inside \fB/\:srv/\:incoming\fR to a pattern,\: \fB/\:etc\fR to the target
T}
T{
\fBLD_\:PRELOAD=\:/\:tmp/\:x.\:so scp \-\:t /\:srv/\:incoming\fR
T}	T{
An environment the \fBallow_\:env\fR policy never sees,\: because it is a shell assignment and not an \fBenv\fR request
T}
.TE
.PP
Tighten the pattern against any one of them and it is still wrong about the next. What the policy means to say is a statement about the command\(aqs \fImeaning\fR, so that is what a rule says: the direction, whether recursion is allowed, whether deletions are, and which paths are in reach. The command line is split the way a shell splits it, the options are read the way the program reads them, and the rule is applied to that.
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.1i) lbw(0.9i) lbw(0.9i) lbx
lw(1.1i) lw(0.9i) lw(0.9i) lx.
T{
Key
T}	T{
Type
T}	T{
Default
T}	T{
Description
T}
T{
\fBcommand\fR
T}	T{
\fBscp\fR,\: \fBrsync\fR,\: \fBsftp_\:server\fR,\: \fBgit\fR
T}	T{
required
T}	T{
The family this rule decides. One rule per family;\: a second is a load error,\: since which of the two was silent would be the policy
T}
T{
\fBdirections\fR
T}	T{
list of \fBupload\fR,\: \fBdownload\fR
T}	T{
required (not for \fBsftp_\:server\fR)
T}	T{
The file movements allowed. \fBupload\fR puts files \fBon\fR the target,\: \fBdownload\fR takes them \fBoff\fR it \[u2014] the movement,\: not the program\(aqs own verb: a git fetch is \fBupload\-\:pack\fR because that name is the server\(aqs,\: and it is a \fBdownload\fR here
T}
T{
\fBpaths\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
\fB[]\fR (every path)
T}	T{
The paths in reach,\: as the \fBsftp\fR policy\(aqs patterns (\fB/\:srv/\:incoming/\:**\fR). Absolute;\: a relative pattern is a load error,\: because the path it is matched against is resolved from the root
T}
T{
\fBdeny_\:paths\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
\fB[]\fR
T}	T{
Refused whatever \fBpaths\fR says
T}
T{
\fBrecursive\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
Allow \fBscp \-\:r\fR. Only scp takes it
T}
T{
\fBdelete\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
Allow the rsync options that remove files at the far end: \fB\-\:\-\:delete\fR and its family,\: \fB\-\:\-\:remove\-\:source\-\:files\fR,\: \fB\-\:\-\:force\fR. Only rsync has them
T}
T{
\fBenforce_\:sftp_\:policy\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
Relay an approved exec of the sftp server binary through the \fBsftp\fR policy. Required for a \fBsftp_\:server\fR rule and refused for the others
T}
.TE
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
sftp: {read_only: true, allow_paths: ["/srv/data/**"]}
command_rules:
  # The deployment robot puts artefacts in one directory and takes
  # nothing out. No recursion: a file is a file.
  \- command: scp
    directions: [upload]
    paths: ["/srv/incoming/**"]
    deny_paths: ["/srv/incoming/keys/**"]
  # Backups pull, and may not delete what they pull from.
  \- command: rsync
    directions: [download]
    paths: ["/srv/data/**"]
  # Clones and fetches, no pushes.
  \- command: git
    directions: [download]
    paths: ["/srv/git/**"]
  # And the sftp server binary run as a command is the sftp subsystem
  # under another name, so it is allowed only inspected.
  \- command: sftp_server
    enforce_sftp_policy: true
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBWith any rule present, a family named by no rule is refused.\fR A rule for scp must not quietly leave rsync to the patterns, so the families this gateway can read are a positive model once the list exists (\fBcommand_no_rule\fR). Everything else \[u2014] every command with no parser here \[u2014] stays with \fBallow_commands\fR exactly as before.
.PP
\fBA rule allows what \fBallow_file_transfer_commands: false\fR refuses.\fR That switch is "scp and rsync, yes or no"; a rule is "scp, uploads, into this directory", which is the decision an operator wanted to make. So a rule wins over the blanket refusal for the family it names, and over \fBallow_commands\fR too: a command a rule allows is not also pattern matched, because the rule is the narrower statement.
.PP
\fBA line that cannot be read is refused, never guessed at\fR (\fBcommand_syntax\fR): a substitution, an unbalanced quote, an option no version of the program takes. This applies to the families the rules cover even where \fBallow_shell_syntax\fR is on \[u2014] \fBscp \-t $(cat /etc/x)\fR is refused as an scp whose words cannot be trusted \[u2014] while a command of no family keeps whatever behaviour that setting gives it.
.PP
\fBA wrapper is not read through.\fR \fBenv scp \-t /etc\fR, \fBsudo rsync\fR, \fBsh \-c "scp \-t /etc"\fR: the command is \fBenv\fR, \fBsudo\fR and \fBsh\fR, so no rule covers it and the blanket check refuses it as it did before. A rule is never a way to reach scp through something else.
.PP
What each refusal is counted and logged as:
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.0i) lbx
lw(1.0i) lx.
T{
Label
T}	T{
What it means
T}
T{
\fBcommand_\:direction\fR
T}	T{
The movement the rule does not allow (\fBscp \-\:f\fR where only uploads are allowed,\: a push where only fetches are)
T}
T{
\fBcommand_\:path\fR
T}	T{
A path outside \fBpaths\fR,\: inside \fBdeny_\:paths\fR,\: above its own root,\: or a glob whose expansion cannot be proven inside an allowed subtree
T}
T{
\fBcommand_\:recursive\fR
T}	T{
\fBscp \-\:r\fR without \fBrecursive:\: true\fR
T}
T{
\fBcommand_\:delete\fR
T}	T{
An rsync option that removes files,\: without \fBdelete:\: true\fR
T}
T{
\fBcommand_\:server\fR
T}	T{
Not the far side of a client\(aqs transfer at all: an scp with neither \fB\-\:t\fR nor \fB\-\:f\fR,\: an rsync with no \fB\-\:\-\:server\fR (which would dial out of the target)
T}
T{
\fBcommand_\:env\fR
T}	T{
A \fBVAR=\:value\fR assignment in front of a transfer command
T}
T{
\fBcommand_\:no_\:rule\fR
T}	T{
A family this gateway reads,\: with no rule of its own
T}
T{
\fBcommand_\:syntax\fR
T}	T{
A transfer line that could not be read as one simple command
T}
.TE
.PP
Two honest limits, because a boundary that is believed to be somewhere it is not is worse than a narrow one:
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBrsync\(aqs file list is inside rsync\(aqs own protocol.\fR In server mode the arguments carry the transfer root and the options; which files move is negotiated afterwards, in a stream this gateway relays but does not parse. So an rsync rule decides the direction, the deletions and the root \[u2014] and everything under that root is in reach of the transfer. Where a per\-file rule is the requirement, \fBsftp\fR is the protocol that can carry one.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBA glob is the target shell\(aqs to expand.\fR \fBscp \-f /srv/data/*\fR is admitted only when \fBpaths\fR covers a whole subtree that contains the directory the pattern sits in (\fB/srv/data/**\fR), since a shell\(aqs \fB*\fR does not cross a \fB/\fR. With a single\-level pattern, or with any \fBdeny_paths\fR \[u2014] which a glob cannot be proven clear of \[u2014] it is refused. Globs need \fBallow_shell_syntax\fR to reach a rule at all.
.RE
.PP
.SS "server.listeners[].ssh.recording"
.PP
The access log says a session happened. It cannot say what was done in it, because what was done is a stream of control sequences inside the channel. This writes that stream to a file per channel, in the asciicast v2 format, so "what did they actually run" is a question with an answer that is watched rather than reconstructed:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
xproxyctl session show /var/log/xproxy/sessions/session\-20260921\-143022.100\-alice.cast
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
The format is line oriented, so a recording cut short by a crash or by \fBmax_file_bytes\fR still plays up to where it stops, and it is text, so the usual tools work on it. It is a stream, not a transcript: what the person saw is what a terminal makes of it, which means \fBreading one is replaying it\fR \[u2014] a terminal is an interpreter, and the bytes in the file were written by the person recorded. \fBxproxyctl session show\fR, \fBshow \-safe\fR and \fBplay\fR read one with the sequences that reach outside the replay taken out: the clipboard writes, the title changes, and the device reports a terminal answers on its own input, which a shell then reads as a command line. \fBasciinema play\fR and \fBcat\fR do not. See USAGE.md.
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.0i) lbw(0.6i) lbw(0.9i) lbx
lw(1.0i) lw(0.6i) lw(0.9i) lx.
T{
Key
T}	T{
Type
T}	T{
Default
T}	T{
Description
T}
T{
\fBenabled\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBtrue\fR
T}	T{
Present so a principal can turn a listener\(aqs recording off;\: there is no reason to write it as \fBtrue\fR
T}
T{
\fBdirectory\fR
T}	T{
path
T}	T{
required
T}	T{
Where the files go. It must exist: the proxy does not create it,\: because where these files live is a decision to make rather than to inherit
T}
T{
\fBfile_\:prefix\fR
T}	T{
name
T}	T{
\fBsession\fR
T}	T{
Begins each file name,\: which is then the time and the login,\: and always ends \fB.\:cast\fR
T}
T{
\fBinput\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
Record what was typed as well as what was shown,\: as asciicast \fBi\fR events beside the \fBo\fR ones. It warns,\: and the warning is the point: a terminal\(aqs input stream carries what the screen never showed,\: which includes every password typed into a \fBsudo\fR or \fBsu\fR prompt. On the graphical gates (\fBvnc\fR,\: \fBrdp\fR) it means the viewer\(aqs or client\(aqs own stream \-\- every key and pointer event,\: and on RDP the channel traffic a file would leave through \-\- and a message or unit the policy refused is written as a mark rather than as bytes,\: so a replay shows that the client tried and the gateway said no
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:file_\:bytes\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB33554432\fR
T}	T{
Bounds one recording,\: counted in session bytes;\: 4096..4294967296. Past it the session carries on and the file says it stopped
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:files\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB1000\fR
T}	T{
Recordings this listener keeps,\: removing the oldest it wrote. It bounds what the proxy leaves behind;\: anything that must be kept belongs somewhere the proxy does not prune
T}
T{
\fBcommands\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBtrue\fR
T}	T{
Record \fBexec\fR sessions too,\: not only the ones with a terminal
T}
.TE
.PP
The header carries the terminal size from \fBpty\-req\fR, the login and the target, and for an \fBexec\fR the command. A \fBwindow\-change\fR becomes a resize event, so a session that was widened replays at both widths rather than wrapping everything after it in the wrong place. Both of the target\(aqs streams are recorded: a terminal does not keep stdout and stderr apart either, and a recording without stderr would be missing exactly the errors.
.PP
An \fBsftp\fR channel is not recorded. It is not a terminal, and its own \fBsftp\fR log line already says what each request did.
.PP
\fBThese files hold everything the session showed.\fR On an administrative session that is a list of everything worth having \[u2014] keys printed, configuration read, tokens echoed. They are written \fB0600\fR by the proxy user, with \fBO_EXCL\fR and proxy\-chosen names, in a directory the operator names; the directory deserves the care the credentials in it will deserve. \fBinput: true\fR goes further still, and is the difference between watching over a shoulder and running a keylogger: it is off by default, it warns when set, and whether it is lawful where you are is not a question this configuration can answer.
.PP
A recording that cannot be opened does not stop the session: it is an error in the log and an \fBssh_recording_failed\fR event, because a bastion that refuses work when a disk fills is its own outage. One that stops part way is logged as short, with the reason.
.SS "server.listeners[].ssh.mfa"
.PP
A second factor after the key or the password. The client is told authentication partially succeeded (RFC 4252 partial success) and is then asked, over keyboard\-interactive, for a one\-time code (RFC 6238 TOTP over the HMAC\-OTP of RFC 4226). Nothing about the session exists until the code verifies: a key alone opens no channel.
.PP
The same section shape, the same enrolment file and the same rules serve the HTTP \fBmfa\fR filter. That is deliberate \[u2014] a second factor that means different things on different ports is not a second factor, because the weakest door decides.
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.0i) lbw(0.9i) lbw(0.9i) lbx
lw(1.0i) lw(0.9i) lw(0.9i) lx.
