.PP
Generate a key and the record to publish with \fBxproxyctl ech keygen\fR; the configuration and key files it writes are what \fBconfig_file\fR and \fBkey_file\fR point at. Enabling ECH raises the listener to TLS 1.3, since a 1.2 handshake has no encrypted hello to carry it.
.PP
\fBThe public name needs a certificate here.\fR A client whose key is stale \[u2014] a cached DNS answer, a record that has not propagated \[u2014] falls back to an ordinary handshake with the public name, and meets a certificate error unless this listener can serve it. Validation warns when no configured certificate covers it. That fallback is not a failure mode to be avoided; it is what makes rotation safe.
.PP
\fBRotating.\fR Generate the new key, add it to \fBkeys\fR with \fBretry: true\fR, publish an \fBech=\fR list containing both, wait out the old record\(aqs TTL everywhere, then drop the old key. \fBxproxyctl tls\fR prints the list the listener is actually serving, so the published value and the served value cannot drift apart unnoticed. \fBxproxyctl reload\-certs\fR re\-reads ECH keys along with certificates.
.PP
\fB\fBrequire\fR is a blunt instrument.\fR It refuses every client that did not use ECH, including one whose DNS answer was stripped by a resolver that does not know the record, and one whose platform does not implement ECH at all. It is for a listener that exists only for ECH clients; validation prints an advice line saying so.
.PP
\fBECH and layer 4 routing.\fR A \fBkind: tcp\fR listener routes on the SNI it can see, which with ECH is the \fIouter\fR name: every ECH client looks like a connection to the public name, so a passthrough listener in front of an ECH listener cannot split them. Terminate TLS where ECH is accepted, or give the public name its own backend.
.PP
\fBFingerprints.\fR The outer hello is what JA3 and JA4 are computed from, so fingerprinting keeps working; what changes is that the SNI in \fBsni\fR is the public name for every ECH client. The access log carries \fBech: true\fR when ECH was accepted, which is how to tell the two apart.
.SS "server.listeners[].tls.expiry"
.PP
A certificate outliving its validity is the single most common way a working service stops working, and by default this proxy serves an expired certificate: the client is the one that decides whether to trust it, so serving one is not a security hole \[u2014] it is an outage nobody can read, because every client discovers it separately.
.PP
This section says so in one place instead. What it can do is bounded, and the boundary is deliberate:
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBrefuse_expired\fR makes an already expired certificate a \fBload error\fR. At start the listener does not come up and the message names the file. On a reload the new configuration is refused and \fBthe certificate already in use keeps working\fR, which is the case where refusing is strictly better than serving: a botched renewal that wrote an expired file no longer replaces a working one.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBwarn\fR is a window before expiry. Certificates inside it are reported, worst first, by \fBxproxyctl tls\fR (an \fBEXPIRY\fR line each), by \fBGET /v1/tls/expiring\fR, and in the security log at every load and reload.
.RE
.PP
.PP
A certificate that expires while the proxy is running is \fBnever\fR unloaded, whatever this section says: a listener that stops answering is worse than one answering with a certificate the client will reject for itself, and unloading it would turn a renewal that ran late into an outage. It is reported and counted, and \fBdocs/HA.md\fR covers why that report matters more than the refusal on a pair of nodes.
.PP
.TS
