.TE
.PP
Two bounds, because they answer different attackers. The \fBtotal\fR rate protects the accept path itself whatever the traffic is spread across. The \fBper source\fR rate protects everyone else from one source, and it is keyed by a \fBnetwork rather than an address\fR on purpose: an attacker with a /64 of IPv6 has more addresses than any table could hold, so a per address bound would be no bound at all while the per address table would itself be the thing that filled up. The default /64 is the smallest block an operator is normally given; above /96 validation warns, because counting single addresses costs memory and stops nothing.
.PP
A refused connection is \fBclosed immediately after accept, before a byte is read\fR, which is all a listener can do about a connection the kernel has already handed it, and it is cheap. Refusals are counted in \fBrate_refused_connections\fR and \fBxproxy_connections_rate_refused_total\fR, and reported in the error log through the same aggregation as the other accept refusals \[u2014] a client looping connections must not make each refusal cost a synchronous log write.
.PP
A listener\(aqs own \fBconnection_rate\fR sections \fBreplace\fR these for that listener rather than adding to them, so one number is the answer to "what bounds this port". A rate change on reload rebinds no socket.
.PP
\fBmax_buffered_body_bytes\fR covers every feature that materialises a whole request body: \fBupload_guard\fR, \fBsensitive_data\fR, \fBaccount_guard\fR, \fBopenapi\fR, \fBgraphql\fR, \fBbody_rewrite\fR, \fBwasm\fR, the WAF\(aqs body inspection, ICAP, a virtual patch\(aqs body pattern and a mirrored request. Each of those is bounded per request, and the product was the real ceiling: \fBmax_connections_per_ip\fR (256) times \fBmax_body_bytes\fR (10 MiB) is about 2.5 GiB of heap from one address, sent slowly enough to stay inside \fBread_timeout\fR. A request whose body cannot be charged is refused before it is read; \fBxproxyctl stats\fR shows what is held, the high\-water mark and the refusals.
.SS "server.normalization"
.PP
What the proxy does with encoding tricks in the request target before routing, rate limits, filters and the WAF look at it. Routing already decodes the path once and resolves dot segments and duplicate slashes; these checks refuse the forms that make two components read a request differently, and optionally fold Unicode spellings for routing. A refusal answers 400 with reason \fBnormalization\fR and the check as \fBdetail\fR, counts in \fBdenied_normalization\fR and feeds ban triggers under \fBnormalization\fR. The WAF still inspects the raw request line, so its rules see what the client sent.
.PP
.TS
