.SS "Client certificate identity headers"
.PP
A backend behind a TLS\-terminating proxy cannot see the handshake, so the proxy tells it. RFC 9440 standardises \fBClient\-Cert\fR and \fBClient\-Cert\-Chain\fR for that; Envoy has used \fBX\-Forwarded\-Client\-Cert\fR for years; and nginx and Apache deployments read a handful of \fBX\-SSL\-Client\-*\fR names their own configurations set. Every one of them is a statement about something only the terminating proxy can know.
.PP
Which makes every one of them an authentication bypass when a client can send it. The backend has no way to tell the proxy\(aqs header from the client\(aqs \[u2014] they are the same bytes in the same field \[u2014] so a request carrying its own \fBClient\-Cert\fR is a request that chooses its own identity. RFC 9440 section 3 says so in as many words: the terminating proxy must sanitise the header.
.PP
So \fBall of them are removed from every forwarded request\fR, unless the immediate peer is inside \fBtrusted_proxies\fR \[u2014] the one case where the header belongs to a proxy that did terminate a handshake. The list is \fBClient\-Cert\fR, \fBClient\-Cert\-Chain\fR, \fBX\-Forwarded\-Client\-Cert\fR, \fBX\-Client\-Cert\fR, \fBX\-Client\-Cert\-Chain\fR, \fBX\-Client\-Verify\fR, \fBX\-Client\-Subject\-DN\fR, \fBX\-Client\-Issuer\-DN\fR, \fBX\-SSL\-Client\-Cert\fR, \fBX\-SSL\-Client\-Verify\fR, \fBX\-SSL\-Client\-S\-DN\fR, \fBX\-SSL\-Client\-I\-DN\fR, \fBX\-SSL\-Client\-Serial\fR, \fBX\-SSL\-Client\-Fingerprint\fR, \fBSSL\-Client\-Cert\fR, \fBSSL\-Client\-Verify\fR and \fBSSL\-Client\-Subject\-DN\fR. A backend that reads one of these therefore reads what this proxy said or nothing at all.
.PP
\fBroutes[].client_cert_headers\fR then writes the truth over the blank: \fBrfc9440\fR sets \fBClient\-Cert\fR to the leaf and \fBClient\-Cert\-Chain\fR to the rest of the chain in order, each as a Structured Fields Byte Sequence (RFC 8941: standard base64 with padding, between colons, which a structured\-fields parser accepts and anything else it rejects); \fBxfcc\fR sets Envoy\(aqs header. A route that wants a different shape sets its own header from \fB${cert:...}\fR, which strips a client copy of that name too.
.SS "Expressions"
.PP
\fBroutes[].when\fR, \fBrequest_headers.when\fR and \fBresponse_headers.when\fR hold a condition that is parsed at load (unknown names, functions, arities, patterns and capture groups are errors) and evaluated per request. Values are strings; a variable without a value is the empty string. \fB==\fR and \fB!=\fR compare as strings; \fB<\fR, \fB<=\fR, \fB>\fR and \fB>=\fR compare numerically when both sides are numbers and by string otherwise. A bare string is true when it is neither empty, \fB0\fR nor \fBfalse\fR.
.PP
.TS
