.SS "Dual\-stack endpoints: the race, and restricting it"
.PP
An endpoint name with both an A and an AAAA record has two ways to be reached, and the one that is broken costs a connect timeout on every request that tries it first. With \fBaddress_family: any\fR (the default) the families are \fBraced\fR as RFC 8305 describes: the first is tried, the other follows after \fBfallback_delay\fR (300ms, the value the RFC recommends), and whichever connects first wins. An estate that has just turned IPv6 on does not have to know about any of this.
.PP
\fBipv4\fR or \fBipv6\fR dials that family \fBonly\fR. It is for the estate where one family is the only one that works, and naming it means a name that also has the other kind of record cannot quietly use the family the policy meant to exclude \[u2014] which is the failure a preference would hide.
.PP
A negative \fBfallback_delay\fR turns the race off, so the families are tried in order; naming one family makes the delay meaningless and warns.
.SS "Two balancers that read what the endpoints are doing"
.PP
\fBleast_conn\fR scans every endpoint and takes the best, which has a failure mode of its own: every proxy in a fleet sees the same "best" endpoint at the same moment and they all send to it together, so the herd moves from one endpoint to the next.
.PP
\fB\fBp2c\fR\fR \[u2014] power of two choices \[u2014] takes two endpoints at random and uses the better of those, by in\-flight count per unit of weight. One comparison is enough to avoid the worst endpoint, and the randomness means two proxies rarely agree on where to send, so load spreads instead of sloshing. It is the one to reach for when the endpoints are equal and the load varies, and it is the one that fits a pool with no responses to measure \[u2014] a layer 4 relay\(aqs.
.PP
\fB\fBewma\fR\fR weighs endpoints by the smoothed time to first byte the pool already keeps for outlier detection, times the queue a request would join: an estimate of how long a request sent now would take. An endpoint answering slowly gets less work \fBlong before\fR it is slow enough to fail a health check or be ejected, which is the difference between shedding load away from a struggling machine and waiting for it to break.
.PP
An endpoint with no latency sample yet costs nothing, so a new or recovered one is tried rather than starved by the fact that nothing is known about it; \fBslow_start\fR is what keeps that from being a flood. On a quiet pool, where nothing distinguishes the endpoints, both balancers spread uniformly rather than settling on the first.
.SS "Tiers: priority, backup and locality"
.PP
\fBendpoints[].priority\fR tiers a pool. The endpoints of the \fBlowest priority number that has an available member\fR carry the traffic and the rest are ignored; when that tier has nothing left, the next one takes over, and hands it back when the first returns. A retry that has used up a tier falls to the next one too.
.PP
That is how a failover pool is written, and a \fBbackup endpoint is simply one in a later tier\fR \[u2014] not a special kind of endpoint:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
upstreams:
  \- name: app
    endpoints:
      \- {address: "10.0.1.10:8080"}               # priority 0
      \- {address: "10.0.1.11:8080"}
      \- {address: "10.9.9.9:8080", priority: 1}   # only when both are gone
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBlocality\fR prefers the endpoints in this node\(aqs own \fBserver.zone\fR over the ones elsewhere, which keeps traffic off the links between sites and away from their latency:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
server:
  zone: east
upstreams:
  \- name: app
    locality: {prefer_zone: true, min_local: 2}
    endpoints:
      \- {address: "10.0.1.10:8080", zone: east}
      \- {address: "10.0.1.11:8080", zone: east}
      \- {address: "10.1.1.10:8080", zone: west}
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
It is a \fBpreference, not a pin\fR. An estate that pinned traffic to one zone would lose the service when the zone lost it, which is the opposite of what zones are for \[u2014] so when the local endpoints are gone, the others take the traffic. \fBmin_local\fR is how many local endpoints must be available before the remote ones are ignored: below it everything is used, so a zone down to one surviving endpoint does not take the whole load alone.
.PP
An endpoint with \fBno zone is local to every zone\fR: "somewhere unknown" is not a reason to send traffic across a site. \fBprefer_zone\fR without \fBserver.zone\fR is refused at load \[u2014] there would be nothing to compare against \[u2014] and a pool where no endpoint names a zone warns, because it would prefer nothing.
.PP
Tiering is applied by leaving the other endpoints out of the choice rather than by shortening the list the balancer sees, which matters for \fBhash\fR: shortening it would move every key, while leaving endpoints out moves only theirs.
.SS "Taking something out of rotation: drain and maintenance"
.PP
Draining is \fBnot\fR the same as unhealthy, and the difference is the point. An unhealthy endpoint is one the proxy found broken; a drained one is one a person decided to stop sending work to. So it is set from outside \[u2014] \fBxproxyctl drain POOL [ADDRESS]\fR, \fBPOST /v1/drain\fR, the GUI \[u2014] and it \fBsurvives a reload\fR, because a reload builds new pools and an operator who drained a machine to patch it did not mean "until the next configuration change".
.PP
Nothing is closed. New work stops; the requests, sessions and connections already there run to their own end. That is what makes it usable for a rolling restart, and it is why draining is a separate idea from the connection bounds, which do end things.
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBAn endpoint\fR: \fBendpoints[].drain: true\fR, or \fBxproxyctl drain POOL ADDRESS\fR. It is passed over by every balancer and shows as \fBdraining\fR in \fBxproxyctl upstreams\fR, still \fBhealthy\fR, so the two stay distinguishable.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBA pool\fR: \fBmaintenance: true\fR, or \fBxproxyctl drain POOL\fR. The pool offers nothing at all.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBBack in\fR: \fBxproxyctl drain \-restore POOL [ADDRESS]\fR.
.RE
.PP
.PP
A decision made through the API \fBoverrides the file\fR until the daemon restarts, because the person who made it knew something the file did not. \fBxproxyctl drain\fR with no argument lists what has been decided, including an explicit restore of something the file drains.
.PP
A decision about a pool or an address that is not there is recorded rather than refused, and the answer says so: an endpoint may be about to arrive from discovery.
.SS "Bounding one endpoint, and the age of a connection"
.PP
\fBendpoints[].max_connections\fR (or \fBmax_connections_per_endpoint\fR for a whole pool) bounds what is in flight to one endpoint. Past it the endpoint is \fBpassed over\fR rather than queued behind: the pool has others, and holding work for one while they are idle is the opposite of balancing. When every endpoint is at its bound the pool offers nothing, which is what its own \fBqueue\fR and \fBcircuit_breaker\fR are there to answer. It is for the endpoint that cannot take what the pool can give it \[u2014] a small instance beside large ones, a service with a database connection pool of its own, a machine that answers slowly under load rather than refusing.
.PP
\fBmax_connection_age\fR bounds how long one upstream connection is kept, so a pool\(aqs traffic follows its endpoints instead of sticking to whichever ones were there when the connections were made: a keep\-alive connection can outlive a deploy, a scale\-out and an endpoint\(aqs whole useful life, and every request on it goes where that connection goes.
.PP
\fBIt does not close anything mid\-exchange.\fR Closing a connection at its age would cut a request that has done nothing wrong, and from inside a socket there is no way to tell an exchange in progress from an idle connection \[u2014] both are a blocked read. So the age marks the connection, and the layer that does know where an exchange ends closes it once that exchange is over. The connection is never reused past its age and nothing in flight is disturbed; \fBxproxyctl upstreams\fR counts the retirements.
.PP
That end only exists for \fBHTTP/1.1\fR, where a connection carries one exchange at a time. An HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 connection carries many streams and is never between exchanges, so the bound does not apply to one: \fBh2c\fR and \fBh3\fR refuse it at load, and an \fBhttps\fR pool (which may negotiate HTTP/2) warns.
.SS "A Unix domain socket as an endpoint"
.PP
\fBaddress: unix:/run/app.sock\fR reaches a service that lives beside the proxy rather than across a network: an application server on the same host, a local scanner, a sidecar. A socket is the better way to reach one \[u2014] there is no port for anything else on the machine to connect to, file permissions decide who may open it, and nothing about it is routable.
.PP
The path must be absolute. Socket and \fBhost:port\fR endpoints mix in one pool, which is what a service moving from a port to a socket needs, and everything else about a pool applies unchanged: the balancer, weights, canaries, affinity, outlier ejection, and the \fBtcp\fR health check (which connects to the socket, because a probe to a port would be proving something about a path nothing uses).
.PP
Two things do not work with one, and are refused at load rather than discovered later:
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fB\fBscheme: https\fR without \fBtls.server_name\fR.\fR A URL has a host and a socket has a path, so the proxy gives a socket endpoint a synthetic authority for the URL \[u2014] which is not a name a certificate can match. Naming the one the backend presents makes it verifiable again.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fB\fBh3\fR, and \fBdiscovery\fR.\fR HTTP/3 needs UDP to a host, and discovery produces \fBhost:port\fR records; neither has anything to say about a path.
.RE
.PP
.PP
The synthetic authority ends in \fB.socket.invalid\fR, a name that must never resolve, so a dialler that somehow ignored the socket fails immediately instead of reaching a machine on the network. It is never sent to the backend: the \fBHost\fR header is the client\(aqs own, as with any other endpoint, so a service that routes on it keeps working.
.SS "upstreams[].retry_budget"
.PP
A retry (or a hedged copy) is only sent while the retries in flight to the pool stay below \fBpercent\fR of the requests in flight, with \fBmin_concurrency\fR always allowed so a low\-traffic pool can still retry. As live traffic falls the allowance falls with it, so retries cannot amplify an outage.
.PP
.TS
