\fBallow_\:stale\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
Let the agent answer from its own state without asking a server. Faster,\: and may be a moment behind,\: so it warns: a balancer acting on stale membership sends traffic to an instance that has gone
T}
T{
\fBwait\fR
T}	T{
duration
T}	T{
\fB5m\fR
T}	T{
How long one blocking query may be held open;\: 1s to 10m
T}
.TE
.PP
\fBWhy a type of its own rather than \fBhttp\fR with \fBformat: consul\fR.\fR The polled form is still there and still works. What this adds is Consul\(aqs \fBblocking query\fR: the agent holds the request open until the answer changes and returns an index the next request carries, so the proxy learns about an instance that went away in about the time Consul takes to notice it, instead of up to a polling interval later. For a load balancer that gap is the whole point \[u2014] a polled registry keeps sending traffic to a machine that is already gone for as long as the interval lasts.
.PP
The loop therefore waits on the agent rather than on a ticker: after an answer it asks again at once, because the next answer \fIis\fR the next change. \fBinterval\fR becomes the pause after a \fBfailure\fR \[u2014] without one, an agent that is down or answering 403 would be asked again immediately and forever, which is a loop against somebody else\(aqs machine. An index that goes backwards (a Consul server restart) resets to 1 rather than blocking against an index that will never be reached.
.PP
Only instances whose checks all pass are used, and the check is made here as well as asked for in the query: the filter in the query is the agent\(aqs opinion, and this one is the proxy\(aqs. \fBWeights.Passing\fR becomes the endpoint weight, and a blank service address falls back to the node\(aqs.
.PP
\fBHow large a resolution may be.\fR \fBmax_endpoints\fR bounds one resolution. It is not a limit on the estate \[u2014] 4096 endpoints in one pool is more than a load balancer can usefully spread across \[u2014] but on what a single answer can do to this process: a DNS response over TCP carries thousands of A records, and a four megabyte registry response tens of thousands of entries, each of which would become a health\-check goroutine and a place in the hash ring. Beyond the bound the resolution is \fBtruncated rather than refused\fR, because refusing keeps the previous set, and for a pool whose backends have all moved that is a pool serving nothing. \fBxproxyctl upstreams\fR counts the truncations, and the log line names how many were returned.
.SS "An endpoint that arrives while the pool is serving"
.PP
A registry announces an instance when its process starts, not when it is ready to answer: the service registers itself, then opens its database connections, loads its caches and finally listens. An address appearing in a resolution is therefore a weaker claim than it looks, and a pool that sends traffic to it immediately produces a burst of failures the proxy caused \[u2014] the outlier ejection then has to clean up after a decision that should not have been made.
.PP
So with \fBhealth_check\fR configured, an endpoint that \fBarrives after the pool started serving\fR begins unhealthy and is not picked until it passes \fBhealthy_threshold\fR probes. Its first probe runs at once rather than after the usual jitter, so the wait is one probe rather than up to one \fBinterval\fR.
.PP
Three cases are deliberately not that:
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBNo \fBhealth_check\fR.\fR There is no probe to wait for, so a discovered endpoint serves as soon as it is resolved. Waiting would mean never.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBNothing else can carry the traffic.\fR If every other endpoint in the pool is unhealthy, draining, ejected or at its own \fBmax_active\fR, the new one is used immediately: an unprobed endpoint is better than an empty pool, and there is no all\-unhealthy fallback to catch that case.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBThe endpoints the pool starts with\fR, both the static \fBendpoints\fR and discovery\(aqs first resolution, start healthy \[u2014] at process start nothing has been probed yet, and making them wait would serve nothing at all for a probe interval on every restart.
.RE
.PP
.PP
An address that leaves a resolution and comes back is a new endpoint, so it waits again; what is listening there now is not the process that was healthy before. \fBxproxyctl upstreams\fR shows \fBhealthy: false\fR for an endpoint in this state, the same as for one a probe has failed.
.SS "upstreams[].health_check"
.PP
.TS
