.SS "upstreams[].origin_signature"
.PP
Network filtering keeps most traffic off an origin, but not where the origin is reachable from the internet by design (a cloud service, a shared host) or where another tenant sits on the same network. A signed header lets the origin refuse anything that did not pass through the proxy. Every forwarded request carries
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
X\-Xproxy\-Signature: v1;t=<unix seconds>;kid=<key id>;sig=<base64url HMAC\-SHA256>
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
where the MAC covers, as newline separated lines: \fBv1\fR, the method, the \fBHost\fR sent upstream (lower case), the path, the raw query, the timestamp, the client address (the \fBX\-Real\-Ip\fR value), the request id (\fBX\-Request\-Id\fR) and the values of the \fBinclude\fR headers in order (repeated values joined with commas). The key id is the first eight hex digits of the SHA\-256 of the key. A signature header sent by a client is always replaced.
.PP
.TS
