T}
T{
\fBbody_\:digest\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
Cover the request body as well: the proxy buffers it,\: appends \fBsha\-\:256=\::\:<base64>:\fR as a final signed line,\: sends it as \fBContent\-\:Digest\fR and adds \fB;bd=\:1\fR to the signature header. Without it a signature proves that a request passed through the proxy,\: not what it carried \[u2014] anything that can reach the origin can replay a captured header set with a body of its own while the timestamp is inside the TTL. A bodied request larger than 8 MiB is refused with \fB413\fR rather than forwarded with a signature that stops at the headers
T}
.TE
.PP
At the origin, recompute the MAC with the shared key selected by \fBkid\fR, compare in constant time, and refuse when it differs, the key id is unknown or \fBt\fR is older than the TTL. When the header carries \fBbd=1\fR, the last signed line is the body digest: hash the body and compare. Dropping \fBbd=1\fR does not turn that off, because the digest is part of what was signed.
.PP
Within the TTL a signature is replayable by anything that captured it (a log, a proxy in front of the origin, a browser extension). Where that matters, have the origin remember the \fBX\-Request\-Id\fR values it has already answered for the TTL and refuse a repeat: the proxy sets a fresh one per request and the id is covered by the signature, so a replay carries the same id. \fBbody_digest\fR bounds what a replay can change; the id dedupe stops the replay itself. HARDENING.md has verifier snippets; \fBinternal/originsig\fR has \fBVerify\fR for origins written in Go. Combine with mutual TLS (\fBtls.client_cert_file\fR) and with network filtering: each closes what the others cannot.
.SH "http"
.PP
\fBkind: http\fR has no section of its own here, because it is most of this document. The other twenty\-seven kinds each get one section, since each reads one protocol and has one policy about it; HTTP is a pipeline of policies, and they are written up where each belongs.
.PP
Where to look, in roughly the order a request passes through:
.PP
