T}	T{
\fB[]\fR
T}	T{
Clients always in block mode whatever the share (internal testers,\: a pilot customer)
T}
.TE
.PP
Gradual roll\-out: detect everywhere, then \fBblock_percent: 0\fR with the testers in \fBblock_cidrs\fR, then raise the share in steps while the security log\(aqs \fBwaf_detected\fR entries (the requests detect mode would have blocked) stay explainable, then \fB100\fR. \fBxproxyctl waf\fR shows the share and the canary prefixes per route, and the access log carries \fBwaf_enforced: true\fR or \fBfalse\fR for every request of such a route.
.SH "degradation"
.PP
Present means suspect clients are served slowly instead of being refused.
.PP
Every other answer in this file is binary: a client is served, or it is refused. For a client that has done something wrong but not enough to ban \[u2014] touched a decoy, scored badly, arrived from a range with a history \[u2014] both are wrong. Serving it in full funds the next request. Refusing it tells it exactly which request to change, and hands a scanner a clean signal to tune against: it will try variations until one is not refused, and the refusal tells it when it has found one.
.PP
A degraded client is served, correctly, slowly. The page arrives, so there is nothing to report as broken and nothing to tune against; it arrives at eight kilobytes a second on a connection that cannot be reused, so a crawl that cost the scanner nothing now costs it the one thing it has least of.
.PP
Levels are tried in order and the first that admits the request decides, so the narrowest goes first.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
degradation:
  levels:
    # A client a honeypot or a honeytoken marked: slow, held, and no
    # keep\-alive.
    \- name: marked
      marked: true
      bytes_per_second: 8192
      delay: 500ms
      close: true

    # A high bot score, on the endpoints worth scraping.
    \- name: likely\-bot
      bot_score_at: 60
      routes: [catalogue, search]
      bytes_per_second: 65536

    # A range with a history, on writes only.
    \- name: known\-range
      client_cidrs: ["203.0.113.0/24"]
      methods: [POST, PUT, PATCH]
      delay: 2s
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
.TS
