At least one of \fBmfa_users_file\fR and \fBkeys_file\fR is required: with neither there is nothing to provision.
.PP
\fBWhat is implemented\fR, and deliberately nothing else \[u2014] an endpoint that half\-understands an operation is worse than one that refuses it, because the directory believes the change landed:
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(2.3i) lbx
lw(2.3i) lx.
T{
Request
T}	T{
What happens
T}
T{
\fBPOST /\:Users\fR
T}	T{
Creates the resource,\: enrols a second factor,\: issues a key
T}
T{
\fBGET /\:Users\fR
T}	T{
Lists,\: with \fBfilter=\:userName eq "name"\fR,\: \fBstartIndex\fR and \fBcount\fR
T}
T{
\fBGET /\:Users/\:{id}\fR
T}	T{
Reads one,\: with what is \fIcurrently\fR in place rather than what was provisioned once
T}
T{
\fBPUT /\:Users/\:{id}\fR
T}	T{
Replaces \fBexternalId\fR,\: \fBdisplayName\fR and \fBactive\fR
T}
T{
\fBPATCH /\:Users/\:{id}\fR
T}	T{
\fBreplace\fR of \fBactive\fR,\: \fBexternalId\fR or \fBdisplayName\fR,\: by path or as a value object
T}
T{
\fBDELETE /\:Users/\:{id}\fR
T}	T{
Deprovisions and forgets the resource
T}
T{
\fBGET /\:ServiceProviderConfig\fR,\: \fB/\:ResourceTypes\fR,\: \fB/\:Schemas\fR
T}	T{
What a provider fetches before it provisions anything
T}
.TE
.PP
Everything else answers a SCIM error object (\fBurn:ietf:params:scim:api:messages:2.0:Error\fR) with the \fBscimType\fR RFC 7644 section 3.12 gives it: a filter on another attribute is \fBinvalidFilter\fR, a \fBuserName\fR that would be changed is \fBmutability\fR, an attribute this endpoint does not keep is \fBinvalidSyntax\fR, a second create of one name is \fBuniqueness\fR (409).
.PP
\fB\fBactive: false\fR and \fBDELETE\fR do the same thing to the credentials\fR: every API key of that user is \fIrevoked\fR \[u2014] kept in the file as the record of what it reached and when it stopped \[u2014] and the enrolment is removed. The difference is the resource: a deactivated user is still there to be read, which is what \fBstate_file\fR is for, and a deleted one is not. There is no "disabled enrolment" in the enrolment file, so a suspension that left one in place would be a suspension in name only.
.PP
Which makes one setting elsewhere load\-bearing: \fB\fBrequire_enrolment\fR must be on\fR wherever the enrolment file this endpoint writes is used \[u2014] on a gate listener\(aqs \fBmfa\fR section and on the \fBmfa\fR filter alike. With it off, a user with no enrolment is let through \fIunchallenged\fR, so removing an enrolment opens the door instead of closing it, and a deprovisioning would be the opposite of what the directory asked for.
.PP
Which means \fBreactivating mints new credentials.\fR The old secret is gone and cannot be handed back, so \fBactive: true\fR on a deactivated user enrols again and issues a new key; with \fBreturn_secrets: false\fR nobody can read them, and the operator delivers a fresh enrolment with \fBxproxyctl mfa enrol\fR and a fresh key with \fBxproxyctl apikey rotate\fR.
.PP
\fB\fBreturn_secrets\fR is off by default.\fR With it on, the response to a create or a reactivation carries the \fBotpauth://\fR URI, the recovery codes and the key plaintext under the extension attribute \fBsecrets\fR \[u2014] which is what an automated onboarding needs, and which puts them in the provider\(aqs response logs and in whatever it stores. Validation says so at load. With it off the credentials are still made; they are simply not in the answer.
.PP
\fBWhat is not changed here.\fR \fBuserName\fR is immutable, because it is what the credentials are keyed on: a rename is a new user and the old one deprovisioned, said in the directory rather than inferred here. The scopes of an issued key cannot be changed either \[u2014] that is a new credential, and this endpoint does not replace one nobody asked it to replace.
.PP
\fBWhere it runs.\fR Before routing, like the virtual \fBsecurity.txt\fR: the provider needs no route, and no route can take the endpoint away by matching the path first. A request on the endpoint\(aqs path that the selectors refuse is answered 404 and \fBnot\fR routed on \[u2014] passing it to a proxied application would hand it a request meant for the control plane.
.PP
\fBscim\fR and \fBscim_user\fR are in the access log, every change writes a security event (\fBscim\fR with the operation), a refusal writes a deny event with \fBscim\fR as the reason \[u2014] which a ban trigger can name, and a bad token feeds it, because somebody trying tokens against a provisioning endpoint is not a client making a mistake twice. \fBscim_requests\fR and \fBscim_denied\fR are in \fBxproxyctl stats\fR, and \fBxproxy_scim_requests_total{result="answered"|"refused"}\fR in the metrics.
.SH "virtual_patches[]"
.PP
A virtual patch blocks a known vulnerability by the shape of the requests that exploit it, while the application is being fixed, and records how often it fired. Patches run right after route matching, before rate limits, filters and the WAF, so they cost nothing for other traffic and need no \fBwaf\fR section. Every listed condition must hold for a patch to apply; at least one of \fBpaths\fR, \fBpath_regex\fR, \fBquery\fR, \fBheaders\fR, \fBcookies\fR or \fBbody\fR is required. A match with \fBaction: block\fR answers \fBstatus\fR with reason \fBvirtual_patch\fR and the id as \fBdetail\fR (ban category \fBvirtual_patch\fR); with \fBaction: log\fR the request continues and the security event and the access log carry \fBvirtual_patch: <id>\fR. \fBxproxyctl patches\fR and \fBGET /v1/patches\fR list the patches with hits, last hit, expiry and state; \fBxproxy_virtual_patch_hits_total{patch}\fR counts per patch and hits survive reloads.
.PP
.TS
