.SS "jwt.providers[].token_exchange: a token the backend cannot reuse"
.PP
A token the client sent to the gateway is a token the gateway forwards, and everything behind the gateway then holds a credential that works at the gateway. That is the confused\-deputy problem in one sentence: a backend with a bug \[u2014] a log line, an error page, an outbound request to somewhere it should not go \[u2014] leaks a token that reaches the front door again with all of the client\(aqs scopes on it.
.PP
Exchange replaces it. The proxy presents the verified client token to the authorization server and asks for one issued for \fIthis\fR backend: a different \fBaudience\fR, usually fewer \fBscopes\fR, and no standing anywhere else. The backend never sees the client\(aqs token, so there is nothing there to leak that would work at the gateway. The client\(aqs identity survives \[u2014] the authorization server puts the same subject in the new token, which is what makes this an exchange rather than an impersonation.
.PP
The exchange runs \fBafter\fR the client\(aqs token has been verified, never before: sending an unverified token to the authorization server would spend its capacity on whatever a client posted, and caching the answer against the token\(aqs digest would let one client\(aqs garbage occupy the table. It also runs after \fBstrip_token\fR, so the client\(aqs token is gone from the forwarded request whether the exchange succeeded or not.
.PP
An exchange that names neither an audience nor a resource is refused at load: it asks for a token as broad as the one it replaces, which is the feature undone while the configuration reads as if it were on. The \fBissued_token_type\fR in the answer is checked rather than assumed \[u2014] a server that answered with a refresh token would otherwise have it forwarded as an access token, which is a long\-lived credential handed to a backend.
.PP
Outcomes: a refusal from the authorization server (400, 401 or 403 there) is 403 here with detail \fBexchange_refused\fR, because the token is valid and this backend is not somewhere it reaches; an endpoint that cannot be asked is 503 with \fBRetry\-After\fR and detail \fBexchange_unavailable\fR. Refusals are cached for \fBcache_ttl\fR like successes \[u2014] they are the authorization server\(aqs decision about this client and this backend, and asking again per request turns one misconfiguration into load the login flow shares \[u2014] while an unreachable endpoint is never cached. \fBrequired: false\fR lets the request reach the backend with no token, which validation warns about: it is the control failing open on exactly the requests where it went wrong.
.SS "jwt.providers[].dpop: proof of possession"
.PP
A bearer token is a password: whoever holds it is whoever it says. That is the whole of its security model, and it is why a token stolen from a log, a browser\(aqs storage, a proxy\(aqs cache or a crash dump is as good as the original \[u2014] nothing about the request says it came from the client the token was issued to.
.PP
DPoP (RFC 9449) adds the missing part. The client keeps a key pair, the authorization server records the public key\(aqs thumbprint in the token as \fBcnf.jkt\fR, and every request carries a small JWT \[u2014] the proof \[u2014] signed with the private key over \fIthis\fR method, \fIthis\fR URI and \fIthis\fR moment. A stolen token without the key produces no proof, and a proof captured from one request does not fit another.
.PP
What is checked, in this order, because each step is only meaningful once the one before it holds:
.PP
1. The proof is a JWT with \fBtyp: dpop+jwt\fR and an algorithm from \fBdpop.algorithms\fR. Nothing symmetric and no \fBnone\fR: a proof the verifier could have written itself proves nothing about the client. A key carrying private material is refused too \[u2014] that is a client that has sent its secret. 2. Its signature verifies under the key embedded in its own header. On its own that proves nothing, since anybody can generate a key; step 5 is why it matters. 3. \fBhtm\fR and \fBhtu\fR match the request. The method is compared exactly (HTTP methods are case\-sensitive); the URI is compared without query or fragment, as RFC 9449 section 4.3 says, because the query is not what a replay changes. The scheme comes from the connection and the authority from the \fBHost\fR header \[u2014] never from \fBX\-Forwarded\-Proto\fR, since a client that can set that header could otherwise choose which URI its proof has to match. Behind another terminating proxy, \fBdpop.external_url\fR says what the client sees. 4. \fBiat\fR is within \fBmax_age\fR (plus \fBclock_skew\fR, both ways) and the \fBjti\fR has not been seen. Together they bound replay to a window and then remove it. The \fBjti\fR is spent \fIlast\fR, once everything else holds, so a proof refused for another reason does not consume the identifier a correct retry would use. 5. The RFC 7638 thumbprint of the embedded key equals the token\(aqs \fBcnf.jkt\fR. This is the step the rest exists for: it ties the key that signed the proof to the key the authorization server bound the token to. The claims are read from a token \fBthis proxy has already verified\fR \[u2014] reading \fBcnf\fR from an unverified token would let an attacker write their own thumbprint into it. 6. \fBath\fR equals the base64url SHA\-256 of the access token, so a proof cannot be moved between two tokens the same client holds.
.PP
\fBmode: allow\fR costs nothing to turn on: it never refuses an ordinary bearer token, and it closes the replay hole for every token the authorization server did constrain. \fBrequire\fR is the stricter statement that this route takes constrained tokens only.
.PP
With DPoP on, the \fBAuthorization: DPoP <token>\fR scheme RFC 9449 defines is read as well as \fBBearer\fR \[u2014] a server that reads only \fBBearer \fR does not see a sender\-constrained token at all. The proof is removed before forwarding: it is a signed statement about \fIthis\fR hop, and a backend verifying it against its own URI would fail.
.PP
Refusals are 401 with \fBWWW\-Authenticate: DPoP error="invalid_dpop_proof", algs="..."\fR, or \fBerror="invalid_token"\fR when the problem is the token rather than the proof (\fBdpop_missing\fR, \fBdpop_unbound\fR). The access log carries \fBdpop_jkt\fR with the thumbprint that was proved. The details are \fBdpop_proof\fR, \fBdpop_binding\fR, \fBdpop_replay\fR, \fBdpop_missing\fR and \fBdpop_unbound\fR.
.PP
A provider whose key set has never loaded (for example the JWKS URL is unreachable at start) rejects tokens with 503 and \fBRetry\-After\fR until a fetch succeeds; a fetch that returns no keys keeps the previous set.
.SS "jwt.providers[].certificate_binding: the certificate the token names"
.PP
The other answer to a stolen bearer token, and the cheaper one. Where DPoP has the client sign a proof per request, a certificate\-bound token needs no proof at all: the client already proved possession of its private key in the TLS handshake, and the authorization server recorded the certificate\(aqs SHA\-256 thumbprint in the token as \fBcnf["x5t#S256"]\fR (RFC 8705 section 3). So the check is a comparison \[u2014] the thumbprint of the certificate on this connection against the one in the token \[u2014] and a token lifted out of a log, a crash dump or a proxy\(aqs cache is useless on any other connection.
.PP
It is also the more limited one: it works only where the client can present a certificate, which in practice means machine to machine. A browser cannot, which is why DPoP exists. The two can be on together, and a token carrying both confirmations must satisfy both.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
jwt:
  providers:
    \- name: partners
      issuer: https://idp.example.com/
      audiences: [api]
      jwks_url: https://idp.example.com/.well\-known/jwks.json
      certificate_binding: {mode: require}
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
The certificate compared is the one from the handshake \fBthis proxy terminated\fR (\fBserver.listeners[].tls.client_auth\fR must ask for it, and validation warns when no listener does). Where TLS is terminated in front, \fBtrust_forwarded_header\fR reads the certificate from RFC 9440\(aqs \fBClient\-Cert\fR instead \[u2014] and only when the immediate peer is inside \fBtrusted_proxies\fR, because a client that could set that header would otherwise choose which certificate its own token is checked against, which is the whole of the check. A certificate on the connection always wins over a header. The header is parsed as a certificate before it is hashed, so a header that is not one is no certificate rather than a thumbprint of something else, and one over 16 KiB is refused before it is decoded.
.PP
Like DPoP, the comparison runs on claims \fBthis proxy has already verified\fR: \fBcnf\fR read out of an unverified token is a value whoever presented it chose. A padded thumbprint is accepted as the same thumbprint \[u2014] RFC 8705\(aqs encoding has no padding, but an authorization server that adds it has not issued a different value, and refusing it would look exactly like an attack in the log.
.PP
Refusals are 401 with \fBWWW\-Authenticate: Bearer error="invalid_token"\fR and a detail: \fBcert_missing\fR (a bound token presented with no certificate), \fBcert_binding\fR (a bound token on another certificate) or \fBcert_unbound\fR (\fBrequire\fR, and the token carries no binding). The access log carries \fBcert_thumbprint\fR with the thumbprint that matched.
.SS "routes[].jwt"
.PP
