.PP
The access log carries \fBoidc_user\fR for requests with a session and \fBflow: <name>:login\fR, \fBlogin_complete\fR, \fBlogout\fR or \fBfrontchannel_logout\fR for the flow steps. Sessions record the ID token\(aqs \fBsid\fR claim when the provider sends one; a logout at the proxy revokes it as well, so other browsers sharing that provider session end too.
.SS "Kind \fBsaml_sp\fR"
.PP
Logs browsers in as a SAML 2.0 service provider and keeps the result in an encrypted, HttpOnly, SameSite Lax session cookie. A request without a session is redirected to the identity provider with an authentication request (HTTP Redirect binding, deflated); the provider posts the signed response back to \fBacs_path\fR (HTTP POST binding), where it is verified against the configured signing key and checked whole before a cookie is set and the browser is sent back to the page it asked for. Requests with a session carry the listed attributes to the upstream as headers (client supplied values of those headers are always removed) and the cookie is stripped upstream. \fBmetadata_path\fR serves this service provider\(aqs metadata for the provider to import.
.PP
\fBThe profile is deliberately narrow, and the narrowness is the feature.\fR Web single sign\-on breaks in one place \[u2014] the reader that verifies a signature and the reader that consumes the assertion disagreeing about what was signed \[u2014] so everything that lets one document mean two things is refused rather than ignored:
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
A document type declaration, an entity declaration, any entity reference but the five XML predefines, a processing instruction, a CDATA section, a name outside ASCII, an undeclared prefix, a duplicate attribute. There is no external entity resolution to disable, because there is no entity resolution.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
An encrypted assertion, attribute or name identifier (\fBEncryptedAssertion\fR and friends). XML Encryption in a responder has been a decryption oracle more than once, and TLS already covers the hop the response takes. The refusal names itself, so a provider configured to encrypt is a clear message rather than a mystery.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
More than one assertion in a response, and two elements sharing an \fBID\fR. Those are the shapes signature wrapping needs.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
Any signature in the document that does not verify, including one nothing would have read.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
A signature whose single \fBReference\fR is not \fB#\fR plus the \fBID\fR of the element the signature is enveloped in; more than one reference; a transform other than the enveloped\-signature transform followed by exclusive canonicalization; a canonicalization other than \fBxml\-exc\-c14n#\fR; SHA\-1, HMAC or DSA.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
A response with no \fBInResponseTo\fR, so provider\-initiated ("unsolicited") single sign\-on is not supported: there is no state to bind it to.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
A bearer subject confirmation carrying \fBNotBefore\fR, a condition this profile does not understand, an attribute value wrapped in markup, a timestamp with no zone.
.RE
.PP
.PP
What it accepts, it accepts completely: the issuer, \fBDestination\fR, \fBInResponseTo\fR on both the envelope and the subject confirmation, the \fBRecipient\fR, the audience, both condition windows, the confirmation window, the provider\(aqs session bound, the status code, the name identifier format, and a one\-time check on the assertion identifier. The signing key comes from the configuration; \fBKeyInfo\fR in the document is not read at all, so a response signed by a key it carries is simply an unverifiable response.
.PP
.TS
