.PP
The session cookie carries only the attributes something names \[u2014] a header, a log field, a policy, a requirement, the groups \[u2014] because a cookie is four kilobytes and an assertion can carry far more. It is sealed under both entity identifiers, so two filters sharing one \fBcookie_secret_file\fR cannot open each other\(aqs sessions and a login through a lenient provider does not satisfy a stricter one.
.PP
The access log carries \fBsaml_user\fR for requests with a session and \fBflow: <name>:login\fR, \fBlogin_complete\fR, \fBlogout\fR or \fBmetadata\fR for the flow steps. Login and logout redirects are not security events; a refused response is, with reason \fB<filter name>\fR and a detail (\fBsignature\fR, \fBrefused\fR, \fBprofile\fR, \fBprovider_status\fR, \fBreplay\fR, \fBstate_missing\fR, \fBstate_invalid\fR, \fBrelay_state\fR, \fBattribute:<name>\fR), and counts towards ban triggers.
.PP
\fBSingle logout is not implemented, on purpose.\fR \fBlogout_path\fR clears the session at this proxy; signing out at the provider is the provider\(aqs own page. A SAML logout request arrives as a cross\-site POST or redirect carrying a name identifier, which is a way to sign other people out, and the response half needs a signed document sent \fIto\fR a provider \[u2014] a different set of machinery for a feature whose safe part (forgetting the session here) needs none of it. Sessions are short and the provider\(aqs own \fBSessionNotOnOrAfter\fR caps them.
.PP
\fBRelay state is not the binding.\fR The filter sends a digest of its own state cookie as \fBRelayState\fR and refuses a response that returns a different one, but the binding that matters is \fBInResponseTo\fR against the request identifier sealed in that cookie, checked on both the response element and the subject confirmation. A provider that drops \fBRelayState\fR entirely still works.
.SS "Kind \fBxml_guard\fR"
.PP
Decides whether an XML request body is one the application should see.
.PP
The WAF reads bodies as text and the \fBopenapi\fR filter validates JSON; between them sits every XML and SOAP API with neither. XML is also the format with the oldest and most reliable parser attacks, and all of them arrive the same way:
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
an \fBexternal entity\fR that reads a file off the machine (\fB<!ENTITY x SYSTEM "file:///etc/passwd">\fR) or makes a request from inside the network on the application\(aqs behalf;
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBentity expansion\fR \[u2014] the billion laughs \[u2014] that turns a kilobyte into gigabytes of heap inside the application\(aqs parser;
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBparameter entity\fR loops and external DTD fetches.
.RE
.PP
.PP
Every one of those needs a document type declaration or an entity reference in the body, and a gateway cannot know how the application\(aqs parser is configured \[u2014] the defaults of most XML libraries were unsafe for years. So this refuses the shapes those attacks need before that parser sees them, and names which shape it refused.
.PP
.TS
