T}
.TE
.PP
Default ladders (thresholds reached within the window): \fBlogin\fR delays 2s at 5 address, 3 account or 3 pair failures, challenges at 15 address, 5 account, 5 pair, 10 accounts per address, 5 addresses per account or 10 accounts per device, blocks 15m at 50 address, 20 account, 10 pair, 30 accounts per address, 20 addresses per account, 50 events or 30 accounts per device; \fBregister\fR delays 2s at 2 requests per address, challenges at 3 per address or device or 2 per identity, blocks 1h at 10 per address or device or 5 per identity; \fBreset\fR delays 2s at 3 per address or 2 per account, challenges at 5 per address or device or 3 per account, blocks 1h at 20 or 10; \fBcart\fR delays 1s at 30, challenges at 60 and blocks 30m at 150 requests per address, identity or device; \fBscrape\fR delays 1s at 200 requests or 100 distinct paths per address, challenges at 400 or 200 and blocks 1h at 1000. Tables are bounded per endpoint (65536 keys each, oldest evicted with a throttled warning). A delay holds a request slot, so keep \fBmax_delayed\fR under the route\(aqs concurrency.
.SS "Kind \fBapi_abuse\fR"
.PP
Watches what a caller does with an API rather than what it sends.
.PP
Every request in this sequence is valid on its own \[u2014] the right method, the right path, an authenticated caller, a well formed identifier \[u2014] and the attack is the sequence:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
GET /api/orders/1041   200
GET /api/orders/1042   403
GET /api/orders/1043   403
GET /api/orders/1044   200   <\- somebody else's order
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
That is broken object level authorisation, the first item on the OWASP API Security Top 10, and nothing that reads one request at a time can see it. What it is visible in is the shape of the sequence, per caller and per endpoint, over a window:
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.2i) lbx
lw(1.2i) lx.
T{
Signal
T}	T{
What was observed
T}
T{
\fBenumeration\fR
T}	T{
The caller touched more than \fBmax_\:objects\fR \fBdistinct\fR identifiers on one endpoint. A person reads their own orders;\: a script reads everybody\(aqs
T}
T{
\fBsequential\fR
T}	T{
The numeric identifiers are consecutive: \fBsequential.\:min\fR of them covering a span they fill to \fBsequential.\:density\fR. A catalogue read in order is a scrape,\: and that is a different fact from having read a lot
T}
T{
\fBrefused\fR
T}	T{
Of \fBrefused.\:min_\:requests\fR,\: at least \fBrefused.\:share\fR were answered 401,\: 403 or 404. Probing for objects that are not yours looks exactly like this and little else does
T}
.TE
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.2i) lbw(1.1i) lbw(0.8i) lbx
lw(1.2i) lw(1.1i) lw(0.8i) lx.
