T}
.TE
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
filters:
  \- name: abuse
    kind: api_abuse
    options:
      window: 5m
      max_objects: 200
      sequential: {min: 20, density: 0.8}
      refused: {min_requests: 20, share: 0.5}
      action: challenge
      paths: ["/api/"]
routes:
  \- name: api
    hosts: [api.example.com]
    upstream: api
    # After the identity filters, so a caller is an account rather than
    # an address.
    filters: [jwt\-auth, abuse]
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
What it counts, and what it does not:
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBA caller is the authenticated identity when the chain established one, and the client address otherwise.\fR An API abused through one account is one caller however many addresses it arrives from, which is why this filter belongs \fIafter\fR the identity filters in the chain.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBObjects, not requests.\fR A caller re\-reading its own order fifty times has touched one object. That is what keeps an ordinary page refresh out of the \fBsequential\fR signal, which counts distinct identifiers over the span they cover.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBAn endpoint is the method and the path template\fR, with identifiers folded out (\fBGET /api/orders/*\fR), so one busy endpoint never flags a caller on another. A path with no identifier in it \[u2014] a collection endpoint \[u2014] has no object to count.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBIdentifiers are held per (caller, endpoint) up to 1024\fR, and past that the count continues as a lower bound rather than the memory: \fBxproxy_api_abuse_objects\fR and \fBxproxy_api_abuse_overflowed\fR say which. An identifier longer than 128 characters is not counted at all.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBThe response is the other half of the probing signal\fR, so the filter reads both phases, and which request the action lands on follows from that. A bound crossed on the way in \[u2014] one object too many, a walk long enough to judge \[u2014] refuses \fIthat\fR request. The \fBrefused\fR signal is raised by the answer instead, so it applies from the caller\(aqs next request: the answer that revealed the probing has already been sent.
.RE
.PP
.PP
A flagged request adds \fBapi_abuse\fR to the access log with the signals that were raised; \fBblock\fR and \fBchallenge\fR also write a security event with the \fBapi_abuse\fR reason, which a ban trigger can name \[u2014] a caller that keeps walking after a refusal is one to stop at the edge rather than at the filter.
.PP
Per filter, \fBxproxy_api_abuse_requests_total\fR, \fB_flagged_total\fR, \fB_blocked_total\fR, \fB_challenged_total\fR and \fB_dropped_total\fR count what it did, and \fBxproxy_api_abuse_subjects\fR, \fBxproxy_api_abuse_objects\fR and \fBxproxy_api_abuse_overflowed\fR say how much it is holding: a \fBsubjects\fR gauge pinned at \fBmax_subjects\fR with \fB_dropped_total\fR climbing is a filter watching more callers than it was given room for.
.SS "Kind \fBapi_key\fR"
.PP
API keys with a life cycle: issued by \fBxproxyctl apikey add\fR (the plaintext \fBxpk_<id>_<secret>\fR is printed once; the file keeps a SHA\-256), scoped, expiring, rotated with a grace period for the previous secret (\fBapikey rotate \-grace 24h\fR) and revoked (\fBapikey revoke\fR, kept in the file so the id is never reused). The filter re\-reads the file when its contents change, at most every \fBreload\fR, and keeps the previous table when the new file does not parse.
.PP
.TS
