The only operation names that may run (\fBoperation\fR). It implies \fBrequire_\:operation_\:name\fR,\: because an anonymous operation is on no list and a list that let it through would be a list in name only. This is the strongest control here for a closed client set: the queries are known,\: so anything else is not a query this API serves
T}
T{
\fBpersisted\fR
T}	T{
\fBallow\fR,\: \fBdeny\fR
T}	T{
\fBallow\fR
T}	T{
What to do with a request that carries no query text \[u2014] a persisted query the origin looks up by hash. There is nothing to parse and nothing to measure,\: so every bound above walks past it. \fBallow\fR is the default because the origin only runs documents it already has,\: which is usually the safest thing a client can send;\: \fBdeny\fR is for a deployment whose API is reached through this filter and not otherwise,\: where an unreadable query should not be an allowed one (\fBpersisted\fR)
T}
.TE
.PP
\fBVariables are read.\fR A pagination argument given as a variable \[u2014] \fBfriends(first: $count)\fR with \fB{"count": 10}\fR in the request \[u2014] is a real number the server will use, and scoring it as \fBmax_list\fR refuses a query that costs nothing. That is how a complexity bound ends up switched off by the operator it kept annoying. A variable the request does not carry, or carries as something that is not a whole non\-negative number (a string, a null, a fraction, a negative), or that has a schema default this filter never sees, is still the worst case; and a literal larger than the variable still wins.
.SS "Kind \fBupload_guard\fR"
.PP
Inspects file uploads before the application stores them: multipart bodies (and, with \fBraw_uploads\fR, any other body of a write request) are buffered up to \fBmax_total_bytes\fR, every file part is checked and the body is replayed to the upstream unchanged. Checks, in order: file count, the form field, the file name (no path separators, control characters or traversal, at most \fBmax_filename_length\fR), the extension chain (\fBinvoice.pdf.exe\fR has two; a denied extension anywhere in the chain refuses, and with an allow list an unexpected extension before the last one, \fBphoto.html.jpg\fR, refuses too, while \fBreport.2024.pdf\fR passes), the size, the content (PE, ELF and Mach\-O images, \fB#!\fR scripts, PHP, JSP and ASP tags are refused whatever the name, unless \fBdeny_executables\fR is off), and the bytes against the extension\(aqs family and the declared media type (a PNG named \fB.jpg\fR, a PDF declared as an image). Denials answer 400, 413 or 415 with reason \fBupload\fR, a detail \fBcheck:filename\fR and a JSON body; the access log carries \fBupload_files\fR and \fBupload_bytes\fR. Malware scanning stays with ICAP.
.PP
.TS
