.SS "Kind \fBauthz\fR"
.PP
Decides what a verified identity may do.
.PP
Every authenticating filter here answers "who". None of them answers "what may they do", so each grew its own small allow list \[u2014] required scopes on the API key, a required group on the directory bind, required claims on the session \[u2014] and an allow list per filter is a policy nobody can read in one place. This reads the identity those filters verified and decides once, where the decision can be seen.
.PP
It decides nothing on its own authority. The subject, the groups, the scopes and the claims all come from a filter that verified them, so a header a client sent cannot reach a rule here. That also means it has to run \fBafter\fR the filters that authenticate: put it last in a route\(aqs \fBfilters\fR list.
.PP
.TS
