\fBlog\fR records and forwards,\: which is how a bound is tried out before it decides anything
T}
.TE
.PP
A length\-delimited protobuf field is a nested message or a string, and without a schema there is no way to be certain which. It is tried as a message first: a nested message read as a string is a subtree the depth and field bounds never see, and the bounds are the part that matters.
.PP
Denials carry the reason \fBgrpc_guard:\fR and one of \fBmessage_too_large\fR, \fBtoo_many_messages\fR, \fBshort_frame\fR, \fBcompressed\fR, \fBtoo_deep\fR, \fBtoo_many_fields\fR, \fBmalformed\fR or \fBcontent\fR; a content refusal names the field path it matched at. The access line carries \fBgrpc_messages\fR, \fBgrpc_depth\fR and \fBgrpc_fields\fR, so a route\(aqs shape is visible before anybody has to guess at a bound for it.
.PP
What this does not do is understand the fields. \fBdeny_patterns\fR run against every string in a message, not against a named field, so they are the blunt instrument they look like.
.SS "Kind \fBsensitive_data\fR"
.PP
Detects personal and secret data in requests and responses and, per direction, logs the findings, masks them or blocks the message. The request phase scans the query string (raw and decoded, plus parameter names that carry credentials), header values and the body; the response phase scans header values and the body. Bodies of a listed media type are buffered up to \fBmax_bytes\fR and scanned whole; \fBgzip\fR, \fBdeflate\fR, \fBbr\fR and \fBzstd\fR bodies are decoded first (up to \fBmax_decoded_bytes\fR); a body larger than that is streamed through the scanner as it flows, with 4 KiB held back between reads so a value split across two reads is still seen; an unlisted media type, an unknown encoding or a partial (ranged) body passes unscanned. A masked or streamed compressed body is forwarded decoded (the encoding header is removed); a streamed body loses its content length. Because the decoded bytes are what the other side receives, a streamed decode is also bounded by \fBmax_decompression_ratio\fR: past 8 MiB decoded, a body that keeps expanding beyond that multiple of its compressed size has its transfer cut, so a compression bomb small enough to pass \fBmax_body_bytes\fR cannot be amplified through the proxy. Blocking a streamed body cuts the transfer at the finding, since the head of the message has already been forwarded. Masking rewrites the value in place (\fB************1111\fR, \fBa***@example.com\fR, the first eight characters of a token) and updates \fBContent\-Length\fR; blocking answers \fBblock_status\fR with reason \fBsensitive_data\fR, a detail \fBresponse:card,email\fR and a JSON problem naming the kinds found, never the values. The access log carries \fBsensitive_types\fR, \fBsensitive_count\fR and \fBsensitive_where\fR for every message with a finding, in every mode; blocks count in \fBdenied_sensitive_data\fR, findings and message outcomes in the \fBxproxy_sensitive_*\fR metric families.
.PP
Built\-in detectors: \fBcard\fR (Luhn checked payment cards), \fBpersonnummer\fR (Swedish personal and coordination numbers with a valid date and checksum), \fBiban\fR (mod 97), \fBssn_us\fR, \fBemail\fR, \fBjwt\fR (three base64url parts with a JSON header), \fBprivate_key\fR (PEM headers), \fBapi_keys\fR (AWS, Google, GitHub, Slack, Stripe and GitLab formats) and \fBpassword_query\fR (query parameter names such as \fBpassword\fR, \fBtoken\fR or \fBapi_key\fR with a value; requests only).
.PP
.TS
