.PP
What is not guessed at:
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBAnother unit is left alone.\fR \fBRange: items=0\-9\fR is passed through: an origin that does not implement a unit ignores the header, and this proxy has nothing to say about a unit it cannot read.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBA header that is not a range set is dropped\fR, once, here \[u2014] a malformed value, a spec with no dash, a descending range, more specs than are worth reading (256). RFC 9110 says a recipient that cannot parse the header ignores it; deciding that in one place is what keeps this proxy and the origin reading the request the same way.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBA suffix range is kept as it is.\fR \fB\-500\fR means the last 500 bytes, and how that overlaps \fB0\-99\fR depends on a length this proxy does not know. The largest suffix covers the smaller ones; nothing else about them is assumed.
.RE
.PP
