\fBranges\fR and \fBranges_sent\fR appear in the access log for a request the policy acted on, a refusal is logged as \fBranges\fR, and \fBxproxy_ranges_total{result="dropped"|"refused"}\fR counts them (\fBranges_dropped\fR and \fBranges_refused\fR in \fBxproxyctl status\fR).
.SS "routes[].threat_intel"
.PP
\fBtrue\fR (default) or \fBfalse\fR: whether the imported \fBthreat_intel\fR lists apply to this route.
.PP
\fBfalse\fR exempts it. A list is an import, and an import with one wrong line in it must not take the health endpoint an operator watches the outage with, or the status page they diagnose it from. The ban list is a separate decision and still applies: a ban is this proxy\(aqs own finding about that client.
.SS "routes[].trailers"
.PP
\fBpass\fR (default) or \fBstrip\fR: the fields an upstream sends after the body \[u2014] a checksum, a signature, gRPC\(aqs status.
.PP
\fBstrip\fR removes both the announcement and the fields. An announced trailer with nothing behind it leaves a client waiting for a field that never arrives, so the two go together. It is refused on a gRPC route: gRPC carries its status in the trailers, and a route that strips them answers every call with no status at all.
.SH "maintenance"
.PP
Present means the maintenance gate is available; \fBenabled\fR is the state at start and the runtime toggle (\fBPOST /v1/maintenance\fR, \fBxproxyctl maintenance on|off\fR) overrides it and survives reloads. While it is on, every request is answered with \fBstatus\fR and \fBRetry\-After\fR except an allowlisted client and a route with \fBmaintenance: false\fR (health and status endpoints). The gate runs right after routing, before authentication, rate limits and filters.
.PP
