Selectors within a rule are AND, values within a selector are OR, and rules are tried in order. To capture one route for one client, put both selectors in one rule; to capture two unrelated things, write two rules.
.SH "mysql"
.PP
\fBkind: mysql\fR is a relay in front of a MySQL or MariaDB server.
.PP
It has the same four jobs as the postgres kind \-\- refuse the encryption downgrade, refuse the weak credentials, refuse what is not a statement, decide by the shape of what is \-\- plus two that are MySQL\(aqs alone.
.PP
\fBOn MySQL the dangerous operations are commands, not statements.\fR After the handshake every client message begins with a one\-octet command code, and several of those carry no SQL at all, so a statement policy would never see them. \fBCOM_SHUTDOWN\fR is one octet and stops the server. The three replication commands are a copy of every change to every database. \fBCOM_TABLE_DUMP\fR is a whole table. \fBCOM_PROCESS_KILL\fR ends somebody else\(aqs query. \fBCOM_DEBUG\fR writes the server\(aqs internals to its error log. \fBCOM_CREATE_DB\fR and \fBCOM_DROP_DB\fR predate the DDL statements and bypass a statement policy entirely. Hence \fBallow_commands\fR, which the postgres kind has no equivalent of.
.PP
Two commands are subtler and are why a capability policy alone is not enough. \fBCOM_CHANGE_USER\fR re\-authenticates a \fIlive\fR connection as somebody else, so a relay that did not read it would have a user policy that applied to the first message and nothing after it. And \fBCOM_SET_OPTION\fR turns \fBCLIENT_MULTI_STATEMENTS\fR on \fBafter the handshake is over\fR \-\- a policy a client lifts with one command, unless the relay reads it.
.PP
\fBThe relay rewrites the server\(aqs greeting.\fR MySQL\(aqs handshake runs the opposite way round from PostgreSQL\(aqs: the server speaks first and advertises its capabilities, and the client answers. So the relay reads the greeting, clears the bits \fBdeny_capabilities\fR names, and forwards the edited one \-\- a client that never sees \fBCLIENT_LOCAL_FILES\fR offered cannot negotiate it, so the server can never ask that client for a file, \fBand the application still works\fR. That is the same move the tftp kind makes with RFC 7440\(aqs window: rewrite rather than refuse, because a control that breaks every application on a segment is a control somebody switches off. The strip is logged as an \fBalert\fR rather than a refusal, since nothing was denied.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
\- name: app
