.SH "postgres"
.PP
\fBkind: postgres\fR is a relay in front of a PostgreSQL server that reads the frontend/backend protocol (the PostgreSQL manual, part IV chapter 55).
.PP
\fBIt is not a SQL firewall, and will not become one.\fR Knowing which tables a statement touches means parsing SQL properly \-\- every alias, subquery, CTE, view, function body and \fBsearch_path\fR interaction \-\- and a relay that got that 95% right would have a policy with a hole in exactly the place somebody is looking. Restricting a role\(aqs tables is the database\(aqs own job, done properly, with \fBGRANT\fR.
.PP
What a relay can do is everything that happens before the database has an opinion, two things it does better than the database, and one the database cannot do at all:
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBRefuse the encryption downgrade.\fR The protocol negotiates TLS in cleartext: the client sends eight octets asking, and the server answers with one unsigned octet, \fBS\fR or \fBN\fR. libpq\(aqs default \fBsslmode\fR is \fBprefer\fR, which means "ask for TLS and carry on in the clear if refused, without telling anybody" \-\- so the default configuration of the most widely deployed client in the world downgrades silently when something on the path rewrites that octet. The relay answers the request itself rather than forwarding it, and \fBrequire_tls\fR (on by default) refuses a client that will not encrypt at all.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBRefuse the weak authentication methods.\fR \fBpassword\fR is the password in cleartext. \fBmd5\fR is worse than it looks: the stored verifier is \fBmd5(password+username)\fR, so the hash \fIis\fR a password\-equivalent and anybody who reads \fBpg_authid\fR can authenticate without cracking anything. PostgreSQL has shipped SCRAM since version 10. \fBpg_hba.conf\fR can refuse both too, and on every estate that has been audited, it does not.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBRefuse what is not a statement at all\fR: a replication connection (a startup \fIparameter\fR, so no statement policy would ever see it), the legacy fast\-path function call, a cancel request from an address that has no business sending one.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBDecide by the shape of a statement.\fR An allow list of statement \fIkinds\fR, where a statement the classifier cannot name is refused. That is a much smaller claim than a SQL firewall and it holds.
.RE
.PP
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
