T}	T{
The rule\(aqs own narrowing. A rule that names a kind \fBwidens\fR the listener for its own traffic,\: which is what makes one listener serve a reporting account that may only select and a migration account that may also change the schema. The deny lists always win,\: on the rule and the listener both
T}
.TE
.SS "What shadow mode never shadows"
.PP
\fBmonitor_only\fR evaluates the policy and enforces nothing, with these exceptions \-\- forwarding any of them and writing it down is not a trial of anything:
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.3i) lbx
lw(1.3i) lx.
T{
Refusal
T}	T{
Why it is hard
T}
T{
\fBclient_\:not_\:allowed\fR
T}	T{
An address that may not connect
T}
T{
\fBtls_\:required\fR
T}	T{
The identity is \fIinside\fR the startup packet,\: so a packet that crossed in the clear has already disclosed the role and database
T}
T{
\fBweak_\:auth\fR,\: \fBauth_\:not_\:allowed\fR
T}	T{
A credential an observer can reuse is disclosed by being sent
T}
T{
\fBreplication_\:not_\:allowed\fR
T}	T{
The connection becomes a copy of the whole server
T}
T{
\fBstatement_\:unreadable\fR
T}	T{
The classifier could not name the statement,\: so the relay has no opinion to observe
T}
T{
\fBcopy_\:program\fR
T}	T{
\fBCOPY .\:.\:.\: FROM PROGRAM\fR runs a shell command as the server\(aqs operating\-system user
T}
T{
\fBfunction_\:call\fR
T}	T{
The fast\-path interface bypasses the parser
T}
T{
\fBstatement_\:too_\:long\fR
T}	T{
A bound
T}
.TE
.SS "How a statement is classified"
.PP
A deny list of strings is a list of the spellings somebody thought of: \fBDROP\fR does not stop \fBDR/**/OP\fR, and a statement that merely \fImentions\fR the word in a string literal is innocent. So the classifier reads the leading keyword of every statement after stripping comments and quoting properly \-\- PostgreSQL\(aqs block comments nest, and dollar\-quoted strings have no escaping at all \-\- and anything it cannot name becomes \fBunknown\fR, which is refused. The failure mode of a spelling nobody thought of is a refusal rather than a pass.
.PP
Three cases it is deliberately conservative about, because when a classifier must be wrong it must be wrong towards the more restricted answer:
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(0.8i) lbw(1.1i) lbx
lw(0.8i) lw(1.1i) lx.
T{
Written
T}	T{
Classified as
T}	T{
Why
T}
T{
\fBWITH x AS (DELETE FROM t .\:.\:.\:) SELECT .\:.\:.\fR
T}	T{
\fBdelete\fR
T}	T{
A data\-modifying CTE is a write wearing a SELECT\(aqs leading keyword
T}
T{
\fBEXPLAIN ANALYZE INSERT .\:.\:.\fR
T}	T{
\fBinsert\fR
T}	T{
\fBANALYZE\fR executes the statement it explains. Plain \fBEXPLAIN\fR does not,\: and is \fBexplain\fR
T}
T{
\fBCOPY t FROM PROGRAM \(aq.\:.\:.\:\(aq\fR
T}	T{
\fBcopy\fR + program
T}	T{
Three operations share one keyword and one of them is remote code execution
T}
.TE
.PP
Text that cannot be lexed at all \-\- an unterminated quote, comment or dollar quote \-\- is refused rather than classified, because the relay and the server would disagree about where the statement ends, and disagreeing about that is how a statement gets past a relay that read a different one.
.SH "tds"
.PP
\fBkind: tds\fR is a relay in front of a Microsoft SQL Server, or anything else speaking TDS.
.PP
It has the postgres kind\(aqs four jobs \-\- refuse the encryption downgrade, refuse the credential that crosses in the clear, refuse what is not a statement, decide by the shape of what is \-\- and two that belong to SQL Server. Like the other two, \fBit is not a SQL firewall and will not become one\fR: restricting a login\(aqs tables is the database\(aqs job, with \fBGRANT\fR.
.PP
\fBThe password is an encoding, not a secret.\fR A \fBLOGIN7\fR carries the password with its nibbles swapped and XORed with 0xA5. There is no key. Anybody who read the packet has the password, so \fBrequire_tls\fR here is not hardening \-\- it is the difference between a reusable credential on the wire and none. There is no equivalent of \fBallow_weak_auth\fR on this kind because there is no stronger method to contrast one with, short of integrated security; the only credential setting is \fBallow_cleartext_password\fR, named for exactly what it permits.
.PP
And the negotiation that decides whether the connection is encrypted is one octet in a \fBPRELOGIN\fR option table, answered by the server, signed by nothing:
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.0i) lbw(0.9i) lbx
lw(1.0i) lw(0.9i) lx.
T{
Value
T}	T{
Means
T}	T{
On the wire
T}
T{
\fBoff\fR (0x00)
T}	T{
"I would rather not"
T}	T{
plaintext \-\- and what a great deal of deployed software sends
T}
T{
\fBon\fR (0x01)
T}	T{
"let us"
T}	T{
encrypted
T}
T{
\fBnot_\:supported\fR (0x02)
T}	T{
"I cannot"
T}	T{
plaintext \-\- and what something on the path rewrites the \fIserver\(aqs\fR answer to
T}
T{
\fBrequired\fR (0x03)
T}	T{
"I will not continue without it"
T}	T{
encrypted
T}
.TE
.PP
\fBoff\fR reads like a preference and \fBnot_supported\fR reads like a capability, and their wire consequence is identical. A client that asked for \fBoff\fR and hears \fBnot_supported\fR proceeds in the clear without complaint, which is the third time this shape has appeared after PostgreSQL\(aqs \fBSSLRequest\fR octet and MySQL\(aqs \fBCLIENT_SSL\fR bit \-\- and it gets the same answer. The relay does not forward the server\(aqs value: with \fBrequire_tls\fR on it answers every client \fBrequired\fR, so every client becomes one the downgrade cannot touch, and it negotiates the leg to the server separately. Each connection whose negotiation it raised is logged as \fBtds_encryption_forced\fR, which is the list to read \fBbefore\fR turning the setting on rather than after: every client on it is one that would have gone in the clear, and one that will fail if it turns out not to speak TLS at all.
.PP
\fBThe dangerous operations are procedures.\fR Not statements, and not one\-octet commands as on MySQL. \fBxp_cmdshell\fR is a shell command running as the service account; the \fBsp_OA\fR family instantiates arbitrary COM objects, which is the same thing with more steps; the registry procedures read and write the host\(aqs configuration; \fBsp_addlinkedserver\fR turns one compromised database into a route to another; \fBsp_configure\fR is how \fBxp_cmdshell\fR gets turned back on after somebody disabled it. To a statement classifier every one of those is an \fBEXECUTE\fR, and a statement policy strict enough to catch them would refuse every stored procedure in the estate. Hence \fBallow_procedures\fR, which defaults to what a client library calls \-\- the dynamic\-SQL family, the cursor family, and the metadata calls JDBC and ODBC make to describe a result set \-\- and nothing else.
.PP
\fBThe statement an application runs is not a statement.\fR Every client library that uses parameters sends \fBsp_executesql\fR with the SQL in a parameter, so a relay that classified only \fBSQLBATCH\fR would be inspecting the \fBSET\fR statements a driver emits on connect and nothing an application ever runs. The relay reads that one parameter \-\- and only that one, on the six procedures whose documented signature has a statement in it, because the rest are the caller\(aqs data and a relay that held them would put one in a log line \-\- and applies the same statement policy to it as to a batch. A dynamic\-SQL call whose statement it cannot read is refused: it is the one message on this protocol that carries arbitrary SQL.
.PP
One structural oddity, which needs no configuration but explains the code: \fBthe TLS handshake runs inside TDS packets and then stops.\fR For the length of the handshake TDS wraps TLS; afterwards TLS wraps TDS. The nesting inverts once, part way through a connection.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
\- name: app
  address: "10.0.0.30:1433"
  kind: tds
  tls:
    certificates: [{cert_file: /etc/xproxy/tls/db.pem, key_file: /etc/xproxy/tls/db\-key.pem}]
  tds:
    upstream: sql
    allow_clients: ["10.0.2.0/24"]
    allow_users: [svc_sales]
    allow_databases: [sales]
    allow_statements: [select, insert, update, delete, set, begin, commit, rollback]
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
.TS
