T}
T{
\fBtls_\:required\fR,\: \fBupstream_\:no_\:tls\fR
T}	T{
The login name,\: the database and a recoverable password are inside the LOGIN7
T}
T{
\fBcleartext_\:password\fR
T}	T{
Anybody who read the packet has the password;\: there is nothing left to observe
T}
T{
\fBintegrated_\:not_\:allowed\fR,\: \fBno_\:user\fR
T}	T{
A login no user list can be applied to. Admitting it would be a user policy with a hole,\: noted and permitted
T}
T{
\fBprocedure_\:denied\fR,\: \fBprocedure_\:not_\:allowed\fR for a procedure in the dangerous set
T}	T{
Forwarding a shell command,\: a COM object,\: a registry write or a linked server and writing down that it was noticed is not a trial of a policy
T}
T{
\fBlegacy_\:login\fR
T}	T{
The pre\-TDS7 login is a message shape the relay does not read,\: and forwarding octets it has not understood is what this kind exists not to do
T}
T{
\fBbatch_\:unreadable\fR,\: \fBrpc_\:unreadable\fR,\: \fBstatement_\:unreadable\fR
T}	T{
The relay has no opinion to observe
T}
T{
\fBstatement_\:too_\:long\fR
T}	T{
A bound
T}
.TE
.PP
The set whose refusal is never shadowed is \fBxp_cmdshell\fR, the OLE automation family, the registry procedures, \fBsp_addextendedproc\fR, \fBsp_addlinkedserver\fR, \fBsp_serveroption\fR, \fBsp_configure\fR, \fBxp_servicecontrol\fR, the mail procedures, the filesystem procedures, the SQL Agent job procedures and the role\-membership procedures. A procedure that is merely \fInot on the allow list\fR is a soft refusal, because it is most likely an application nobody has listed yet \-\- which is what monitor mode is for. An operator who names one of the hard set in \fBallow_procedures\fR has said so, and is not overruled.
.PP
Forcing the encryption negotiation upward is not in the table either, because it is not a refusal: nothing is denied, the connection goes through, and it happens in shadow mode too. It is logged as an \fBalert\fR.
.SH "redis"
.PP
\fBkind: redis\fR is a relay in front of a Redis or Valkey server.
.PP
\fBIt is the protocol in this project where a relay earns its place fastest\fR, and the reason is Redis\(aqs own default: no password. An instance with \fBrequirepass\fR unset accepts every command from anybody who can reach the port, so an instance that is \fIreachable\fR is an instance that is \fIadministrable\fR. And on this protocol the distance between an administrative command and remote code execution is one command:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
CONFIG SET dir /var/spool/cron
CONFIG SET dbfilename root
SAVE
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
writes a file of the attacker\(aqs choosing wherever the server can write. Pointed at a cron directory or an \fBauthorized_keys\fR it is a shell, and it has been used that way for a decade.
.PP
There is also almost nothing for a relay to reason about. A command is an array of opaque byte strings: no schema, no statement grammar, nothing to classify by shape the way the three SQL kinds classify a statement. So the policy is built on four things, in the order they are decided:
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBAuthentication, before anything else.\fR \fBrequire_auth\fR refuses a command that arrives before the connection has authenticated, and the answer is taken from the \fIserver\(aqs\fR reply rather than from the relay having seen an \fBAUTH\fR \-\- a relay that trusted the attempt would treat a wrong password as a login. What a client may legitimately send first is named rather than guessed (\fBAUTH\fR, \fBHELLO\fR, \fBPING\fR, \fBQUIT\fR, \fBRESET\fR, \fBCOMMAND\fR), because the alternative \-\- letting everything through until an \fB+OK\fR arrives \-\- is a window an attacker fills with one command.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBThe command name, as an allow list\fR, defaulting to what an application does to a cache. The absences are the value; the table below says what each one is.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBThe subcommand, where it decides.\fR \fBCONFIG GET\fR is a read and \fBCONFIG SET\fR is the paragraph above, so \fBallow_subcommands\fR exists to allow the one and not the other. A container command allowed with no subcommand listed allows all of them.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBThe key prefix\fR, which is the closest this protocol has to the database\-and\-table boundary the SQL kinds leave to \fBGRANT\fR.
.RE
.PP
.SS "The key prefix policy, and the commands it refuses"
.PP
A command\(aqs keys sit where its own signature puts them, and the relay reads them from a table of 202 commands \-\- including the interleaved ones (\fBMSET\fR is key, value, key, value) and the ones whose last argument is a timeout rather than a key (\fBBLPOP\fR). Fifteen more declare their key count in an argument (\fBEVAL\fR, \fBLMPOP\fR, \fBSINTERCARD\fR), and that count is read rather than assumed: it is the client\(aqs number and it decides which arguments are keys, so a relay that assumed a position would check a prefix policy against a Lua script\(aqs text.
.PP
Ten commands have key positions that depend on an option that may or may not be present, and while a prefix policy is in force \fBthose are refused\fR with \fBkey_position_unknown\fR rather than checked against a guess:
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.8i) lbx
lw(1.8i) lx.
T{
Command
T}	T{
Why its keys cannot be located
T}
T{
\fBXREAD\fR,\: \fBXREADGROUP\fR
T}	T{
the keys follow a \fBSTREAMS\fR token whose position depends on \fBCOUNT\fR,\: \fBBLOCK\fR,\: \fBGROUP\fR and \fBNOACK\fR,\: and after it the streams and their identifiers are two halves rather than alternating pairs
T}
T{
\fBSORT\fR,\: \fBSORT_\:RO\fR
T}	T{
a \fBSTORE\fR option adds a destination key at the end,\: and \fBBY\fR and \fBGET\fR take key \fIpatterns\fR rather than keys
T}
T{
\fBGEORADIUS\fR,\: \fBGEORADIUSBYMEMBER\fR
T}	T{
\fBSTORE\fR and \fBSTOREDIST\fR each add a key at the end. The \fB_\:RO\fR variants have neither,\: so they are locatable and are not on this list
T}
T{
\fBZUNIONSTORE\fR,\: \fBZINTERSTORE\fR,\: \fBZDIFFSTORE\fR
T}	T{
a destination,\: then a \fBnumkeys\fR count,\: then that many source keys: two different mechanisms in one command
T}
T{
\fBMIGRATE\fR
T}	T{
the key is the third argument \-\- unless \fBKEYS\fR is used,\: in which case the third is an empty string and the keys are at the end
T}
.TE
.PP
Refusing them is the honest answer and the refusal is hard, because checking some other argument instead would be a prefix policy that passes exactly what it was meant to stop, and it would do it silently. With no prefix policy set, all ten are ordinary commands.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
\- name: cache
  address: "10.0.0.40:6379"
  kind: redis
  tls:
    certificates: [{cert_file: /etc/xproxy/tls/db.pem, key_file: /etc/xproxy/tls/db\-key.pem}]
  redis:
    upstream: rd
    allow_clients: ["10.0.2.0/24"]
    allow_key_prefixes: ["app:", "session:"]
    read_only: false
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
.TS
