T{
Refusal
T}	T{
Why it is hard
T}
T{
\fBclient_\:not_\:allowed\fR
T}	T{
An address that may not connect
T}
T{
\fBtls_\:required\fR
T}	T{
An \fBAUTH\fR on this connection puts the password on the wire as an ordinary command argument,\: and so does every value after it
T}
T{
\fBnot_\:authenticated\fR
T}	T{
Forwarding an unauthenticated command means the command ran. In front of a server whose \fBrequirepass\fR is unset,\: that is the whole of what the setting was for
T}
T{
\fBunreadable_\:command\fR
T}	T{
The relay has no opinion to observe
T}
T{
\fBkey_\:position_\:unknown\fR
T}	T{
Checking the wrong argument against a prefix list is a policy that passes what it was meant to stop
T}
T{
\fBtoo_\:many_\:commands\fR
T}	T{
A bound
T}
T{
any refusal of a command in the table above
T}	T{
Forwarding a \fBCONFIG SET\fR,\: an \fBEVAL\fR or a \fBREPLICAOF\fR and writing down that it was noticed is not a trial of a policy. \fBKEYS\fR is in the set on different grounds: a monitor\-mode listener that forwarded one would cause the outage it was installed to prevent
T}
.TE
.PP
A command merely \fIoff\fR the allow list is a \fBsoft\fR refusal, which is the distinction that makes monitor mode useful: it is most likely an application nobody has listed yet, and finding those is what the mode is for. An operator who names one of the hard set in \fBallow_commands\fR has said so, and is not overruled.
.SH "bacnet"
.PP
\fBkind: bacnet\fR is a relay in front of a building. The controllers behind it hold the setpoints for air handling, chillers, boilers, lighting, lifts, access control, smoke control and the pressurisation in an operating theatre, and a great many of them have been in a ceiling void since before the estate had a security team.
.PP
\fBThere is no identity in this protocol.\fR No user, no session, no password that means anything, and no transport security. Clause 24\(aqs \fBauthenticate\fR service was withdrawn from the standard; the network security of the same clause \-\- the challenge, the wrapped payloads, the key distribution \-\- is implemented by almost nothing in the field. A device answers whoever asks it. So this listener decides about a datagram from three things and no others: the address it came from, the service it asks for, and the object and property it names.
.PP
Three properties of the protocol shape every control below.
.PP
\fBWriting is a service, not a mode.\fR \fBreadProperty\fR and \fBwriteProperty\fR are different service choices in the same request shape, so the difference between reading a zone temperature and setting it is one octet. \fBreinitializeDevice\fR restarts a controller and \fBdeviceCommunicationControl\fR tells one to stop talking for a while; both take an optional password that is sent in the clear and that most devices leave unset. An empty \fBservices\fR list therefore allows reading, discovery and the notifications a device sends of its own accord, and nothing that changes anything.
.PP
\fBWriting has a priority, and the priority is the privilege.\fR A commandable object \-\- an analogue output, a binary output, a lighting output, a lift \-\- holds sixteen command slots, and the plant follows the highest\-priority slot that is filled. Slots 1 and 2 are manual and automatic life safety: a value written there cannot be overridden by the management system, by a schedule, or by an operator at a workstation, and it stands until whoever wrote it relinquishes it. \fBmax_command_priority\fR is the bound, it defaults to 8, and a request above it is refused \fIhard\fR \-\- the refusal stands in monitor mode, because a relay that shadowed this one would be watching somebody take a piece of plant.
.PP
\fBIt is broadcast, and it amplifies.\fR \fBwho\-Is\fR is a broadcast that every device answers with an \fBi\-Am\fR. A BBMD \-\- a broadcast management device \-\- forwards broadcasts between subnets, and its foreign\-device registration lets a host ask to be sent every broadcast on a network it is not even on, from a single unauthenticated datagram. \fBForwarded\-NPDU\fR carries the address a message came from \fIinside the payload\fR, where whoever sent the datagram chose it. So \fBallow_broadcast\fR, \fBallow_bbmd\fR and \fBallow_forwarded\fR all default to false, \fBmax_broadcast_replies\fR bounds the answers one broadcast brings back, and a \fBForwarded\-NPDU\fR whose claimed origin is not the address it arrived from is refused.
.PP
.TS
