.TE
.PP
The refusals that are never shadowed are the bounds: \fBcommand_priority_too_high\fR, \fBwhois_unbounded\fR, \fBwhois_range_too_wide\fR and \fBrate_limited\fR. Everything else is a policy choice, and monitor mode is for finding out what an estate actually sends before refusing any of it.
.SS "Pairing an answer with its question"
.PP
The standard makes an invoke identifier unique only between one client and one device. This relay speaks to the building from a socket of its own, so two clients that both use identifier 1 towards the same controller would be indistinguishable on the way back, and one client\(aqs answer would be delivered to the other. So the relay allocates an identifier of its own towards the device and translates it back on the answer, which is what a BACnet router does for the same reason. There are two hundred and fifty\-six of them; when all are outstanding a request is refused (\fBtoo_many_pending\fR) rather than reusing one, because reusing one delivers somebody else\(aqs answer.
.PP
An unconfirmed request has nothing to pair with at all \-\- an \fBi\-Am\fR is sent of the device\(aqs own accord \-\- so those answers are matched to the clients that broadcast inside \fBbroadcast_reply_window\fR and bounded by \fBmax_broadcast_replies\fR. An answer arriving outside anybody\(aqs window is not forwarded: that bound is this protocol\(aqs amplification control, not a convenience. A virtual link layer request \-\- a table read, a registration \-\- gets a window of exactly one reply, since that is what it expects.
.PP
A segmented exchange is translated in \fBboth\fR directions. The reply\(aqs segments carry the identifier this relay chose; the client\(aqs segment acknowledgements, and any abort or error it sends about its own request, carry the one the \fIclient\fR chose \-\- so those are translated back the other way, and one naming an exchange this relay is not holding is refused (\fBno_such_exchange\fR) rather than forwarded. A segment also renews the exchange\(aqs deadline, because a segmented reply is one exchange however many datagrams it takes and a deadline measured from the request would expire in the middle of a long trend log download.
.PP
\fBservices\fR applies to the building\(aqs direction too, because it is a statement about which services cross this listener rather than about which a client may send. The one that matters is \fBtimeSynchronization\fR: a device, or something on the plant network wearing a device\(aqs address, broadcasting one at a client network sets the clock on every host that listens, and it is not on the default list in either direction. The object and property rules are \fInot\fR applied to the building\(aqs direction \-\- they are written about the objects a client may reach, and applying them backwards would refuse every \fBi\-Am\fR, which names a device object.
.SH "amqp"
.PP
\fBkind: amqp\fR is a relay in front of a message broker. It is the only listener here that reads \fBtwo protocols on one port\fR, because AMQP is two protocols: a client picks one in its first eight octets.
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBAMQP 0\-9\-1\fR is what RabbitMQ speaks and what almost every deployment means by AMQP. A frame protocol with a class\-and\-method catalogue: declaring an exchange, binding a queue, publishing, consuming and deleting are each a method frame with typed arguments.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
\fBAMQP 1.0\fR (ISO/IEC 19464) is a different protocol that kept the name. Nine performatives over a self\-describing type system, where the thing being authorised is the \fIaddress a link attaches to\fR and everything after it is a handle.
.RE
.PP
.PP
Both are read, and one policy decides both: the nouns are an exchange, a queue and a routing key on 0\-9\-1, and a link address on 1.0, which the brokers that serve both versions spell \fB/exchange/X/key\fR and \fB/queue/Q\fR.
.SS "Why a relay in front of a broker"
.PP
A broker is where an estate\(aqs data is in transit \-\- orders, payments, telemetry, the events that drive everything else \-\- and four things about it are worth a listener.
.PP
\fBA broker\(aqs permissions are per user and per virtual host.\fR RabbitMQ\(aqs model is three regular expressions (configure, write, read) for each user in each vhost: more than most brokers offer, administered inside the broker, and outside the estate\(aqs own review. This holds the same boundary in the configuration that is reviewed with everything else, and holds it in front of brokers whose model is weaker.
.PP
\fBTopology is not work.\fR Declaring an exchange, deleting a queue, binding, unbinding and purging are the broker\(aqs \fIconfiguration\fR, and an application that publishes to an exchange somebody else declared needs none of them. So \fBallow_topology\fR is false by default, and a client library that declares its own queue on connect becomes a decision an operator makes rather than a default nobody noticed.
.PP
\fBThe credential is in the clear.\fR Both versions authenticate with SASL, and PLAIN \-\- what every deployment uses \-\- is the username and the password in one field separated by zero octets. \fBrequire_tls\fR is therefore the setting that matters most. The relay reads the \fIusername\fR out of the exchange for its rules and its logs, and never the password: no code path between the wire and a log line holds a broker credential.
.PP
\fBThe dangerous arguments are not the obvious ones.\fR These four name something a policy written against the obvious fields would miss:
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.1i) lbw(0.9i) lbx
lw(1.1i) lw(0.9i) lx.
T{
Where
T}	T{
What it names
T}	T{
Why it matters
T}
T{
\fBx\-\:dead\-\:letter\-\:exchange\fR on \fBqueue.\:declare\fR
T}	T{
an exchange
T}	T{
where this queue\(aqs rejected and expired messages go. A client that may not publish to an exchange can have the broker deliver to it
T}
T{
\fBalternate\-\:exchange\fR on \fBexchange.\:declare\fR
T}	T{
an exchange
T}	T{
where this exchange sends what it could not route
T}
