\fBreply\-\:to\fR in a message\(aqs properties
T}	T{
a queue
T}	T{
where a request\-reply service will send its answer,\: named inside the message rather than in the publish
T}
T{
\fB/\:exchange/\:X/\:key\fR as a 1.0 link address
T}	T{
an exchange and a routing key
T}	T{
the same boundary in the other protocol\(aqs vocabulary
T}
.TE
.PP
All four are checked against the same \fBallow_exchanges\fR, \fBallow_queues\fR and \fBallow_routing_keys\fR as the fields that carry them.
.SS "The pattern language"
.PP
The name lists are written in the protocol\(aqs own topic language, because that is the one an operator already knows from writing bindings. A name is words separated by dots:
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(0.8i) lbw(1.1i) lbx
lw(0.8i) lw(1.1i) lx.
T{
Pattern
T}	T{
Matches
T}	T{
Does not match
T}
T{
\fBorders\fR
T}	T{
\fBorders\fR
T}	T{
\fBorders.\:created\fR
T}
T{
\fBorders.\:*\fR
T}	T{
\fBorders.\:created\fR,\: \fBorders.\:paid\fR
T}	T{
\fBorders\fR,\: \fBorders.\:eu.\:created\fR
T}
T{
\fBorders.\:#\fR
T}	T{
\fBorders\fR,\: \fBorders.\:created\fR,\: \fBorders.\:eu.\:created\fR
T}	T{
\fBpayroll.\:created\fR
T}
T{
\fB#\fR
T}	T{
everything
T}	T{

T}
T{
\fBsvc\-\:*\fR
T}	T{
\fBsvc\-\:a\fR,\: \fBsvc\-\:billing\fR
T}	T{
\fBsvc\-\:a.\:internal\fR
T}
T{
\fBapp\-\:?\fR
T}	T{
\fBapp\-\:1\fR
T}	T{
\fBapp\-\:12\fR
T}
.TE
.PP
\fB*\fR is exactly one word and \fB#\fR is zero or more, as in a binding. Inside a word an ordinary shell glob applies, which is what the last two rows are. A name with no metacharacter matches itself, which is what most of these lists hold \-\- and the \fBdefault exchange is named by the empty string\fR, so a policy that means to allow publishing to a queue by name has to write \fB""\fR in \fBallow_exchanges\fR deliberately.
.PP
