Refuse every operation until the \fBbroker\fR has accepted a credential. The outcome is the broker\(aqs answer and not the client\(aqs claim: a 0\-9\-1 broker that refuses a password closes the connection instead of sending \fBconnection.\:tune\fR,\: so tune is the answer;\: on 1.0 it is the \fBsasl\-\:outcome\fR
T}
T{
\fBallow_\:users\fR,\: \fBdeny_\:users\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
any
T}	T{
The identities a connection may authenticate \fIas\fR,\: read out of the SASL exchange. The broker decides whether the password is right;\: this decides which names may be tried
T}
T{
\fBallow_\:vhosts\fR,\: \fBdeny_\:vhosts\fR
T}	T{
list of pattern
T}	T{
any
T}	T{
The virtual hosts a connection may open: \fBconnection.\:open\fR\(aqs virtual\-host on 0\-9\-1,\: \fBopen\fR\(aqs hostname on 1.0. Decided before the exchange and queue lists,\: because a name means something different in each vhost
T}
T{
\fBallow_\:methods\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
the application set
T}	T{
0\-9\-1 method names,\: spelled \fBbasic.\:publish\fR,\: \fBqueue.\:declare\fR. Empty allows the handshake,\: the channel methods,\: publishing,\: consuming,\: acknowledging,\: publisher confirms and transactions \-\- and no topology method at all
T}
T{
\fBdeny_\:methods\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
\fB[]\fR
T}	T{
The deny list,\: which no rule can override
T}
T{
\fBallow_\:performatives\fR,\: \fBdeny_\:performatives\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
all nine
T}	T{
The same for AMQP 1.0,\: spelled \fBattach\fR,\: \fBtransfer\fR,\: \fBflow\fR. Empty allows all of them,\: because on that version the policy is about the address a link attaches to rather than about which performative carries it
T}
T{
\fBallow_\:topology\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
Permit the methods that change the broker\(aqs configuration: declare,\: delete,\: bind,\: unbind and purge
T}
T{
\fBallow_\:publish\fR,\: \fBallow_\:consume\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBtrue\fR
T}	T{
Putting messages in and taking them out. A listener in front of a broker that only ingests events sets consume false;\: one in front of a read model sets publish false. On 1.0 the same two decide an \fBattach\fR,\: because a sender is publishing and a receiver is consuming
T}
T{
\fBallow_\:exchanges\fR,\: \fBdeny_\:exchanges\fR
T}	T{
list of pattern
T}	T{
any
T}	T{
Exchange names. Checked wherever an exchange is named,\: including the two arguments in the table above
T}
T{
\fBallow_\:queues\fR,\: \fBdeny_\:queues\fR
T}	T{
list of pattern
T}	T{
any
T}	T{
Queue names,\: including a message\(aqs \fBreply\-\:to\fR
T}
T{
\fBallow_\:routing_\:keys\fR,\: \fBdeny_\:routing_\:keys\fR
T}	T{
list of pattern
T}	T{
any
T}	T{
Routing keys
T}
T{
\fBallow_\:addresses\fR,\: \fBdeny_\:addresses\fR
T}	T{
list of pattern
T}	T{
any
T}	T{
1.0 link addresses. A listener that names exchanges and queues already covers the addresses whose shape says which they are;\: these are for the node names that have no shape,\: which is what Azure Service Bus and Qpid use
T}
T{
\fBdeny_\:management_\:nodes\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBtrue\fR
T}	T{
Refuse the names a broker keeps for administering itself: \fB$management\fR and \fB$cbs\fR on 1.0,\: and the \fBamq.\:rabbitmq.\:*\fR exchanges on 0\-9\-1 \-\- the log stream,\: the trace stream and the event stream. An operator who needs one names it in \fBallow_\:exchanges\fR or \fBallow_\:addresses\fR
T}
T{
\fBrequire_\:user_\:id\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
Refuse a published message with no \fBuser\-\:id\fR property. It is the one field that ties a message to a person,\: and nothing makes a publisher set it
T}
T{
\fBmatch_\:user_\:id\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBtrue\fR
T}	T{
Refuse a message whose \fBuser\-\:id\fR is not this connection\(aqs identity. Costs nothing when nobody sets the property
T}
T{
\fBallow_\:no_\:ack\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBtrue\fR
T}	T{
Permit \fBbasic.\:consume\fR with \fBno\-\:ack\fR,\: which takes messages off a queue without acknowledging them: whatever was in flight when the consumer died is gone
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:priority\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
unbounded
T}	T{
Bound on a message\(aqs \fBpriority\fR property. A priority queue serves the highest first,\: so a publisher that sets the maximum on everything starves the others
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:frame_\:bytes\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB131072\fR
T}	T{
One frame,\: and with it the \fBframe\-\:max\fR the two sides negotiate. A negotiation that settles above this is \fBrefused rather than rewritten\fR: rewriting it would make the relay a party to the negotiation,\: and a connection that agreed a frame size and then had a frame refused mid\-message is a harder fault to find than one that failed at the start
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:channels\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB256\fR
T}	T{
Channels per connection (sessions,\: on 1.0),\: enforced by \fBcounting the channels that are opened\fR rather than by refusing the negotiation: a broker\(aqs own default channel\-max is in the thousands and every client echoes it,\: so refusing that would refuse every ordinary connection
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:links\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB256\fR
T}	T{
Links per 1.0 session
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:message_\:bytes\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
unbounded
T}	T{
One message. On 0\-9\-1 it is checked against the size the \fBcontent header declares\fR,\: before the body arrives;\: on 1.0 it is the sum over a run of transfers,\: because a message may be split across them
T}
T{
\fBrequire_\:heartbeat\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
Refuse a connection that negotiated no heartbeat. One with none holds the broker\(aqs resources until the kernel notices the socket is gone,\: which can be hours \-\- and off by default because a client behind something that keeps the socket open is not doing anything wrong
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:methods\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
unbounded
T}	T{
Methods or performatives per connection. Off by default because a broker connection is long\-lived;\: it is here for a bastion front where a session is a person
T}
T{
\fBrate_\:limit\fR,\: \fBrate_\:burst\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
off
T}	T{
Methods per second per client address
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:sessions\fR,\: \fBmax_\:sessions_\:per_\:client\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
unbounded
T}	T{
Concurrent connections
T}
T{
\fBidle_\:timeout\fR,\: \fBsession_\:duration\fR,\: \fBhandshake_\:timeout\fR
T}	T{
duration
T}	T{
\fB0\fR,\: \fB0\fR,\: \fB30s\fR
T}	T{

T}
T{
\fBdefault_\:action\fR
T}	T{
\fBallow\fR,\: \fBdeny\fR
T}	T{
\fBdeny\fR
T}	T{
When no rule matched
T}
T{
\fBdeny_\:response\fR
T}	T{
\fBclose\fR,\: \fBdrop\fR
T}	T{
\fBclose\fR
T}	T{
\fBclose\fR sends the protocol\(aqs own statement \-\- a \fBconnection.\:close\fR with reply code 403 on 0\-9\-1,\: a \fBclose\fR carrying \fBamqp:\:unauthorized\-\:access\fR on 1.0 \-\- so a client library reports a refusal rather than a dropped socket
T}
T{
\fBlog_\:methods\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
An access line per method,\: which on a busy broker is a great many lines
T}
T{
\fBalert_\:on_\:deny\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBtrue\fR
T}	T{
A security event for every refusal
T}
T{
\fBmonitor_\:only\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
Evaluate and do not enforce,\: except the hard decisions below
T}
.TE
.SS "rules[]"
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.5i) lbw(0.8i) lbx
lw(1.5i) lw(0.8i) lx.
