.TE
.SS "A refusal ends the connection"
.PP
On the other relay kinds a refused operation is answered and the session continues. Here it is not, and the reason is the protocol: AMQP is stateful in both directions, so dropping one frame out of a conversation leaves the two sides disagreeing about what happened \-\- the client waiting for a reply the broker was never asked for, or the broker answering a method the client never learned was refused. So a refusal sends the protocol\(aqs own statement of why and closes both legs. A client library reports that as an access refusal, which is what it reports when a broker refuses one of its own.
.PP
A refused \fBprotocol header\fR is answered the way both specifications say (0\-9\-1 \[u00A7]4.2.2, 1.0 \[u00A7]2.2): with a header this listener does serve, and then the socket closes. A client library reports "the server speaks 0\-9\-1" rather than "the connection dropped".
.SS "What monitor mode never shadows"
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.4i) lbx
lw(1.4i) lx.
T{
Refusal
T}	T{
Why it is hard
T}
T{
\fBclient_\:not_\:allowed\fR,\: \fBtls_\:required\fR
T}	T{
An address that may not connect;\: a credential that would go on the wire
T}
T{
\fBprotocol_\:version_\:not_\:allowed\fR,\: \fBprotocol_\:version_\:unknown\fR,\: \fBtls_\:negotiation_\:refused\fR
T}	T{
The header decides the framing of everything after it,\: so there is no "observe" for it
T}
T{
\fBnot_\:authenticated\fR
T}	T{
Forwarding an operation before the broker accepted a credential means the operation ran
T}
T{
\fBunreadable_\:frame\fR,\: \fBframe_\:type_\:unknown\fR,\: \fBarguments_\:unreadable\fR
T}	T{
The relay has no opinion to observe
T}
T{
\fBmechanism_\:not_\:allowed\fR,\: \fBmechanism_\:missing\fR
T}	T{
A mechanism is chosen once,\: at the start,\: and a downgrade observed is a downgrade
T}
T{
\fBvhost_\:not_\:allowed\fR
T}	T{
The vhost decides what every name after it means
T}
T{
\fBframe_\:max_\:unbounded\fR,\: \fBframe_\:max_\:too_\:large\fR,\: \fBheartbeat_\:disabled\fR,\: \fBtoo_\:many_\:channels\fR,\: \fBtoo_\:many_\:links\fR,\: \fBtoo_\:many_\:methods\fR,\: \fBmessage_\:too_\:large\fR,\: \fBrate_\:limited\fR,\: \fBbody_\:past_\:declared_\:size\fR
T}	T{
Bounds
T}
T{
\fBmanagement_\:node_\:denied\fR
T}	T{
The broker\(aqs own administration
T}
T{
\fBdelivery_\:denied\fR
T}	T{
The message is already on its way to the client
T}
T{
\fBno_\:such_\:link\fR
T}	T{
A 1.0 transfer on a handle this relay never saw attached,\: so its address was never checked
T}
T{
any refusal of a \fBqueue.\:delete\fR,\: \fBexchange.\:delete\fR,\: \fBqueue.\:purge\fR or \fBconnection.\:update\-\:secret\fR
T}	T{
A purge forwarded so that it could be written down is a queue that is empty
T}
.TE
.PP
A method merely \fIoff\fR the allow list is a \fBsoft\fR refusal, which is what makes monitor mode useful: it is most likely an application nobody has listed yet.
.SS "The inbound direction"
.PP
The deny lists apply to what the broker hands the client \-\- \fBbasic.deliver\fR, \fBbasic.get\-ok\fR, \fBbasic.return\fR \-\- and the allow lists do not. The difference is deliberate. An allow list says what a client may \fIask for\fR; a delivery names where the message came \fIfrom\fR, which the consumer need not be allowed to name: a queue bound to an exchange by somebody else delivers messages carrying that exchange\(aqs name, and a relay that required it on the allow list would break every ordinary consumer. A deny list says something else \-\- this connection must never see messages from there, whoever routed them \-\- and that one holds in both directions.
.PP
Refusals are \fBamqp_denied\fR for the ban triggers, with the reasons in the table above plus \fBmethod_not_allowed\fR, \fBmethod_denied\fR, \fBmethod_unknown\fR, \fBperformative_not_allowed\fR, \fBperformative_denied\fR, \fBperformative_unknown\fR, \fBtopology_not_allowed\fR, \fBpublish_not_allowed\fR, \fBconsume_not_allowed\fR, \fBexchange_not_allowed\fR, \fBexchange_denied\fR, \fBqueue_not_allowed\fR, \fBqueue_denied\fR, \fBrouting_key_not_allowed\fR, \fBrouting_key_denied\fR, \fBaddress_not_allowed\fR, \fBaddress_denied\fR, \fBreply_to_not_allowed\fR, \fBuser_not_allowed\fR, \fBuser_id_missing\fR, \fBuser_id_mismatch\fR, \fBpriority_too_high\fR, \fBno_ack_not_allowed\fR, \fBaddress_missing\fR, \fBadministrative_not_allowed\fR, \fBno_rule_matched\fR, \fBrule_denied\fR, \fBoutside_schedule\fR, \fBtoo_many_sessions\fR, \fBtoo_many_sessions_per_client\fR, \fBno_protocol_header\fR, \fBtls_handshake\fR and \fBupstream_unavailable\fR.
.SH "s7"
.PP
\fBkind: s7\fR is a relay in front of a Siemens PLC. It is the listener for the protocol with the least security of any in this project.
.PP
S7comm is three layers on TCP 102: \fBTPKT\fR (RFC 1006, a four\-octet length), \fBCOTP\fR (X.224 class 0, whose connection request carries the address of the CPU) and \fBS7comm\fR itself. What matters about it is what it does not have. There is no transport security at all, which is why this listener takes no \fBtls\fR section \-\- a certificate here would promise something the protocol cannot do. And there is no authentication worth the name: the optional password protects a handful of functions on some CPU families and nothing on others, and an S7\-300 with no password accepts a \fBstop\fR from anybody who can open a socket to it. An engineering station on the same segment can read and write every byte of memory in every controller on that segment.
.PP
The equipment cannot be fixed. A controller in a line is replaced on a capital cycle, not a release cycle, and its firmware is qualified against the process it runs. So the boundary has to be somewhere else, and this is somewhere else.
.SS "The vocabulary"
.PP
A relay\(aqs job here is to know what an operation \fIis\fR, and this protocol spreads that across two layers: a \fBfunction code\fR for reading and writing memory and for the block and control services, and a \fBuser\-data group and subfunction\fR for everything else \-\- the diagnostic buffer, the block list, the clock, the password, the debugger. A policy written against function codes would have nothing to say about setting the clock; one written against user\-data groups would have nothing to say about a write.
.PP
So both are mapped onto one vocabulary of nineteen words, and every list in this section is written in it:
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.2i) lbx
lw(1.2i) lx.
T{
Word
T}	T{
What it is
T}
T{
\fBread\fR
T}	T{
Reading memory: a data block,\: the process image,\: a timer
T}
T{
\fBwrite\fR
T}	T{
Writing it \-\- on a plant,\: the operation that moves something physical
T}
T{
\fBsetup\fR
T}	T{
The connection negotiation,\: without which there is no session
T}
T{
\fBupload\fR
T}	T{
Reading a block \fBout\fR of the PLC: the program,\: as source an engineering tool can open
T}
T{
\fBdownload\fR
T}	T{
Writing one in,\: which is changing the program the machine runs
T}
T{
\fBcontrol\fR
T}	T{
The control service: a warm restart,\: inserting or deleting a block,\: compressing memory
T}
T{
\fBstop\fR
T}	T{
Stopping the CPU
T}
T{
\fBcpu_\:services\fR
T}	T{
Function code 0,\: which the families in the field answer in ways nobody has documented
T}
T{
\fBszl\fR
T}	T{
Reading a system status list: the CPU\(aqs type,\: its firmware,\: its diagnostic buffer
T}
T{
\fBdiagnostics\fR
T}	T{
The rest of the CPU function group: the message service and the alarm machinery
T}
T{
\fBblocks\fR
T}	T{
Listing the blocks and reading their headers
T}
T{
\fBcyclic\fR
T}	T{
Subscribing to cyclic data,\: which is how an HMI reads a screenful of values
T}
T{
\fBtime_\:read\fR
T}	T{
Reading the CPU clock
T}
T{
\fBtime_\:write\fR
T}	T{
Setting it \-\- and the clock is what every log line and batch record is stamped with
T}
T{
\fBsecurity\fR
T}	T{
The password functions: supplying one,\: clearing one,\: asking how protected the CPU is
T}
T{
\fBprogrammer\fR
T}	T{
The debugger: forcing a variable,\: setting a breakpoint,\: stepping the program
T}
T{
\fBmode\fR
T}	T{
The mode transitions requested through the user\-data layer rather than the control service
T}
T{
\fBpbc\fR
T}	T{
The programmable block communication a pair of PLCs uses between themselves
T}
T{
\fBnc\fR
T}	T{
The numerical control layer of a machine tool
T}
.TE
.PP
\fBoperations\fR defaults to \fBsetup\fR, \fBread\fR, \fBszl\fR, \fBblocks\fR, \fBcyclic\fR, \fBtime_read\fR and \fBdiagnostics\fR: what an HMI, a historian and an inventory do, and nothing that changes anything. \fBThe absences are the policy.\fR No write, no download, no control service, no stop, no mode transition, no clock setting, no password function, no programmer command \-\- and \fBno upload\fR, which is the one worth pausing on, because an upload changes nothing and is still off. Reading a block out of a PLC is how a plant\(aqs control logic leaves the site.
.PP
An engineering station needs several of those. Naming them in the file is a line a reviewer can see.
.SS "The address is the rack and the slot"
.PP
Which controller a client asked for arrives in the \fBCOTP connection request\fR, before any S7 request exists: the called TSAP\(aqs two octets hold a connection resource, a rack (0\-7) and a slot (0\-31). So a client that may not reach that CPU is refused \fBbefore the PLC is dialled\fR, and that ordering is the point rather than an optimisation \-\- a CPU has very few connection resources, an S7\-300 sixteen altogether, and a client that may not reach it should not take one of them.
.PP
\fBresources\fR is the cheapest useful line in this section. \fBpg\fR is the programming device connection an engineering station opens, \fBop\fR is an operator panel and \fBbasic\fR is what one PLC opens to another; a listener that admits only \fBop\fR has refused every engineering station without naming a single function.
.SS "The memory is the boundary inside the CPU"
.PP
\fBareas\fR and \fBdbs\fR say which memory a client may reach at all, and \fBaddresses\fR and \fBwrite_addresses\fR bound it by byte. The areas are \fBdb\fR, \fBinstance_db\fR, \fBinputs\fR, \fBoutputs\fR, \fBflags\fR (Siemens calls them merkers), \fBtimer\fR, \fBcounter\fR, \fBlocal\fR, \fBprevious_local\fR, \fBperipheral\fR, and the 200\-family areas \fBsysinfo_200\fR, \fBsysflags_200\fR, \fBanalog_in_200\fR, \fBanalog_out_200\fR, \fBcounter_200\fR and \fBtimer_200\fR.
.PP
\fBperipheral\fR is the one worth putting on \fBdeny_areas\fR on any listener that allows writing: it is direct access to the I/O hardware, past the process image the program reads.
.PP
Two details of how the ranges are applied:
.RS 3n
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
The protocol carries a \fBbit\fR address. The configuration is written in \fBbytes\fR, because that is how an operator thinks about a data block, and the relay divides by eight rather than making anybody else do it.
.PP
.ti -3n
\(bu
A range is checked against the \fBwhole span\fR a request covers, not its first byte. A read of bytes 0 to 200 against a range of \fB0\-99\fR is a read of bytes the policy does not name, and it is refused rather than split: splitting it would be this relay deciding which half the operator meant.
.RE
.PP
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
