.SS "Rules"
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.2i) lbw(0.8i) lbx
lw(1.2i) lw(0.8i) lx.
T{
Key
T}	T{
Type
T}	T{
Description
T}
T{
\fBname\fR
T}	T{
string
T}	T{
Names the rule in logs and counters
T}
T{
\fBaction\fR
T}	T{
\fBallow\fR,\: \fBdeny\fR,\: \fBobserve\fR
T}	T{
Default \fBallow\fR
T}
T{
\fBclients\fR,\: \fBracks\fR,\: \fBslots\fR,\: \fBresources\fR
T}	T{
lists
T}	T{
Selectors;\: AND within a rule,\: OR within one
T}
T{
\fBschedule\fR
T}	T{
object
T}	T{
\fBdays\fR,\: \fBfrom\fR,\: \fBto\fR,\: \fBtimezone\fR. This is how "the integrator may download during the shutdown window" is written
T}
T{
\fBoperations\fR,\: \fBdeny_\:operations\fR,\: \fBareas\fR,\: \fBdeny_\:areas\fR,\: \fBdbs\fR,\: \fBaddresses\fR,\: \fBwrite_\:addresses\fR,\: \fBblock_\:types\fR,\: \fBmax_\:items\fR
T}	T{

T}	T{
The rule\(aqs own narrowing. A rule that names operations \fBwidens\fR the listener for its own traffic;\: the deny lists and \fBread_\:only\fR always win
T}
T{
\fBcomment\fR
T}	T{
string
T}	T{
Carried into every log line the rule decides,\: for the change record a plant keeps
T}
.TE
.SS "How a refusal is answered"
.PP
A refused \fBrequest\fR is answered and the connection carries on, which is the modbus kind\(aqs choice and for the same reason: a plant connection is a poll loop, and dropping it because one request was refused turns a refusal into an outage. The answer is an acknowledgement with error class \fB0x87\fR, \fIaccess fault\fR \-\- what a password\-protected CPU answers a client that has not supplied one \-\- so the client library reports the refusal it would have reported from the controller itself. A refused \fBuser\-data\fR request is answered in its own layer instead: the same group and subfunction, with the error code for a function the CPU does not offer, because that is where a client that asked to set the clock looks.
.PP
A refused \fBconnection\fR is answered with a COTP disconnect request, which is what a CPU with no free connection resources sends. A silent close reads to an engineering station as a network fault, and an engineer chasing a network fault that is really a policy is an afternoon wasted.
.PP
The exceptions \-\- the cases that end the connection \-\- are the frames the relay could not read at all: a COTP PDU type it does not know, and a data PDU that is not an S7 PDU. There is nothing left to be sure of after either.
.SS "What monitor mode never shadows"
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.3i) lbx
lw(1.3i) lx.
T{
Refusal
T}	T{
Why it is hard
T}
T{
\fBclient_\:not_\:allowed\fR,\: \fBrack_\:not_\:allowed\fR,\: \fBslot_\:not_\:allowed\fR,\: \fBresource_\:not_\:allowed\fR
T}	T{
An address or a controller a client may not reach. The connection is what would be forwarded
T}
T{
\fBnot_\:a_\:connection_\:request\fR,\: \fBdestination_\:unreadable\fR,\: \fBcotp_\:type_\:unknown\fR,\: \fBunreadable_\:frame\fR,\: \fBunreadable_\:pdu\fR,\: \fBitems_\:unreadable\fR,\: \fBitem_\:not_\:addressable\fR,\: \fBunexpected_\:message\fR
T}	T{
The relay has no opinion to observe. An operation it cannot read is one it cannot have a policy about
T}
T{
\fBoperation_\:unknown\fR
T}	T{
A function code or user\-data group with no name is an operation with no policy
T}
T{
\fBread_\:only\fR
T}	T{
The listener said so
T}
T{
\fBtoo_\:many_\:items\fR,\: \fBtoo_\:many_\:bytes\fR,\: \fBtoo_\:many_\:requests\fR,\: \fBpdu_\:length_\:too_\:large\fR,\: \fBrate_\:limited\fR,\: \fBtoo_\:many_\:sessions\fR,\: \fBtoo_\:many_\:sessions_\:per_\:client\fR
T}	T{
Bounds
T}
T{
any refusal of \fBwrite\fR,\: \fBdownload\fR,\: \fBcontrol\fR,\: \fBstop\fR,\: \fBmode\fR,\: \fBtime_\:write\fR,\: \fBsecurity\fR or \fBprogrammer\fR
T}	T{
A write forwarded so that it could be written down is a moved actuator,\: and a stop forwarded is a stopped machine. A report afterwards undoes none of it
T}
.TE
.PP
An operation merely \fIoff\fR the allow list \-\- a read of a data block nobody has listed, an upload \-\- is a \fBsoft\fR refusal, which is what makes monitor mode useful on a plant nobody has an inventory of.
.SS "What the PLC itself refuses"
.PP
One record here is not a refusal by this relay and is the one that matters most after an incident: an \fBaccess fault from the controller\fR, logged as \fBs7_plc_refused\fR. That is the CPU refusing something this listener allowed, which on this protocol almost always means the controller is password\-protected and the client has not supplied a password. It is the case where the two policies disagree, and an operator needs to know which one to change.
.PP
What is never logged is a \fBvalue\fR. A write\(aqs payload is a process value, and on a plant those are pressures, temperatures and recipe parameters: not secrets, but not something a relay should copy into a log file at poll rate either. The \fIaddress\fR is logged, because an address is what a policy is written about, and a refusal nobody can attribute to a byte range is a refusal nobody can act on.
.PP
Refusals are \fBs7_denied\fR for the ban triggers, with the reasons in the table above plus \fBoperation_not_allowed\fR, \fBoperation_denied\fR, \fBarea_not_allowed\fR, \fBarea_denied\fR, \fBdb_not_allowed\fR, \fBaddress_not_allowed\fR, \fBblock_type_not_allowed\fR, \fBno_rule_matched\fR, \fBrule_denied\fR, \fBoutside_schedule\fR, \fBno_connection_request\fR and \fBupstream_unavailable\fR.
.SH "asset_inventory"
.PP
One record per device, built from traffic the proxy was already carrying.
.PP
An operational estate\(aqs oldest problem is that nobody knows what is on the network: the drawings are out of date, the spreadsheet was abandoned, and the one thing nobody may do is run a scanner \-\- an active scan is how a programmable controller gets knocked over. A security proxy is an unusually good place to solve that, because it already parses the protocols. The relay kinds read Modbus function codes, IEC 104 common addresses, SNMP object identifiers, MQTT client identifiers, DHCP vendor classes and TFTP filenames, and each of those says something about what sent it.
.PP
Nothing here probes, scans or connects to anything.
.PP
Off by default. An inventory is a record of somebody\(aqs estate, and a proxy that started keeping one without being asked would be making a decision about their data for them.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
asset_inventory:
  enabled: true
  state_file: /var/lib/xproxy/assets.json
  ttl: 720h
  roles: [plc, rtu, hmi, scada_server, field_gateway, network_switch]
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(0.9i) lbw(0.9i) lbw(0.8i) lbx
lw(0.9i) lw(0.9i) lw(0.8i) lx.
T{
Key
T}	T{
Type
T}	T{
Default
T}	T{
Description
T}
T{
\fBenabled\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
Keep an inventory
T}
T{
\fBstate_\:file\fR
T}	T{
path
T}	T{
\fI(none)\fR
T}	T{
Where the inventory is written and read back. Empty keeps it in memory,\: which means the whole estate is reported as new after every restart \-\- the fastest way to teach an operator to ignore the alerts
T}
T{
\fBsave_\:interval\fR
T}	T{
duration
T}	T{
\fB5m\fR
T}	T{
How often the file is written. It is also written once on shutdown,\: so the last minutes are not lost
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:assets\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB8192\fR
T}	T{
How many devices to hold. Past the bound the least recently seen record goes,\: and the count is exported as \fBassets.\:dropped\fR. This is the opposite of the pairing tables in the relay kinds,\: deliberately: forgetting here loses history rather than making a decision wrong,\: and refusing would stop the inventory noticing the estate at the moment something is filling it up
T}
T{
\fBttl\fR
T}	T{
duration
T}	T{
\fB720h\fR
T}	T{
How long a device nobody has seen is kept,\: so a decommissioned device leaves the record instead of being reported for ever
T}
T{
\fBvendor_\:file\fR
T}	T{
path
T}	T{
\fI(none)\fR
T}	T{
Hardware prefixes and manufacturer names,\: one per line,\: added to the built\-in seed list rather than replacing it. Format: a prefix,\: whitespace or a comma,\: and a name;\: \fB#\fR comments. A malformed line refuses the whole file rather than being skipped,\: because a list an operator trusted and which silently dropped half its entries is worse than one that would not load. Point this at a copy of the IEEE registry for full coverage
T}
T{
\fBalert_\:on_\:new\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBtrue\fR
T}	T{
Write a security event (\fBasset_\:new_\:asset\fR) for a device that was not in the frozen baseline. Does nothing until a baseline exists,\: because before that everything is new
T}
T{
\fBalert_\:on_\:change\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBtrue\fR
T}	T{
Write a security event when a device\(aqs identity changes: an address taken over by another device,\: a role that changed,\: a vendor re\-resolved. This is the setting worth leaving on \-\- a steady\-state inventory is a reference document,\: and the deltas are the security value
T}
T{
\fBroles\fR
T}	T{
list
T}	T{
any
T}	T{
The roles this estate expects. A device classified as anything else raises \fBasset_\:unexpected_\:role\fR,\: which is how "there are no engineering workstations on the process network" gets written down. \fBunknown\fR is always accepted,\: since a role nothing can be classified as would make every unclassified device a finding
T}
.TE
.SS "What each listener kind contributes"
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(0.7i) lbx
lw(0.7i) lx.
T{
Kind
T}	T{
What it can honestly see
T}
T{
\fBdhcp\fR
T}	T{
The lease: the one message in which a device states its own hardware address,\: vendor class,\: user class,\: client identifier,\: host name and boot file together
T}
T{
\fBmodbus\fR
T}	T{
Unit identifiers and function codes,\: at most 64 of each per device,\: and whether the peer asked or answered
T}
T{
\fBiec104\fR
T}	T{
Common addresses,\: the same shape over a different protocol
T}
T{
\fBsnmp\fR
T}	T{
The object identifiers a manager asks for and an agent serves \-\- \fBnot\fR their values. The SNMP parser keeps no varbind values by design,\: so \fBsysDescr\fR is not available to read;\: the OID set is weaker evidence and it is the evidence that exists
T}
T{
\fBmqtt\fR
T}	T{
The client identifier on CONNECT
T}
T{
\fBtftp\fR
T}	T{
The filename and direction of a transfer,\: which on a boot segment is often the only thing that names a device at all
T}
.TE
.PP
Every other listener kind contributes nothing. An inventory on an estate with no relay listeners will be empty, which is honest rather than broken.
.SS "Roles"
.PP
Each role carries the Purdue level it sits at, so a segmentation review can ask the question it actually asks: what is on this wire that does not belong at this level.
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.3i) lbx
lw(1.3i) lx.
T{
Role
T}	T{
Purdue level
T}
T{
\fBsensor\fR,\: \fBdrive\fR
T}	T{
0
T}
T{
\fBplc\fR,\: \fBrtu\fR
T}	T{
1
T}
T{
\fBhmi\fR,\: \fBscada_\:server\fR,\: \fBfield_\:gateway\fR
T}	T{
2
T}
T{
\fBhistorian\fR,\: \fBengineering_\:workstation\fR
T}	T{
3
T}
T{
\fBprinter\fR,\: \fBcamera\fR,\: \fBvoip_\:phone\fR,\: \fBserver\fR,\: \fBworkstation\fR
T}	T{
4
T}
T{
\fBnetwork_\:switch\fR,\: \fBrouter\fR,\: \fBembedded_\:device\fR,\: \fBunknown\fR
T}	T{
\fI(none)\fR \-\- the network carries every level and sits at none
T}
.TE
.SS "How a guess is made, and why it says so"
.PP
Behaviour outweighs self\-description. A vendor class, a host name and an SNMP description are strings a device chose, and a hardware address is three bytes of vendor prefix anybody can set. What a device \fIdoes\fR \-\- answering Modbus function 3 on unit 1, carrying IEC 104 interrogations, asking for a firmware image over TFTP \-\- is much harder to fake without becoming the thing it is pretending to be. So rules that fire on behaviour carry more confidence than rules that fire on a string.
.PP
Every classification keeps a confidence from 0 to 100 and the evidence that produced it, strongest first. An inventory that reports "PLC" with no confidence and no evidence is one an engineer cannot argue with, and being unable to argue with it is how a wrong entry survives for years. When two rules of equal weight disagree the asset is marked ambiguous and loses ten points rather than one of them silently winning.
.SS "The baseline"
.PP
\fBxproxyctl assets baseline\fR takes the current set of devices as what the estate has. Everything seen afterwards that is not in it is reported as new. This is what turns the inventory from a reference document into a detection, so both freezing and forgetting a baseline (\fBxproxyctl assets baseline \-forget\fR) are written to the audit log with the caller\(aqs kernel\-reported credentials, like a ban.
.SS "Reading it"
.PP
\fBGET /v1/assets\fR answers the summary, the known roles and the devices, with \fBrole\fR, \fBlistener\fR, \fBproto\fR, \fBvendor\fR, \fBnew=1\fR, \fBchanged=1\fR and \fBtop\fR as filters, or \fBid\fR to look one device up by identifier, address or hardware address \-\- whichever a log line happened to carry. \fBxproxyctl assets\fR is the same thing as a table, \fBxproxyctl assets \-long\fR one block per device with the evidence, and \fBxproxyctl assets show KEY\fR one device.
.SH "access"
.PP
Just\-in\-time access to the gate listeners: nobody opens a session unless there is a live grant naming them, the listener and the target.
.PP
A bastion with standing access is a bastion whose accounts are worth as much as the machines behind it. The keys sit in the estate all the time, so whoever reaches a key, a laptop or a session reaches production at a moment of their choosing. This is the other arrangement: a grant somebody asked for, somebody else approved, that ends by itself, and that is written down.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
.ft CR
access:
  ledger: /var/lib/xgate/access.log
  approvals: 1            # four eyes: the person who asked and one other
  max_duration: 4h
  max_lead: 24h
  max_uses: 0             # the window is the bound
  max_open: 256

server:
  listeners:
    \- name: bastion
      kind: ssh
      ssh:
        upstream: prod\-hosts
        require_grant: true
.ft
.fi
.RE
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(1.0i) lbw(0.9i) lbw(0.8i) lbx
lw(1.0i) lw(0.9i) lw(0.8i) lx.
T{
Key
T}	T{
Type
T}	T{
Default
T}	T{
Description
T}
T{
\fBledger\fR
T}	T{
path
T}	T{
none
T}	T{
The append\-only file every request,\: approval,\: denial,\: revocation and use is written to,\: with a hash chain over the records. Absolute. Without it the grants live only in this process \-\- gone at the next restart,\: with no trail \-\- which is warned about rather than refused,\: because a test estate legitimately runs that way
T}
T{
\fBapprovals\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB1\fR
T}	T{
Approvals a grant needs \fBin addition to\fR the request. 1 is four eyes: the person who asked and one other. 0 means a request is in force the moment it is made \-\- still just\-in\-time and time\-boxed,\: but nobody else has to agree,\: and it is warned about. At most 8
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:duration\fR
T}	T{
duration
T}	T{
\fB4h\fR
T}	T{
The longest window a grant may cover;\: 1m to 24h
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:lead\fR
T}	T{
duration
T}	T{
\fB24h\fR
T}	T{
How far ahead of now a window may start,\: so an approval today cannot be a key for next quarter;\: 0 to 720h
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:uses\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB0\fR
T}	T{
Sessions one grant may open. 0 leaves the window as the only bound;\: 1 is a one\-shot grant. 0 to 1000
T}
T{
\fBmax_\:open\fR
T}	T{
int
T}	T{
\fB256\fR
T}	T{
Grants that may be pending or in force at once. A request queue nobody drains is how an approval system becomes a rubber stamp;\: 1 to 4096
T}
T{
\fBself_\:approval\fR
T}	T{
bool
T}	T{
\fBfalse\fR
T}	T{
Let the requester approve their own request. It is here for the estate with one operator,\: where the alternative is switching the requirement off altogether. Warned about every time
T}
.TE
.PP
Each gate kind \-\- \fBssh\fR, \fBtelnet\fR, \fBvnc\fR, \fBrdp\fR and \fBftp\fR \-\- takes \fBrequire_grant: true\fR, in that one spelling, so an estate does not have to remember which protocol calls it what. A listener that requires a grant with no \fBaccess\fR section fails the load; an \fBaccess\fR section no listener asks is warned about.
.SS "What a grant names"
.PP
A grant names a \fBsubject\fR, a \fBlistener\fR and a \fBtarget\fR.
.PP
The subject is the identity the estate knows after authentication \-\- the SSH principal entry when a key matched one, otherwise the login \-\- rather than a name a client is free to offer. The listener is one listener by name: a grant on the jump host is not a grant on the bastion. The target is either the listener\(aqs \fBupstream\fR pool, which means any machine in it, or the \fBhost:port\fR of one endpoint in that pool, which means that machine and \fBpins the dial to it\fR \-\- otherwise "alice may reach db\-2 to restart a service" would be access to whichever machine the balancer felt like.
.PP
An address that leaves the pool and comes back is the same address; a \fIgrant\fR that is revoked, denied, spent or expired is finished and cannot be approved back to life.
.SS "Four eyes"
.PP
A grant is in force only once \fBapprovals\fR people have approved it, and \fBneither the person who asked nor the person who gains the access may be one of them\fR. Names are compared trimmed and case\-insensitively, so \fBAlice\fR approving what \fBalice\fR asked for is one person rather than two, and one approver cannot count twice.
.PP
The approvals a grant needs are fixed when it is requested. Loosening \fBapprovals\fR later does not bring a half\-approved grant into force, and the record says what was required at the time rather than what is required now.
.PP
Revocation is not slowed down the same way: anybody who can reach the management API may revoke a grant, including one in force. Taking access away is not the decision this requirement exists to guard.
.SS "The time box"
.PP
Every grant carries a window, checked against the policy when the request is made rather than left to an approver to notice. \fBmax_duration\fR bounds its length and \fBmax_lead\fR how far ahead it may start.
.PP
The window ends the session \fBthat is running\fR, not only the next one somebody opens: a gate sets the session\(aqs deadline to the earlier of its own \fBsession_timeout\fR and the end of the window. Without that, a four\-hour grant used at the last minute is a session that lasts as long as the operator likes.
.PP
A request nobody approved before its window closed is expired rather than pending: it cannot come into force any more, and leaving it in the queue would hide the ones that still can.
.SS "The trail"
.PP
Every act is one line of the ledger, and each line carries a hash over the previous one. A removed, edited, reordered or forged line is found when the file is read at start, and the daemon refuses to serve a trail it cannot stand behind rather than presenting it as intact. One process holds the file exclusively \-\- two daemons appending would interleave their chains \-\- so a second daemon pointed at the same path fails to start and says so.
.PP
A record is written, flushed and synced \fBbefore\fR the grant it describes is in force. A grant that is in force in memory but not on disk is a grant nobody approved after the next restart, and an approval the caller was told about but that was never written is worse than one that was refused. \fBmax_uses\fR is durable for the same reason: a one\-shot grant that refills itself on restart is not one shot.
.SS "What a refusal says"
.PP
A session turned away is counted under its own reason, because an operator answering a call needs to know which. They are the listener\(aqs ordinary refusal counters (\fBxproxyctl status\fR, the \fB_refusals_total\fR metrics) and each is a deny event on the listener\(aqs usual reason (\fBssh_denied\fR and so on), so bans apply as they always did.
.PP
.TS
allbox;
lbw(0.8i) lbx
lw(0.8i) lx.
T{
Reason
T}	T{
What happened
T}
T{
\fBno_\:grant\fR
T}	T{
Nobody has asked for access for this subject on this listener
T}
T{
\fBgrant_\:pending\fR
T}	T{
A request exists and not enough people have approved it
T}
T{
\fBgrant_\:not_\:yet\fR
T}	T{
Approved,\: and its window has not opened
T}
T{
\fBgrant_\:expired\fR
T}	T{
The window closed,\: or nobody approved in time
T}
T{
\fBgrant_\:denied\fR
T}	T{
An approver refused it
T}
T{
\fBgrant_\:revoked\fR
T}	T{
It was withdrawn
T}
T{
\fBgrant_\:spent\fR
T}	T{
\fBmax_\:uses\fR is used up
T}
T{
\fBgrant_\:wrong_\:target\fR
T}	T{
There is a grant for this subject on this listener,\: for another machine \-\- named separately because "wrong target" is the mistake an operator makes and "no grant" would send them looking for the wrong thing
T}
.TE
.PP
A listener in \fBpolicy: {mode: shadow}\fR records what it would have refused and carries on, which is how an estate turns this on without locking its operators out on the first evening.
